\par\par \b \uc2 Correction to  \highlight22\uc2 South Africa Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2020\par 16:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 South African \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's commitment to a flexible exchange rate won't change unless there is a structural shift, said \uc2 Rabobank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Piotr Matys\uc2  . "\b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  Unlikely to Start \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk," at 1431 GMT, misstated the name of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst.\par \par \uc2 1048 ET - Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2  says the government is "absolutely" willing to take risks with its investment in Federal Reserve emergency-lending facilities. "We are fully prepared to take losses" on the capital the government has provided to backstop Fed losses on the programs, Mnuchin tells a Senate panel. Congress provided $454B for the Treasury to cover initial losses on the loans, and lawmakers pressed Mnuchin on whether the government is prepared to take risks to ensure credit is available to businesses and states that need it most. (kate.davidson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1031 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to start \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rand from falling sharply, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's commitment to a flexible exchange rate even during intense periods of rand depreciation won't change unless there is a "structural shift that would significantly increase the negative impact of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on inflation," \uc2 Rabobank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says. A weaker rand "improves the competitiveness" of exports and "could be a strong incentive to replace imported goods with domestic production," he says. \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates by 50 basis points at Thursday's policy meeting. USD/ZAR falls 0.6% to 18.2336, having earlier reached a 19-day low of 18.1713, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0954 ET - \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  offers transparency about its planned purchases of Canadian provincial bonds, which began this month and are to unfold for a year. The acquisition of longer-term provincial debt is part of the \uc2 BOC\uc2  's quantitative easing program, which has led to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet nearly tripling to about C$400B in the span of two months. Purchases of provincial bonds, tentatively capped at C$50B, will reflect a weighting given to each province's share of eligible bonds outstanding and their share of GDP, \uc2 BOC\uc2  says. Based on a C$50B cap, \uc2 BOC\uc2  is targeting about C$30B in purchases of bonds from Ontario and Quebec, the country's two biggest provinces. Ontario is considered one of the largest subsovereign bond issuers in the world. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 0855 ET - US companies whose credit ratings were downgraded in March to junk status from a investment-grade rating--dubbed as fallen angels--have significantly outperformed the highest and lowest rungs of the BBB rating bucket in spread terms in April, says \uc2 CreditSights\uc2 . Roughly $200B of U.S. corporate debt has been downgraded to high-yield since February, or about 20% of the weakest rung of the BBB rating category, which sits one step above junk. "We generally see relative spread widening in the days leading up to the downgrade and tightening in the days following, as high-yield investors step-in," says the independent credit rating agency. "We can see the allure of a new, large, generally liquid name hitting the investable universe, making a number of Fallen Angels attractive." (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0849 ET - Construction of new US homes posted their sharpest monthly drop on record in April as efforts to contain the new coronavirus prompted builders to put projects on hold. Starts decreased 30.2% from March to a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 891,000. The level for starts was the lowest since 2015. Residential permits, a bellwether for future home construction, dropped 20.8% from the previous month to a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 1.074 million. (jeffrey.sparshott@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 ET - Fashion sales are seen down between 28% and 35% in 2020, according to a survey conducted by Bernstein and \uc2 Boston Consulting Group\uc2  . European and American consumers' sentiment is quite pessimistic with more than half expecting things to get worse, Bernstein says, adding that 30% of consumers expect to spend less even after lockdowns lift. The coronavirus has also driven a 5% to 15% increase in the adoption of online retail, and Bernstein sees multibrand shops like \uc2 Zalando\uc2  and Asos as likely to benefit disproportionately from this. (cristina.roca@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 ET - Financial-market analysts are optimistic about an economic recovery within the next six months, the V-shaped recovery in the German ZEW expectations index shows, but a more gradual return to normal will turn out to be the macroeconomic reality, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  analyst Christian Fuertjes says. Both consumer spending and business fixed investment is bound to be hampered far beyond the end of a lockdown, the analyst says. "While households need to cope with both lower income as well as uncertainty about the overall health situation, firms' propensity to invest will suffer from drained cash reserves and/or accumulated debt during the crisis," Fuertjes says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0829 ET - Investors are pausing for thought after a positive early-stage vaccine trial sent stocks soaring at the start of the week, says Hugh Gimber, a strategist at \uc2 J.P.Morgan Asset Management\uc2  . Futures tied to the S&P 500 are down 0.1%, suggesting the benchmark index will open slightly lower. "Investors are just pausing for a breather after yesterday's excitement about the potential development of a vaccine," Gimber says. A successful vaccine would clear the way for a faster recovery in economic growth, he adds, but "clearly there's a long way to go before that would come into practice." Gimber doesn't expect major news from Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  's congressional testimony. "We learned quite a lot about his position last week," he says. (joe.wallace@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 ET - The euro could strengthen further in coming days on optimism over the eurozone recovery fund proposed by Germany and France on Monday, BK Asset Management says. "EUR/USD is now looking to run the key 1.1000 level after the Germans and the French put forth a proposal for 500 billion euro rescue package that was based on grants rather than loans," BK's \uc2 Boris Schlossberg\uc2  says. Investors have "felt a wave of relief" that EU leaders are "finally realizing the need for fiscal stimulus" rather than "simply more monetary debt obligation," he says. EUR/USD rises 0.3% to 1.0954 after reaching a two-week high of 1.0977 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 ET - May's ZEW index rose for the second consecutive month amid the coronavirus pandemic for both the eurozone and Germany, driven by a noticeable improvement in expectations, Oxford Economics' Katharina Koenz says. "Financial markets' expectations rose on the back of ample fiscal easing, historic monetary stimulus by all major central banks and the hope that the worst of the pandemic is behind us as countries have started to ease restrictions on economic activity," the economist says. This bodes well for some careful optimism for the flash purchasing managers indexes, to be released on Friday. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0755 ET - Danish government bonds have stabilized around the levels of their Dutch peers, and this is unlikely to change in the near future due to increasing Danish issuance, missing quantitative easing and limited liquidity, says \uc2 Nordea\uc2  Chief Analyst Anders Skytte Aalund. Still, the safe-haven status of Danish government bonds could be an incentive to buy them, he says. "The main reason for buying Denmark is in our view the uncertainty regarding the sustainability of the Italian debt situation where Denmark could benefit from being safe haven," he says. At Wednesday's Danish auction of November 2022- and November 2029-dated government bonds, \uc2 Nordea\uc2  prefers the longer-dated bond. The 10-year Danish government bond trades at a yield of -0.235%, while its Dutch counterpart trades at -0.207%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 19, 2020 10:59 ET (14:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200519eg5j002ak\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
