\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-South Africa's rand falls to 2-year low as Turkey rout spreads\b0\par\par\uc2 519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2018\par 17:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Rand slides 10 percent before paring losses\par \par \uc2 * Yields on government bonds spike\par \par \uc2 * Johannesburg-listed stocks mostly spared pain (Updates prices, adds quote and detail; repeats to fix formatting with no change to text)\par \par \uc2 By Olivia Kumwenda-Mtambo and Alexander Winning\par \par \uc2 JOHANNESBURG, Aug 13 (Reuters) - South Africa's rand plunged more than 10 percent to a 2-year low against the dollar early on Monday and government bonds weakened sharply as a renewed rout in the Turkish lira spread to other emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The rand, which later recovered to trade down 1.6 percent against the U.S. unit by 1500 GMT, is one of the most deeply traded emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making it susceptible to swings in sentiment on global markets.\par \par \uc2 The magnitude of the rand's slide came after three days of heavy losses and caught the \uc2 South African Reserve Bank\uc2  by surprise, but a deputy governor at the bank told Reuters that the regulator was "nowhere near" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Highlighting \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's narrower current account deficit and smaller stock of short-term external debt than Turkey, analysts said investors should not be overly concerned by recent rand weakness.\par \par \uc2 "The rand is currently telling us a lot more about general emerging market sentiment than it is about South Africa in particular," said John Ashbourne of \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The rand is likely to rebound in the short term as the situation in Turkey stabilises," Ashbourne added.\par \par \uc2 The yield on South Africa's benchmark government bond maturing in 2026 rose 19.5 basis points to 9.050 percent, reflecting weaker bond prices.\par \par \uc2 The average yield premium to hold South African debt over safe haven U.S. Treasuries rose to 303 basis points (bps), having added more than 30 bps in the past week.\par \par \uc2 The lira has lost more than 40 percent of its value this year on worries over Turkish President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 's increasing control over the economy and deteriorating relations with the United States.\par \par \uc2 It did find a sliver of support on Monday, when \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would take all measures necessary to maintain financial stability.\par \par \uc2 "The Turkey crisis was caused by monetary policy reacting in a completely unsuitable manner. Countries with a decent monetary policy should be in a better position," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analysts wrote in a research note, referring to South Africa.\par \par \uc2 "The recovery of the rand ... confirms that at least some market participants have trust in the (\uc2 South African Reserve Bank\uc2 ) to react appropriately."\par \par \uc2 Johannesburg-listed stocks were mostly spared the pain felt elsewhere in South African assets.\par \par \uc2 The All-share index closed up 0.32 percent at 57,885 points, while the Top-40 index ended 0.47 percent higher at 51,821 points.\par \par \uc2 One outlier was South African pulp and paper maker \uc2 Sappi\uc2  , however. Its shares fell 9.5 percent after the company reported a 12 percent drop in quarterly profit. (Additional reporting by Karin Strohecker in London Editing by James Macharia and Alison Williams) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-8-13T16:58:13.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180813ee8d00ogl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal