\par\par \b \uc2 NPK - \uc2 NAMPAK LIMITED\uc2  - Summarised consolidated financial results for the six months ended 31 March 2018\b0\par\par\uc2 9,226 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2018\par 17:08\par \uc2 Johannesburg Stock Exchange\par \uc2 JSEXCH\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2018 Johannesburg Stock Exchange. All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 Summarised consolidated financial results for the six months ended 31 March 2018\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nampak Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par (Incorporated in the Republic of South Africa)\par Registration Number: 1968/008070/06\par Share Code: NPK \par ISIN: ZAE000071676\par ("Nampak" or "the Company")\par \par Summarised consolidated financial results\par For the six months ended 31 March 2018\par \par Highlights\par * Revenue increased to R8.8?bn up by 2%\par * Trading profit increased to R1.2bn up by 7%\par * HEPS increased by 10% to 132.0 cents per share\par * R1.3bn (USD97.6M) cash extracted from Nigeria, Angola and Zimbabwe\par * Operating profit decreased to R1.0bn down by 6%\par * Net gearing improved to 39% from 51% \u8212\'20\'14 financial position further \par   enhanced\par \par Nampak's CEO, Andr\u233\'00\'E9 de Ruyter, commented "Our performance from continuing \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is pleasing in a mixed economic and political environment. All \par our beverage can making \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and plastics in the Rest of Africa \par achieved pleasing results while the rest of the group delivered a \par satisfactory performance under adverse conditions, characterised by \par reduced demand, particularly prior to December 2017.\par \par Our focus on strengthening our financial position and prudent capital \par allocation has resulted in improved cash generation, reduced gearing and \par improved margins. We are intent on ensuring we have a solid foundation \par on which to grow our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Management and the Board will continue \par to operate the business in order to build a sustainable, profitable \par business in which shareholder returns are optimised."\par \par Commentary\par \par Revenue from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  grew by 2% and trading profit rose by\par 7% as a result of robust demand in the Metals and Plastics Divisions' \par performance in the Rest of Africa. Nampak's headline earnings and headline \par earnings per share ("HEPS") for continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increased 11% and\par 10% to R849 million (2017: R767 million) and 132.0c (2017: 119.7c)\par respectively.\par \par Cash extraction improved significantly as the introduction of the Nigerian \par Autonomous \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ("NAFEX") market in April 2017 enabled further \par extraction of USD74 million from Nigeria for the six months to March 2018, \par improving the extraction rate to 137% from 80% in the comparative period \par and reducing the cash balance by 57% from R955 million to R410 million. \par Hedging in Angola also improved to 95% from 77% in March 2017.\par \par The results were impacted by the South African Rand ("Rand") which \par was on average 6% stronger versus the US dollar for the six month period and \par 13% stronger as at the end of the period compared to 30 September 2017. This \par meant the translation of revenue growth in the Rest of Africa was moderated. \par Net gearing at 31 March 2018 benefitted from the firmer Rand, however, the \par statement of other comprehensive income reported adverse unrealised \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign \par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  differences on translation of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amounting to \par R665 million. Despite net gearing being managed to the lower levels of the \par group's targeted range, cash extraction from Angola and Zimbabwe was less \par than satisfactory and remains a key focus area.\par \par Good progress has been made in improving key foundational operating \par practices at our Glass Division. Pack-to-Melt ratios have stabilised at \par reasonably satisfactory levels, and further improvements are expected as \par newly introduced skills and practices gain traction. As required by \par International Financial Reporting Standards, rigorous impairment tests \par were carried out on the Glass Division. The results of these tests, as \par verified by our external auditors, indicate that no impairment is \par required, and that sufficient headroom exists.\par \par Following a careful review of the Glass business, challenges in \par leveraging economies of skill and scale, and its significant capital \par requirements going forward, the Board decided to dispose of the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to free up cash for other uses, including growth, \par debt reduction and enhancing free cash flow. Accordingly, Glass has \par been accounted for as a discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  separately in the group \par statement of comprehensive income with associated assets and liabilities \par being grouped together and separately disclosed on the face of the \par statement of financial position. Exploratory discussions have been held \par with a number of strategic players in the packaging industry and a \par formal disposal process is in its initial stages. We expect to conclude \par the transaction by the first half of the 2019 financial year.\par \par Financial performance from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par R million                                        H1 2018  H1 2017 % change\par Revenue                                            8 845    8 668       +2\par Trading profit                                     1 164    1 085       +7\par Net abnormal (losses)/gains                         (121)      24    (amp;gt;100) \par Operating profit                                   1 043    1 109       (6) \par Net profit for the period                            871      896       (3) \par Earnings per share (cents)                         129.4    127.4       +2\par Headline earnings per share (cents)                132.0    119.7      +10\par Cash generated from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                      576      524      +10\par \par Revenue and trading margins\par \par Revenue improved by 2% driven by a strong performance in the Metals \par Division. Bevcan in South Africa and Nigeria experienced pleasing sales volume \par growth and DivFood recovered from low fish can volumes in the comparative \par period. Trading margins were also positively impacted as trading profit \par rose by 7%. Pleasing volume growth in Bevcan Angola was abated by a much \par stronger Rand to the US dollar, which resulted in a modest increase in \par revenue. While the Plastics Division revenue was flat, strong growth in \par the Rest of Africa mitigated volume losses experienced in South Africa \par and Europe and contributed significantly to overall margin improvement. \par The rationalisation of the Paper Division also contributed towards \par improved trading margins for the group of 13.2% from 12.5% in the prior \par period.\par \par Abnormal items and operating profit\par \par Abnormal losses for the period are largely attributable to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses of R75 million from the devaluation of the Angolan \par kwanza. A further R21 million loss was attributable to the cost of \par repatriating cash from Nigeria. Net impairments of R26.6 million are \par largely attributable to the European business while retrenchment costs \par contributed to the 6% reduction of operating profit to R1.0 billion. \par \par Taxation\par The effective tax rate for the period improved to 9.0% from 9.7%. \par Bevcan Nigeria's pioneer status expired on 31 December 2017 and the \par tax rate going forward is expected to increase gradually once \par accumulated tax allowances have been fully utilised in Nigeria. The \par 2019 tax rate is expected to rise as the tax holiday for Bevcan Angola \par comes to an end on 30 April 2019. The tax rate for the full year may be \par impacted by relative contributions from Nigeria and Angola, but, as \par required by accounting standards, the rate communicated for the half \par year is expected to prevail for the full year. The tax rate is lower \par than previously communicated to the market due to significantly \par improved cash extractions from Nigeria.\par \par Net earnings\par \par Headline earnings, which exclude capital items, grew 11% to R849 \par million resulting in headline earnings per share for continuing \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of 132.0c reflecting a 10% improvement from 119.7c. Net \par profit for the period declined 3% to R871 million and benefitted \par from lower net finance costs and a lower tax rate. \par \par Financial position\par The group's financial position continues to improve with gearing at \par 39% from 51% in the comparative period and 45% at year-end in \par September 2017. The balance sheet remains strong, with key ratios \par firmly under control and adequate facilities available to fund all \par of the group's activities. This is largely attributable to net debt \par balances reducing by 27% to R3.8 billion from R5.2 billion resulting \par from the higher cash balances in restricted areas and the strengthening \par of the Rand against the US dollar.\par \par Cash extraction in the Rest of Africa\par \par The Rand equivalent of cash balances held in the currently cash \par restricted areas of Angola and Zimbabwe increased by 25% to R3.6 billion \par on the back of strong cash generation in Angola and Zimbabwe and slower \par than expected repatriation of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  due to in country US dollar \par shortages. In Angola, 95% of cash is hedged and management continues to \par monitor the region closely.\par \par Cash balances in Zimbabwe have grown to R816 million from R654 million at\par 30 September 2017. Following intensive engagements with customers and the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zimbabwe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Nampak expects to be allocated foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on \par a monthly basis, as from the second half of the 2018 financial year.\par \par Cash balances and extraction rates in Angola, Zimbabwe and Nigeria are \par as follows:\par \par                                                                      Non- \par                                                Restricted      restricted\par 31 March 2018                          Angola   Zimbabwe Total    Nigeria\par Cash on hand (Rm)          2 748        816 3 564        410\par Hedged cash (Rm)                        2 622         \u8212\'20\'142 2 622         \u8212\'20\'143\par % cash hedged                              95         \u8212\'20\'142    74         \u8212\'20\'143\par Cash extraction rate (%)1                  61          6    37        137\par \par                                                                      Non-\par                                                 Restricted    restricted\par 30 September 2017                        Angola Zimbabwe Total    Nigeria\par Cash on hand (Rm)                         2 188      654 2 842        828\par Hedged cash (Rm)                          1 954       \u8212\'20\'142 1 954         \u8212\'20\'143\par % cash hedged                                89       \u8212\'20\'142    69         \u8212\'20\'143\par Cash extraction rate (%)1                    47       40    47         93\par \par \par                                                         Non-\par                                                 Restricted     restricted\par 31 March 2017                            Angola Zimbabwe Total    Nigeria\par Cash on hand (Rm)                         1 436      426 1 862        955\par Hedged cash (Rm)                          1 107       \u8212\'20\'142 1 107        344\par % cash hedged                                77       \u8212\'20\'142               36\par Cash extraction rate (%)1                   80                         80\par \par 1 Liquidity ratio of invoices presented for payment in the period.\par 2 There are currently no appropriate hedges available in Zimbabwe.\par 3 Cash balances in Nigeria are no longer considered restricted as a \par   consequence of the liquidity that has been provided by the introduction \par   of the NAFEX.\par \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate movements\par \par Nampak has sizeable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outside South Africa and, as a result, its \par performance is impacted by various foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par movements for key markets are set out in the following table:\par \par Average rates\par \par                                     31 Mar    31 Mar        %   30 Sep\par                                       2018      2017   change     2017\par ZAR/GBP                              17.35     16.83       (3)   16.96\par ZAR/EUR                              15.36     14.54       (6)   14.78\par ZAR/USD                              12.78     13.57        6    13.38\par NGN/USD                             359.75    311.69      (15)  321.90\par AOA/USD                             189.76    171.73      (10)  171.74\par \par Closing rates\par \par          31 Mar   30 Sep         %   31 Mar\par                                       2018     2017    change     2017\par ZAR/GBP                              16.62    18.17         9    16.83\par ZAR/EUR                              14.57    15.98         9    14.29\par ZAR/USD                              11.86    13.56        13    13.41\par NGN/USD                             360.00   358.99         \u8212\'20\'14   314.29\par AOA/USD                             218.64   171.75       (27)  171.73\par \par The Angolan kwanza has been devalued through a series of controlled \par auctions by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Angolan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and has devalued by 27% at the \par end of March 2018 compared to 30 September 2017. While the strengthening \par of the Rand against the US dollar has adversely impacted the translation \par of foreign earnings for Rest of Africa territories, the translation of \par the group's dollar denominated borrowings was positively impacted. The \par Rand's weakened position against the Pound benefitted the European region. \par While there has been no material devaluation in the closing rate of the \par Nigerian naira since the introduction of the NAFEX market in April 2017, \par the average rate reflects a 15% devaluation against the US dollar for the \par comparative period.\par \par The stronger Rand/US dollar rate used to mark to market other monetary \par items led to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  translation losses of R149 million, most \par of which are unrealised.\par \par Capital expenditure\par \par Capital expenditure for the period more than halved to R206 million from \par R470 million for the same period in 2017 as a result of more stringent \par capital allocation by management. Capital expenditure for the full year \par is expected to be similar to, if not below, the 2017 year amount of \par R735 million.\par \par Trading performance\par \par                                            Revenue     Trading profit\par R million                             H1 2018  H1 2017  H1 2018 H1 2017\par Metals                                  5 849    5 570      925     883\par Plastics                                2 393    2 400      121      89\par Paper                                     603      698       78      57\par Corporate services                          \u8212\'20\'14        \u8212\'20\'14       40      56\par Continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                    8 845    8 668    1 164   1 085\par Discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :\par Glass                                     720      663      (55)     23\par Group total                             9 565    9 331    1 109   1 108\par \par \par                                                      Trading margin (%)\par R million                                           H1 2018    H1 2017\par Metals                                                 15.8       15.9\par Plastics                                                5.1        3.7\par Paper                                                  12.9        8.2\par Corporate services                     \u8212\'20\'14          \u8212\'20\'14 \par Continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                   13.2       12.5\par Discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :                     \par Glass                                                  (7.6)       3.5\par Group total                                            11.6       11.9\par \par Continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par Metals\par \par Revenue for the Metals Division increased by 5% propelled by robust \par demand in South Africa and Nigeria. Volume growth was substantially \par higher than GDP growth rates in both countries and suggests increasing \par can pack shares and improved consumer confidence. Buoyant volume growth \par and capacity rationalisation as a result of the closure of the Bevcan \par Cape Town line are expected to significantly mitigate any risk of \par underutilised capacity in Bevcan SA. Bevcan Angola and DivFood in \par South Africa also reported good volume growth compared to the prior \par period and contributed to the strong performance of this division.\par \par Bevcan SA is making good progress with the closure of the Cape Town can \par line which will save annual operating costs of R50 million and a further \par R5 million saving at head office level. DivFood experienced a pleasing \par recovery as the fish category performed well, on the back of an improved\par fish catch and substitution with imported bulk frozen fish. The vegetable \par category continued to do well due to overall improved consumer sentiment \par which drove both revenue growth and margin improvement. As a result, \par revenue for Metals in South Africa rose by 7%. Excellent safety standards \par and efficiency gains as a result of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  excellence initiatives \par contributed to improved margins.\par \par Despite pleasing volume growth, revenue growth at Bevcan Angola was \par moderated by the strengthening of the Rand against the US dollar. Trading \par profit in country was reduced to the extent that ends previously sold by \par the entity are now being directly supplied from South Africa, priced in \par hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and raw material cost pressures emanating from the \par devaluation of the kwanza. The conversion of the tin plate line to \par aluminium is being held in abeyance in anticipation of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par allocation from the government. In addition to selling ends directly from \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Africa\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , management has introduced further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to mitigate \par the exposure of the group in this country by limiting raw material purchases \par to the extent customers and/or the government are able to avail foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Should foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  not be made available to secure raw \par material inputs for customers, production will be constrained, which will \par attenuate the build-up of cash in Angola.\par \par Bevcan Nigeria volumes grew significantly, driven largely by the malt \par category. The recent introduction of excise duty on alcohol beverages has \par not had a discernible impact on volume to date. Additional volumes\par improved \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efficiencies and safety records have been maintained.\par \par The diversified canning \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the Rest of Africa were impacted by \par lower sales in Nigeria and Kenya. Kenya was challenged by backward \par integration by a major customer and a turnaround is in progress to improve \par the profitability of this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The feasibility of a food can line \par in Lagos, Nigeria is also being investigated to cater for anticipated \par growth in the food sector as the economy recovers.\par \par Plastics\par \par This division was impacted by loss of bottle volumes in South Africa and \par Europe which was mitigated by growth in closures in South Africa and \par excellent performance by Zimbabwean entities. As a result, overall revenue \par was flat. Stringent cost management in Europe, capacity filling initiatives \par in South Africa and the strong performance in Zimbabwe saw trading profit \par grow 36% to R121 million from R89 million.\par \par Revenue for Rigid Plastics in South Africa remained steady despite the \par volume lost as a result of a lower allocation of a tender as a key customer \par consolidated its manufacturing footprint. This, coupled with lower preform \par volumes as a result of backward integration by two customers in 2017, led\par to lower efficiencies and hence lower margins for liquid packaging.\par Initiatives to increase capacity utilisation and strong water container \par demand throughout the country mitigated these losses especially in regions \par heavily impacted by the drought. Good growth in closures, following new \par customers gained and additional volumes from existing customers, also \par filled the gap leading to relatively flat revenue growth in South Africa.\par \par In response to a disappointing performance, a turnaround plan has been \par approved which will result in a reduction in headcount of approximately\par 300 employees. Two low margin businesses have been earmarked for disposal, \par three additional plants will be consolidated into two existing facilities, \par warehousing coupled with supply chain will be optimised and an investment \par into selected new equipment will result in efficiency gains. Once fully \par implemented, profitability is expected to improve by R131 million per\par annum, with once off costs of R106 million to implement the restructuring \par over an 18 month period from June 2018 and capital investment of some \par R66 million. The restructuring will result in a more sustainable and \par profitable business by FY2020.\par \par Liquid Cartons in South Africa continues to perform well against the \par backdrop of a tough trading environment at the lower end of the beverage \par sector. Business development \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to leverage the environmental \par advantage of cartons are underway to increase capacity utilisation.\par \par The performance in the Rest of Africa was exceptional and demand in \par Zimbabwe was driven by increased market share from new customers and \par new products as well as the ability to manage \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure \par through stricter credit terms. Margins improved in excess of revenue and \par led to better margins for the region and the division overall. Nampak \par will continue working closely with customers and banks in order to \par continue the supply of packaging products in the market. Strict credit \par limits have been imposed on Nampak's Zimbabwe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to manage the \par risk associated with limited US dollar liquidity in that country.\par \par In spite of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nampak Plastics Europe\f28 \uc2 \f2  being impacted by lower volumes, \par management's focus on turning this business around, managing costs and \par improving \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efficiencies has begun to yield results and the \par operating loss was reduced by 72% to R11 million compared to the prior \par period. This further contributed towards improved margins for the \par Plastics Division of 5.1% from 3.7% in the comparative period. This \par business is expected to return to profitability by the end of the \par financial year.\par \par Paper\par \par Revenue contracted for the period as a result of lower carton sales in \par Zambia and Malawi following a change of pack strategy by brewers in these \par countries. A pleasing recovery in carton sales in Nigeria led to improved\par profitability off modestly higher sales. Tobacco case sales in Zimbabwe \par were moderately lower in light of limited availability of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \par but contributed to improved profitability. Ongoing initiatives to service \par this region collectively continued and Malawi was restructured to function \par as a depot in order to improve profitability of this region going forward.\par \par Discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par Glass\par \par This division experienced good revenue growth, driven by volume growth. \par Performance has, however, been impacted by internal production inefficiencies \par and skills issues which are in the process of being addressed. Whilst energy \par supply challenges were resolved by stabilising the electricity into the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the beginning of the calendar year, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  challenges \par continued, coupled with high depreciation reflective of its capital intensity, \par this division was not profitable and made a trading loss of R55 million for the \par period. Management has decided to dispose of the Glass \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as it is \par difficult to justify further capital allocation to this division to the Board \par and shareholders, with its current performance, owing to lower than required \par returns on invested capital. Recovery plans are in place, relevant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par skills have been introduced and there are early signs of improvement with a \par better pack-to-melt ratio for the month of April, subsequent to the reporting \par period.\par \par Trading performance by region is as follows:\par \par                        Revenue       Trading profit\par R million                               H1 2018 H1 2017  H1 2018 H1 2017\par South Africa                              5 208   4 953      527     458\par Rest of Africa                            2 973   2 933      608     610\par Europe                                      664     782      (11)    (39) \par Corporate services                            \u8212\'20\'14       \u8212\'20\'14       40      56\par Continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                      8 845   8 668    1 164   1 085\par Discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :\par South Africa (Glass)                        720     663      (55)     23\par Group total                               9 565   9 331    1 109   1 108\par \par \par Trading margin (%)\par R million                                              H1 2018  H1 2017\par South Africa             10.1      9.2\par Rest of Africa                                            20.5     20.8\par Europe                                                    (1.7)    (5.0) \par Corporate services                                           \u8212\'20\'14        \u8212\'20\'14 \par Continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                      13.2     12.5\par Discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :\par South Africa (Glass)                                      (7.6)     3.5\par Group total                                               11.6     11.9\par \par \par Outlook\par \par With improving business confidence and higher economic growth forecast \par for South Africa, demand for packaging is expected to grow at increased \par rates in 2018 and 2019. This, together with improved consumer sentiment, \par will boost beverage can market growth and management anticipates that\par additional capacity by a new entrant in the beverage can market is likely \par to be absorbed in the medium term.\par \par The closure of the Cape Town plant will remove 700 million beverage cans' \par capacity in this market. Consequently, cost savings from a reduced \par manufacturing footprint and gains from improved operating efficiencies \par are also expected to mitigate the impact of a new entrant into the market. \par The momentum at DivFood is expected to continue for the rest of the year. \par Top line growth in Plastics is expected to remain challenging as management\par will focus on turning this business around and offset the impact of reduced \par volumes by capacity filling for the rest of the year. Cost savings from the \par extensive restructuring plan to be implemented over the next 12 - 18 months \par should result in improved profitability going forward.\par \par The group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the Rest of Africa are anticipated to continue \par generating cash as demand in Angola, the recovering economy in Nigeria and \par limited competition in Zimbabwe will drive demand for packaging products. \par Overall performance will, however, be impacted by macroeconomic dynamics. \par Unless the kwanza is further devalued and/or initiatives to repatriate \par foreign reserves are progressed in Angola, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shortages are \par expected to continue for the rest of 2018. While the improving oil price \par will assist in increasing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, management will continue \par to only operate to the extent customers and the government are able to supply \par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  required for raw material inputs. If liquidity in Zimbabwe \par does not improve in the second half, performance will be subdued as more \par stringent requirements for customers have been put in place to limit exposure \par in that country. The restructuring in the other entities in the Rest of Africa \par will continue to service this region optimally.\par \par The European business will keep its focus on securing additional volume from \par second-tier dairies and optimise its structure. This business is expected to \par return to profitability during the 2018 financial year, one year earlier than \par previously guided.\par \par Dividend\par \par Despite significantly improved gearing and improved cash extraction from \par Nigeria, no dividend was declared for the period as a consequence of significant \par cash balances currently held in restricted areas.\par \par On behalf of the board\par \par TT Mboweni       AM de Ruyter     GR Fullerton\par Chairman         Chief executive officer     Chief financial officer\par \par Bryanston\par 30 May 2018\par \par Condensed group statement of comprehensive income\par \par                                                 Restated          Restated\par                                     Unaudited  Unaudited           Audited\par                                      6 months   6 months              Year\par                            ended      ended             ended\par                                        31 Mar     31 Mar  Change    30 Sep\par  R million                    Notes      2018       2017       %      2017\par Continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                               \par Revenue                               8 845.3    8 668.2       2  17 401.8\par Operating profit                  3   1 043.5    1 109.1      (6)  1 430.2\par Finance costs                          (270.3)    (243.6)           (508.8) \par Finance income                          181.5      128.8             287.4\par Share of net profit/(loss)                                         \par from associates and joint                                          \par ventures                                  2.3       (2.3)              0.1\par Profit before tax                       957.0      992.0      (4)  1 208.9\par Income tax expense                      (86.0)     (96.2)           (304.0) \par Profit for the period from                                         \par continu