   in cash generated from operating activities of R227.8 million and a \par   decrease in cash utilised in investing activities of R227.8 million for \par   the six months ended 31 March 2017.\par \par 2 As indicated on the condensed group statement of financial position, \par   US dollar indexed Angolan kwanza bonds were reclassified from cash \par   equivalents to loan receivables after a reassessment of their nature \par   in terms of IAS7: Statement of Cash flows. As a result of this \par   reclassification, the movement in these assets is now presented as \par   investing activities. The March 2017 comparative has been restated.\par \par Notes\par \par 1. Basis of preparation\par \par The condensed interim financial statements are prepared in accordance \par with the requirements of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSE Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  Listings Requirements for \par interim reports, and the requirements of the Companies Act of South \par Africa applicable to condensed financial statements. The Listings \par Requirements require interim reports to be prepared in accordance with \par and contain the information required by IAS 34 Interim Financial \par Reporting, as well as the SAICA Financial Reporting Guides as issued \par by the Accounting Practices Committee and the Financial Pronouncements \par as issued by the Financial Reporting Standards Council.\par \par The interim financial statements have been prepared under the supervision \par of the chief financial officer, GR Fullerton CA(SA).\par \par 2. Accounting policies\par \par The accounting policies adopted and methods of computation used are \par consistent with those applied for the group's 2017 annual financial \par statements.\par \par New and revised International Financial Reporting Standards in issue \par and effective for the current financial year\par \par The group adopted all amendments or improvements to standards or \par interpretations that became effective during the current financial \par year with no effect on the financial statements of the group. No new \par standards were effective for the current financial year and the group \par did not elect to adopt any of these standards earlier than their \par effective dates.\par \par New and revised International Financial Reporting Standards in issue \par but not yet effective for the current financial year\par \par At the date of authorisation of these financial statements, the following \par standards, amendments to existing standards and interpretations were in \par issue but not yet effective for the current year and have not been early \par adopted.\par \par These standards, amendments and interpretations will be effective for \par annual periods beginning after the dates listed below:\par \par IFRS 9: Financial Instruments\par \par The standard is effective for years commencing on or after 1 January 2018. \par The standard will be adopted by the group for the financial reporting \par period commencing 1 October 2018.\par \par IFRS 9 provides guidance on the classification, measurement and \par recognition of financial assets and financial liabilities and replaces \par IAS 39. The standard establishes three measurement categories for financial \par assets: amortised cost, fair value through other comprehensive income and fair\par value through profit and loss. Classification of financial assets into these \par categories is dependent on the entity's business model and the characteristics \par of the contractual cash flows of the specific financial asset. There were no \par significant changes to the classification guidance for financial liabilities.\par \par IFRS 9 introduces a new expected credit loss impairment model that replaces \par the incurred loss impairment model used in IAS 39.\par \par The group will have to design impairment models incorporating new principles \par such as twelve months expected credit loss, life time expected credit loss, \par forward-looking information and time value of money in order to comply with \par expected credit loss impairments under IFRS 9. The group has performed a \par preliminary assessment, the results thereof indicate no material adjustment \par is required.\par \par The group is still to make a decision on the transition method applied. \par \par IFRS 15: Revenue From Contracts With Customers\par The standard is effective for years commencing on or after 1 January 2018.\par The standard will be adopted by the group for the financial reporting period \par commencing 1 October 2018.\par \par IFRS 15 requires an entity to recognise revenue in such a manner as to depict \par the transfer of the goods or services to customers, at an amount representing \par the consideration to which the entity expects to be entitled in exchange for \par those goods or services. The standard has a 5-step process to be applied to \par all contracts with customers. The standard provides guidance for identifying \par the contract with the customer, identification of the deliverables \par (performance obligations), determination of the transaction price (including \par the treatment of variability in the transaction price and significant financing \par components), how to allocate the transaction price and when to recognise revenue.\par \par The group has assessed its significant contracts with customers in line with \par the new standard and notes that the treatment of contracts with variable pricing \par will be altered under IFRS 15, however no material impacts are otherwise expected \par with respect to revenue measurement and timing.\par \par The group is still to make a decision on the transition method to be applied.\par \par IFRS 16: Leases\par \par The standard is effective for years commencing on or after 1 January 2019. The \par standard will be adopted by the group for the financial reporting period commencing \par 1 October 2019.\par \par IFRS 16 requires a lessee to recognise a right of use asset and lease obligations \par for all leases except for short term leases, or leases of low value assets which \par may be treated similarly to operating leases under the current standard IAS 17 \par if the exceptions are applied. A lessee measures its lease obligation at the present \par value of future lease payments, and recognises a right of use asset initially \par measured at the same amount as the lease obligation including costs directly related \par to entering into the lease. Right of use assets are subsequently treated in a \par similar way to other assets such as property, plant and equipment or intangible \par assets dependent on the nature of the underlying item.\par \par The group has assessed a majority of its significant lease agreements, in particular \par those relating to property rentals, and the preliminary assessment indicates that \par material adjustments to non-current assets, non- current liabilities and EBIDTA \par are to be expected as a result of the new standard. The current estimate of the \par impact of adopting IFRS 16 on the March 2018 reported numbers is as follows:\par \par * increase in net assets: R429 million\par * increase in EBITDA: R101 million\par * decrease in profit for the period: R17 million\par \par Management continues to assess the implications of the remaining individually \par insignificant lease agreements in which the group is the lessee which may cause the \par final impact to differ from the estimates provided above.\par \par The group is still to make a decision on the transition method to be applied or the \par application of exceptions related to short term and low value asset leases.\par \par Restatement of comparatives\par \par The comparatives to the condensed statement of comprehensive income (March 2017 and \par September 2017), have been restated for the impact of the Nampak Glass Division \par being recognised as a discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the current period. Refer \par note 4.\pa