\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-South Africa's rand recovers, stocks dip as European shares fall\b0\par\par\uc2 504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2016\par 17:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Rand recovers on China's c.bank yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Yields on government bonds hit one week high\par \par \uc2 * Stocks dragged lower as European shares slip (Adds latest prices, analyst comments)\par \par \uc2 JOHANNESBURG, Jan 18 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rand firmed on Monday as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped ease global investor aversion toward emerging market assets in trade thinned by a holiday in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Stocks inched lower on Monday to end just in the red as a rebound in European shares fizzled after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 )said it would quiz euro zone lenders about high levels of bad loans.\par \par \uc2 By 1625 GMT the rand inched 0.09 percent firmer to 16.7670 per dollar, hanging on to earlier gains as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China\uc2 's efforts to stabilise the yuan saw demand for riskier assets in developing economies improve.\par \par \uc2 Lingering concerns over the local economy would however limit the rand's recovery, analysts said, more so with consumer inflation data due on Wednesday likely to show a spike in food prices owing to severe drought conditions.\par \par \uc2 "Expect the rand to battle to improve in the weeks and months ahead. We need quite a bit of water of pass under the bridge before the rand can start clawing back some of that improved sentiment," said economist at Credit Guarantee Insurance Luke Doig.\par \par \uc2 Bonds weakened, unable to shake the increased risk associated with South African assets, with the yield on the government's 10-year issue rising 15.5 basis points to 9.87 percent, its highest in one week.\par \par \uc2 On the bourse the benchmark Top-40 index slipped 0.03 percent to 42,093 while the wider All-share index finished 0.18 percent lower at 46,876.\par \par \uc2 Decliners included petrochemicals group \uc2 Sasol\uc2 , which shed 3.75 percent as Brent and U.S. WTI futures were last down 0.5 percent at $28.88 and $29.38 a barrel, respectively, just above 12-year lows.\par \par \uc2 Technical factors could, however, give the market some support this week, according to analysts.\par \par \uc2 "We are looking pretty oversold at these levels on traditional technicals," said Lloyd Priestman, a markets analyst at Caleo Capital in Johannesburg.\par \par \uc2 Momentum indicators such as the 14-day RSI suggest the Top-40, which has shed 8 percent to date in 2015, has strayed into oversold territory, making a support rally likely in the coming week.\par \par \uc2 Gainers on the day included steel-maker \uc2 ArcelorMittal\uc2  South Africa, which added 9.7 percent to 5.89 rand after it said that 68 percent of its 4.5 billion rand ($270 million) rights offer had been taken up and that its parent group would subscribe for the remainder.\par \par \uc2 Decliners pipped advancers 194 to 127 and over 240 million shares changed hands, a little over the 2015 daily average. (Reporting by Mfuneko Toyana and Ed Stoddard; Editing by Richard Balmforth) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-18T17:51:50.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160118ec1i00k2c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
