\par\par \uc2 Central Banks\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Africa Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Chief Sees No Point in  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 -Market  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cui  And Dan Keeler   \par \uc2 840 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2015\par 18:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The head of \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said he sees no point in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, even as the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently hit its weakest level on record and the economy grapples with plunging commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 "No \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , no matter how much reserves it has, can take on the market and stem the depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Lesetja Kganyago, governor of South African Reserve Bank, said Monday in an interview with The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 South Africa in many ways is regarded as a vulnerable economy among emerging markets. The country's external debt has nearly doubled since 2009 as falling commodity prices have taken a toll. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rand, hit its weakest level on record in late September, losing 26% of its value against the dollar over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kganyago said the depreciation of the rand reflects the sharp declines in commodities that South Africa produces, including gold, platinum and iron ore. The South African \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began to adopt a hands-off approach in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2001 after unsuccessful and costly attempts to defend the level of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We've learned our lesson that if you allow your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fluctuate and find its level, it becomes an effective shock absorber," Mr. Kganyago said.\par \par \uc2 The rand has been bearing the brunt of South Africa's economic woes. The African country's economy has been growing below 3% every year since 2012, a disappointing rate for a developing country. As commodity prices began to take a downturn in 2014, demand for South Africa's exports fell further, leading to a widening current-account deficit. As a result, the government has been borrowing heavily both home and abroad to fund the gap.\par \par \uc2 The country's economy shrank by 1.3% in the second quarter, and is expected to grow 1.5% each year this year and the next, according to Mr. Kganyago.\par \par \uc2 The country's external debt is about 48% of its gross domestic products, he said, compared with Turkey's 49% and Brazil's 14%, according to official data.\par \par \uc2 But the bulk of South Africa's external debt is denominated in rand, which is "something very fundamental that distinguishes South Africa from many other emerging-market countries," he said. Thanks to the country's liquid debt market, foreigners have invested heavily in the rand-denominated market, holding about 40% of its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, he said.\par \par \uc2 Some market observers are worried that the growing debt pile could expose South Africa to the risk of a selloff by foreign bondholders, raising yields and funding costs for the economy. The foreign participation is a double-edged sword, as foreign investors are probably reluctant to sell the rand-denominated debt because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses they would have to take if they decide to exit, Mr. Kganyago said.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, South African companies and institutional investors have been investing in markets outside the country, especially other African nations. These investments are often made in dollars or euros, and have risen dramatically in rand terms, triggering more capital repatriation to South Africa. "This tends to reinforce the resilience of the South African economy," Mr. Kganyago said.\par \par \uc2 Wild \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have been a challenge for emerging-market policy makers, who are already grappling with a potential tightening of monetary policy in the U.S. and massive capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 "It has not been an easy time for emerging markets, but those that have demonstrated a solid history of macroeconomic management, which stayed the course and communicated very clearly and ensured they are acting consistently, will be distinguished from those emerging markets that act abruptly," Mr. Kganyago said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the massive outflows from emerging markets, South Africa continued to see foreign capital flow in. According to the South African Research Bank, nonresidential purchases of equities this year through August were $3.255 billion, while the bond market saw a small outflow of $467 million.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kganyago also defended the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's July decision to raise rates, despite the disappointing economic growth in the country. Some analysts called the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move premature, as inflation was running at 5% that month\u8212\'20\'14within its target between 3% and 6%.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kganyago said policy makers were concerned about the depreciation of the rand and its pass-through effect on inflation. At the same time, other indicators also suggested risks of rising prices, such as electricity tariffs, food prices and an increase in minimum wages. The bank decided to tighten the monetary policy to stop the vicious spiral where higher prices would feed into inflation expectations, he said.\par \par \uc2 In September, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to stay put, as the data and its forecasts indicated that inflation largely would remain within its target. But the bank said in a statement following the meeting that it is still in a tightening cycle.\par \par \uc2 Write to Carolyn Cui at \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120151006eba60009e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
47
