\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Markets Hit Hard as Global Rout Continues\b0\par\par\uc2 1,268 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2015\par 20:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Raymond Zhong, Patrick McGroarty and Ben Otto \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The conflagration now engulfing emerging markets was a long time in the making. Bad news from China provided the spark.\f28 \par \par \uc2 As anxieties about the world's No. 2 economy mount, investors are pulling money from the developing countries that feed China's vast industrial machine with natural resources.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia, Malaysia, South Africa and other commodity exporters have seen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumble to multiyear lows against the U.S. dollar, limiting their central banks' scope to cut interest rates and revive lagging economies. Governments also failed to reorient, during the years of plenty, away from raw materials and toward economic activities, such as manufacturing, that would benefit from a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The prospect of a yearslong, China-led downshift--and the lack of policy ammunition to combat it--is making these countries a target.\par \par \uc2 "We were all fully aware emerging markets were vulnerable," said Malcolm Charles, a portfolio manager at Investec Asset Management in Cape Town, which has $120 billion under management. Now, he said, "I can only see red on my screen. There's a complete pricing-out of risk assets."\par \par \uc2 Clouds had been gathering for months.\par \par \uc2 Anticipating a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest-rate increase, investors have been steadily repatriating funds to safer shores, causing the U.S. dollar to begin strengthening against some emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2013.\par \par \uc2 This month's accelerated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines are reminiscent of the Asian financial crisis that started in 1997. But the foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt built up before that period's precipitous declines was larger than today's as a share of the economy. Also, fewer Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are pegged to the U.S. dollar, freeing central banks from having to empty their reserves to defend exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 Global money managers pulled $26 billion out of emerging-market stocks and bonds between Jan. 1 and July 31, according to data from EPFR Global, a company that tracks fund flows around the world.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI Emerging Markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  index, a broad gauge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of developing nations, is at its lowest level since June 2010, while the MSCI Emerging Markets equity index is down 15% this year, on track for its worst annual performance since 2011, in U.S. dollar terms.\par \par \uc2 Ebbing demand from China is rippling through its suppliers' economies. "There are piles of coal in ports," said Supriatna Suhala, executive director of the Indonesian Coal Mining Association. Big coal miners, he said, have reduced the number of daily shifts and sent workers home to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 In Thailand, a major supplier of rubber to China's tire factories, Perk Lertwangpong, a rubber farmer and former president of the Rubber Planter Cooperatives, said he expects exports to fall by a fifth in 2015 compared with last year.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia, which sells coal, minerals and palm oil to China, was riding high not long ago. Economic growth in 2012 was clocked at around 6%. This year, the stock market is down more than 20%. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupiah, is down 12.5% for the year and is hovering at its lowest level since the Asian financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 For Indonesian commodity companies that have borrowed significantly in U.S. dollars, there are no easy answers. Bumi Resources, one of the country's largest coal miners, has seen its stock price fall 95% over the past three years.\par \par \uc2 China's latest woes haven't come as a complete surprise. \uc2 Dileep Srivastava\uc2 , a director at Bumi, said his company may have been "first off the block," several years ago, to diversify away from China and toward India and others. "We recognized and sensed that China was likely to be a continuing problem," he said.\par \par \uc2 Indonesian Trade Minister Tom Lembong said straitened circumstances could aid the country's efforts to become less resource-reliant.\par \par \uc2 "Short term, we have to let the market do its work," Mr. Lembong said. "The depreciation of the rupiah has already done what it's supposed to do. We've swung from at least four years of trade deficits to, this year, a trade surplus."\par \par \uc2 In Malaysia, the combination of a falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the ringgit is down almost a quarter this year--and political turmoil--this time over alleged misdeeds involving the state-investment fund, \uc2 1Malaysia Development Bhd\uc2 .--has stirred memories of the late-1990s crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Given that sentiment is so bad over there, it's certainly the one we're most worried about in Asia," said Gareth Leather of Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors hold nearly half of Malaysia's government debt, and private overseas borrowing at the end of the second quarter was equivalent to more than a quarter of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia, for every dollar of short-term external debt, had roughly a dollar of international reserves as of Aug. 14, down from $3.70 at the end of 2013.\par \par \uc2 Countries that have reduced their external vulnerability have fared at least slightly better in the latest rout. India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shed a quarter of its value over a few months in 2013 after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  hinted it might reduce its monthly bond purchases. At the time, India relied heavily on foreign capital to fund imports.\par \par \uc2 But since then, it has built up foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and narrowed the trade deficit. Prime Minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2  has championed India as an attractive hub for low-cost manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 The rupee still slid on Monday, though, closing down 5.5% since the start of the year. India's benchmark stock index dropped 5.9%, to its lowest level in a year.\par \par \uc2 "As far as India is concerned," Finance Minister Arun Jaitley said in New Delhi, "our response at this stage is very clear: We have to strengthen our own economy."\par \par \uc2 South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rand, plummeted on Monday to all-time lows of beyond 14 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is rising. Economists warn growth could miss already-lackluster forecasts, leading to a second consecutive year of sub-2% expansion.\par \par \uc2 Yet \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is all but powerless to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , raising rates last month to bolster the rand despite economic distress.\par \par \uc2 Hardest hit are the gold, platinum and iron mines whose riches built South Africa into the continent's most developed economy. Beijing's demand for those metals pushed China past the U.S. as South Africa's top trading partner in 2009.\par \par \uc2 The reversal since then has been severe. \uc2 AngloGold Ashanti Ltd\uc2 . last week reported a loss of $142 million in the second quarter. Platinum miner \uc2 Lonmin PLC\uc2  said it would cut 6,000 jobs, nearly a fifth of its workforce, through 2017. \uc2 Glencore PLC\uc2  is cutting 380 jobs at a South African coal mine.\par \par \uc2 "There's a big crunch," said Anton van der Merwe, a director at I-Cat Environmental Solutions, a contractor that services roads and purifies water at some of South Africa's biggest mines.\par \par \uc2 As miners have laid off workers and trimmed costs, I-Cat has pursued new clients, such as telecoms. The closely held company is still making money, Mr. van der Merwe said. But profits were a fifth lower than expected in the first half of its current fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 "At the end of the day, it's affecting everybody," he said of the turmoil emanating from China.\par \par \uc2 Anjani Trivedi, Wilawan Watcharasakwet and Rajesh Roy contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Raymond Zhong at raymond.zhong@wsj.com, Ben Otto at ben.otto@wsj.com and Patrick McGroarty at patrick.mcgroarty@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 24, 2015 14:27 ET (18:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150824eb8o004rl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
