\par\par \b \uc2 OLOML - \uc2 OLD MUTUAL PLC\uc2  - \uc2 Old Mutual plc\uc2  preliminary results for the year ended 31 December 2014 - Part I\b0\par\par\uc2 29,671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2015\par 10:07\par \uc2 Johannesburg Stock Exchange\par \uc2 JSEXCH\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2015 Johannesburg Stock Exchange. All rights reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  preliminary results for the year ended 31 December 2014 - Part I\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par ISIN CODE: GB00B77J0862\par JSE SHARE CODE: OML\par NSX SHARE CODE: OLM\par ISSURE CODE: OLOML\par \par NEWS RELEASE\par Ref 113-114/15\par 27 February 2015\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  preliminary results for the year ended 31 December 2014\par \par Strong financial performance\par \par -  Adjusted operating profit (AOP) of GBP1.6 billion up 16% in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , flat in reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par -  AOP earnings per share 17.9p up 13% n constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , down 3% in reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par -  Final dividend of 6.25p up 4%, with a total dividend of 8.7p up 7%\par -  Net client cash flow of GBP4.9 billion\par -  FUM at GBP319.4 billion up 6% in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , 9% in reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par -  GBP653 million net free surplus generated\par -  Group ROE 13.3%, within target range of 12-15%\par -  The Group has appropriate and resilient levels of capital, liquidity and leverage\par \par Transforming our future prospects in our chosen markets\par \par -  Building an African financial services champion, with GBP593 million committed to date:\par           -  Acquisition of UAP gives \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  significant scale and broad product offering in fast-growing East African markets\par           -  Nedbank acquisition of 20% stake in ETI provides banking access across 36 African countries\par           -  1.8 million new customers in Africa; 15.8 million African customers in total\par \par -  Building the leading retail investment business in the UK:\par \par           -  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  acquisition completed 25 February 2015: provides high quality discretionary investment management capability\par           -  Gross proceeds of European disposals and OMAM IPO (GBP562 million) redeployed into \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  acquisition (GBP585\par              million)\par           -  Good progress in vertical integration: over 30% of NCCF into OMGI from Platform; OMGI now managing 12% of Platform FUM\par     \par -  Successful IPO of \f28 \uc2 \f2 OM Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OMAM) provides the business with the financial flexibility to grow\par \par Focus on strategic and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  delivery and driving returns from our investments\par \par -  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets strong growth in South Africa, profits up 25%\par -  Nedbank profits up 14%\par -  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth NCCF GBP3.7 billion, up 61%, with open book NCCF of GBP4.8 billion\par -  OMAM NCCF GBP5.8 billion\par -  Focus on integrating acquisitions to drive growth in our markets\par \par Julian Roberts, Group Chief Executive, commented:\par \par  "I am delighted with the way the Group has performed in 2014. We are executing our strategy in a sustainable and profitable way. We have\par taken significant steps in building an African financial services champion. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  in South Africa grew profits by 25%, Nedbank grew profits\par by 14% and collaboration amongst our South African business continues. In the Rest of Africa, we are seeing steady growth which we expect to\par accelerate following the acquisition of UAP and investment in ETI.\par \par "This has been a watershed year in our efforts to build the UK's leading retail investment business. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth has substantially\par completed its investment in building a modern UK retail investment company with its acquisition of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2 , in addition to Intrinsic earlier\par in the year. We are already seeing the benefits of our vertically integrated model and we are ready for the introduction of the liberalised pension\par regime on 6th April. OMAM has had an exceptionally good year with profits up 32% and a successful listing on the New York Stock Exchange.\par \par "We have invested significantly and reallocated capital in our key markets to grow profits over the long term while holding appropriate levels of\par capital and leverage. Our focus for 2015 is on integrating the acquisitions, delivering the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  improvements and creating value from\par these investments."\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  preliminary results for the year ended 31 December 2014\par \par Enquiries\par Investor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Relations\par Patrick Bowes\f28 \uc2 \f2              UK   +44 20 7002 7440\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dominic Lagan\f28 \uc2 \f2              UK   +44 20 7002 7190\par Sizwe Ndlovu              SA    +27 11 217 1163\par Media\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 William Baldwin-Charles\f28 \uc2 \f2         +44 20 7002 7133\par                                +44 7834 524 833\par \par Notes to the financial summary on the front page of this announcement\par \par -     All figures refer to core continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Core continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exclude the results of the Bermuda business, which is\par       classified as non-core.\par \par -     Constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  figures are calculated by translating local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prior-period figures at the prevailing exchange rates for the\par       period under review.\par \par -     Adjusted operating profit (AOP) reflects the directors' view of the underlying long-term performance of the Group. AOP is a\par       measure of profitability which adjusts the IFRS profit measures for the specific items detailed in the notes in Part 4 of these\par       preliminary results and, as such, it is a non-GAAP measure.\par \par       For core life assurance and property and casualty businesses, AOP is based on a long-term investment return, including returns\par       on investments held by life funds in Group equity and debt instruments, and is stated net of income tax attributable to policyholder\par       returns. For all core businesses, AOP excludes goodwill impairment, the impact of accounting for intangibles acquired in a\par       business combination and costs related to completed acquisitions, revaluations of put options related to long-term incentive\par       schemes, profit/(loss) on acquisition/disposal of subsidiaries, associated undertakings and strategic investments, fair value\par       profits/(losses) on certain Group debt instruments and costs related to the fundamental restructuring of continuing businesses.\par       AOP includes dividends declared to holders of perpetual preferred callable securities. Old Mutual Bermuda and Nordic are treated\par       as non-core and discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the AOP disclosure. As such they are not included in AOP. Refer to note B1 for further\par       information on the basis of segmentation.\par \par       Adjusted operating earnings per share is calculated on the same basis as AOP. It is stated after tax attributable to AOP and non-\par       controlling interests. It excludes income attributable to Black Economic Empowerment trusts of listed subsidiaries. The calculation\par       of the adjusted weighted average number of shares includes own shares held in policyholders' funds and Black Economic\par       Empowerment trusts.\par \par Cautionary statement\par \par This announcement contains forward-looking statements relating to certain of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2 's plans and its current goals and\par expectations relating to its future financial condition, performance and results. By their nature, all forward-looking statements involve risk\par and uncertainty because they relate to future events and circumstances that are beyond \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2 's control, including, among other\par things, global, UK and South African, economic and business conditions, market-related risks such as fluctuations in interest rates and\par exchange rates, policies and actions of regulatory authorities, the impact of competition, inflation, deflation, the timing and impact of\par other uncertainties, future acquisitions or combinations within relevant industries, as well as the impact of tax and other legislation and\par regulations in territories where \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  or its affiliates operate.\par \par As a result, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2 's actual future financial condition, performance and results may differ materially from the plans, goals and\par expectations set out in its forward-looking statements. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  undertakes no obligation to update any forward-looking\par statements contained in this announcement or any other forward-looking statements that it may make.\par \par Notes to editors\par \par A webcast of the presentation on the preliminary results and Q&A will be broadcast live at 09:00 am GMT (11:00 am South African time)\par today on the Company's website \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.oldmutual.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.oldmutual.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Analysts and investors who wish to participate in the call should dial the following\par numbers and quote the pass-code 50709853#:\par \par UK/International                                 +44 20 3139 4830\par US                                               +1 718 873 9077\par South Africa                                     +27 21 672 4008\par \par Playback (available for 30 days from 27 February 2015), using pass-code 653969#:\par \par UK/International                                 +44 20 3426 2807\par \par Copies of these results, together with high-resolution images and biographical details of the directors of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2 , are available in\par electronic format to download from the Company's website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.oldmutual.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.oldmutual.com}}}\uc2 \f2 .\par \par The following documents, containing financial data for 2014 and 2013, are also available from the Company's website.\par \par -     Presentation slides\par -     Appendix slides\par -     Financial Disclosure Supplement\par -     MCEV Supplementary information\par -     Economic Capital Statement of Principles\par \par Sterling exchange rates\par \par                                                     Appreciation / (depreciation) of\par                                      2014    2013    local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against sterling\par                      Average Rate   17.87   15.10                              (18%)\par South African Rand\par                      Closing Rate   18.00   17.43                               (3%)\par                      Average Rate    1.65    1.57                               (5%)\par US Dollar\par                      Closing Rate    1.56    1.66                                 6%\par \par Part 1 - 2014 Annual Review\par \par Part 1 - 2014 Annual Review\par \par Contents\par News Release                         1\par Part 1 - 2014 Annual Review          4\par Group Review                         5\par         Overview                     5\par         Business review              8\par         Dividend policy             10\par         Board changes               10\par         Outlook                     10\par Part 2 - Financial Performance      11\par Part 3 - Detailed Business Review   24\par Part 4 - Financial Information      49\par \par Group highlights(1)                               2013\par                                                               (constant            2013 (as             \par                                                   2014        \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )   change   reported)    change   \par Adjusted operating profit (pre-tax, GBPm)(2)     1,605            1,388      16%       1,612         -   \par Adjusted operating earnings per share (pence)    17.9p            15.9p      13%       18.4p      (3%)   \par Group net margin(3)                              47bps            45bps     2bps       48bps    (1bps)   \par Return on equity(4)                              13.3%                                 13.6%   (30bps)   \par Net asset value per share (pence)(5)            140.3p           136.4p       3%      137.7p        2%   \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets MCEV value (GBPm)    3,108            2,860       9%       2,953        5%   \par Net client cash flow (GBPbn)                       4.9             14.6    (66%)        15.5     (68%)   \par Group customer numbers (millions)                 17.5                                  16.1        9%   \par Funds under management (GBPbn)                   319.4            301.7       6%       293.8        9%   \par Total dividend for the year (pence)               8.7p                -        -        8.1p        7%   \par \par (1) The figures in the table are in respect of core continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only, unless otherwise stated\par (2) IFRS profit after tax attributable to equity holders of the parent was GBP582 million for the year ended 31 December 2014 (2013: GBP705 million). A full\par     reconciliation of IFRS profit to AOP can be found In the Financial Performance review\par (3) Ratio of AOP before tax to average funds under management in the period. Nedbank calculation also includes average banking assets\par (4) RoE is calculated as core continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AOP (post-tax) divided by average ordinary shareholders' equity (i.e. excluding the perpetual preferred\par     callable securities). It also excludes the equity associated with non-core \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par (5) Net asset value per share is calculated as ordinary shareholders' equity (i.e. excluding the perpetual preferred callable securities) divided by the actual\par     shares in issue at the end of the period\par \par Group Review\par \par Overview\par \par A strong financial performance\par \par This has been a good year for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  with strong underlying financial performance, significant strategic developments and\par continued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  delivery. Net client cash flows (NCCF) for the Group, excluding our non-US affiliate, were GBP11.2 billion. Gross\par sales of GBP26.3 billion were up 11% in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with funds under management up 6% to GBP319.4 billion also in constant\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Profits grew strongly in the year up 16% in constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to GBP1.6 billion, flat in reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Group return on\par equity (RoE) at 13.3% was within our target range of 12 - 15%.\par \par Equity markets performed strongly in South Africa and the United States with the JSE-All Share up 18% on average and the Russell\par 1000 Value up 16% on average in the year. Equity market performance was more muted in the UK, with the average level of the FTSE\par 100 up 3%. We saw a further weakening of the rand with the average rate declining by 18% against sterling in the period while the\par closing year-end rand exchange rate declined only 3%. This has had a negative impact on our sterling reported results.\par \par In mixed macro-economic conditions\par \par Macro-economic conditions in South Africa remained relatively weak with GDP growing by 1.5%, mainly due to labour disputes in the\par mining and manufacturing sectors. Power shortages are expected to continue in 2015 with the consequence that GDP is forecasted to\par grow moderately by 2.1%. However, a prolonged period of low oil prices could benefit the South African consumer as a significant\par proportion of disposable income at the lower end of the socio-economic scale is spent on transport and food costs, both of which should\par reduce. Sub-Saharan Africa continued to experience strong growth of 4.8%, with growth predicted to increase marginally in the region to\par 4.9% in 2015.\par \par In the UK, the economy grew by 2.6% in 2014, the fastest since 2007, and similar levels of growth are expected in 2015. Inflation fell\par during the year and unemployment continued to fall. The US experienced GDP growth of 2.4% which is forecast to accelerate to 3.6% in\par 2015.\par \par Strategic overview\par \par Strategic delivery in our chosen markets\par \par This year has seen further progress in the transformation of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 . We have reshaped the business and in time this should lead to\par a different earnings profile for the Group. We have a simple, focused strategy based on growing in our chosen markets where we have\par significant competitive advantage: building an African financial services champion; building the leading retail investment business in the\par UK, and growing our multi-boutique asset management business in the US.\par \par The final substantive part of our streamlining, simplification and de-risking programme, embarked on in 2010, was completed with the\par minority Initial Public Offering (IPO) of \f28 \uc2 \f2 OM Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OMAM) on the New York Stock Exchange on 9 October 2014. We also\par disposed of a number of non-core European businesses in the year: the Skandia businesses in Austria, Germany, Poland and\par Liechtenstein, with France and Luxembourg sold on 2 February 2015.\par \par We have invested in the Group via acquisitions and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  improvements to maintain and enhance our performance. Significant\par investments include: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  for up to GBP585 million in February 2015, including GBP42 million in new equity, to provide \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old\par Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth with a high quality discretionary investment management capability, which was financed via the disposals of non-core\par European assets and from the proceeds of OMAM's IPO; in Africa, we announced our intention to acquire a majority stake in UAP\par Holdings (UAP), an East African insurance business for GBP162 million; we purchased an additional 25% of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2  for\par GBP63 million; and Nedbank bought a 20% stake in Ecobank Transnational Incorporated (ETI) for GBP305 million. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operationally\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , we\par are investing in new platform technology in the UK which will transform our customers' experience, boost product capability and lower\par our cost base from 2017 onwards.\par \par Our focus now is on integrating the acquisitions, delivering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  improvements and creating value from these investments.\par Following the completion of the UAP transaction in 2015, we will have deployed R3.6 billion of the R5 billion we identified to fund\par acquisitions. We are investing in Africa for the medium to long term and, while quality insurance assets in Africa have become\par increasingly scarce, we have maintained a disciplined approach to acquisitions, only deploying capital in line with our allocation criteria.\par Additionally, we ensure that any business we acquire has a strong cultural fit with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par We are building an African financial services champion\par \par At the heart of our strategy to build an African financial services champion is our strong Southern African franchise. We are making\par good progress in aligning \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Nedbank and Mutual & Federal (M&F) to become the leading financial services group in southern\par Africa. We are seeing more cross-selling between our businesses, with, for example, our South African Retail Affluent and Mass\par Foundation businesses selling more iWYZE products. We have previously stated that we were targeting pre-tax AOP revenue, cost and\par capital synergies of R1 billion by the end of 2017 and we are making good progress having identified more than 50% of the synergies.\par We are making a significant investment in our technology in South Africa and the Rest of Africa to improve the experience for customers\par and intermediaries, to provide simpler and more efficient back office processes, support growth in the rest of Africa and integrate\par platforms across our life, property & casualty and banking businesses. We have addressed areas which we needed improving: for\par example, new product launches have contributed to an increase in single premium sales in our Retail Affluent business by 29%; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old\par Mutual Investment Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OMIG) continues to deliver good investment performance in our key equity funds, including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\par Investors Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Active Quant Fund, and is showing improving performance in our key multi asset class fund, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old\par Mutual Balanced Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; and at M&F we are continuing to see the benefits of price remediation and claims savings initiatives proving\par particular successful.\par \par The regulatory environment in South Africa is expected to undergo significant transformation in the medium term as changes such as\par the proposed Retail Distribution Review (RDR), Solvency Asset Management, Pensions Reform, Treating Customers Fairly and Twin\par Peaks regulatory reform are implemented. We continue to engage constructively with the various regulatory authorities in this regard.\par \par Our offering in East Africa, where we are looking to buy controlling stakes in financial services businesses with both retail and wholesale\par capabilities, was significantly strengthened when we agreed to buy a majority stake of 60.7% in UAP Holdings, subject to various\par regulatory approvals. UAP has a strong position in East and Central Africa and a product offering that is highly complementary to our\par existing businesses. It is a sizeable business and one that provides a platform for us to expand in the fast growing East African region.\par In Kenya, UAP has the third largest Property & Casualty (P&C) market share; the second largest health insurance business; a\par substantial property investment portfolio and a fast growing life insurance business, which, when combined with our existing Kenyan life\par business, will be the fourth largest in the country. It has well established and diverse distribution networks. In Uganda, it has the second\par largest P&C and health insurance businesses, and the third largest life business. It also has small P&C businesses in Rwanda,\par Tanzania and South Sudan, and an insurance brokerage in the Democratic Republic of Congo.\par \par We expect the acquisition to complete in the first half of 2015, and following completion we will look to combine our Kenyan businesses\par to have one integrated financial services provider. Our focus initially will be on revenue generating initiatives. For example, we see\par excellent opportunities to offer P&C products utilising UAP expertise to Faulu's large retail client base, which is broadly similar to our\par mass foundation cluster in South Africa. In West Africa our life and P&C businesses have been growing organically and will look to grow\par further via partnerships and distribution deals. We have also rolled out new products in Nigeria, leveraging our South African expertise\par and tailored to local needs and culture, including a retail risk product and savings product. In Ghana, we launched a funeral policy in\par June and a credit life product sold via the Ecobank branches in July. We have also added a further 226 advisers in Ghana, bringing the\par total to 327.\par \par Nedbank exercised its right to subscribe for a 20% stake in ETI for a sum of GBP305 million. The transaction strengthens the already\par strong strategic alliance between the two banks, which was established in 2008, to provide their respective clients a seamless one bank\par experience across 39 countries and comprising more than 2,000 branches. Nedbank has appointed a Director to the Board of ETI and\par a programme of collaboration has been put in place to realise synergies and drive cross-border collaboration between the two\par organisations.\par \par Building the UK's leading retail investment business\par \par Two years ago, we made the decision to build a modern, capital-light, advice-led vertically integrated business based on our core UK\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . We wanted leading customer offerings in each layer of the wealth management value chain: advice and distribution; platform\par wraps; wealth solutions; and asset management. We took this decision due to the fundamental shift in UK retail financial services.\par \par Over the past several years, a number of factors have combined to cause a shift from traditional life assurance to a new more customer\par focused, capital light model. These include structural factors such as the introduction of significant new regulatory frameworks, for\par example Solvency II, RDR and, more recently, the liberalisation of the UK pensions regime. The introduction of quantitative easing and\par the resulting sustained period of low interest rates has been significant as the long term liability for guarantee products has been harder\par to match, leading to these products disappearing and investment risk being pushed back on to customers.\par \par These factors have had several consequences. New regulatory regimes have led to the development of capital efficient products as old\par style products are proving too capital intensive. Pricing transparency has caused margin compression, and hence the need for\par businesses to participate in more of the value chain. Following the introduction of new rules on the provision of financial advice, the High\par Street banks, historically providers of this service have largely taken the decision to exit and therefore financial adviser networks have\par become more popular - particularly given the significant changes in retirement provisions. RDR is encouraging financial advisers to\par switch from independent to restricted advice, so the need for investment providers to have their own distribution network is becoming\par increasingly important. Additionally, financial advisers are focusing on providing financial planning and pensions advice and outsourcing\par investment management to discretionary investment managers.\par \par Customer demands have also forced changes on the sector. Products need to be designed to meet specific outcomes that customer's\par desire in their retirement - retirements that are becoming increasingly long as longevity increases. Customers now expect their financial\par services provider to be digitally accessible necessitating significant investment in IT overhauls.\par \par These changes to the industry provide a compelling opportunity for businesses which have the right customer offering, which we believe\par must include advice given changing regulations and the complexity of the current landscape.\par \par In asset management, we have scaled up \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Global Investors\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OMGI) by hiring expertise in certain asset classes, with the\par consequence of FUM reaching GBP21.0 billion at the end of 2014, up from GBP12.6 billion when OMGI was created in August 2012.\par During the year we added capabilities in Asian Equities, Fixed Income Absolute Return and European Equities. We will continue to add\par capabilities selectively. Additionally, our purchase of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  provides \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  with a leading position in discretionary\par investment management with 165 investment managers directly managing customers' money through a bespoke advisory service.\par \par We have introduced a number of wealth solutions that can help our customers in the accumulation phase of their life, as well as in the\par decumulation phase. Managed portfolio services (MPS), which allow financial advisers to outsource the investment management\par function, are proving particularly popular. For example, WealthSelect, which provides financial advisers with access to the most\par comprehensive range of portfolio management solutions in the market, with a free to the client MPS, has attracted around GBP700\par million of net new money in 2014.\par \par We already have one of the leading wrap platforms with GBP30.8 billion of FUM and a wide selection of products available. Last year,\par we took the decision to transform our platform into one of the most flexible in the market, with added functionality and product offering,\par through an outsourcing agreement with IFDS. The transformation will take time, cost and effort but is critical to the success of the\par business.\par \par On 1 July 2014 we bought Intrinsic, one of the UK's largest financial adviser networks with over 3,000 advisers and the business is now\par integrated in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth model and we are well placed to capitalise on the RDR-driven trend towards restricted advisers and\par financial planners. The UK Platform, personal protection products and elements of OMGI's fund range have been added to the\par Intrinsic's product panel for its 930 restricted advisers, up from 699 at the end of 2013. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  also increases our distribution\par reach, through financial advisers, professional service firms and direct sales teams.\par \par We believe that the liberalisation of the UK pensions market will result in an increase in the demand for advice as those approaching\par retirement explore their options. When the liberalised pension's regime comes into force from 6 April 2015, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth will be\par able to offer a full suite of flexible options. Through our Collective Retirement Account (CRA) we already offer both capped and flexible\par drawdown as well as investment solutions for decumulation, and from April we will offer all CRA customers flexible access to their\par pensions savings.\par \par We are pleased with the significant progress that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth is making in its transition to a vertically integrated business and we\par are seeing evidence that the model is working: more money is flowing into the Platform from our adviser network and more money is\par flowing from the Platform into OMGI. We have set a target for the existing \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth businesses to achieve GBP270 million of\par AOP by the end of 2015, not including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and we are confident that we will achieve this.\par \par Continuing to grow and improve \f28 \uc2 \f2 OM Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par In 2010, we said that we were exploring a minority IPO of our US asset management business, with the timing determined by our\par progress against our goals of growth, improved margins and investment performance, as well as by the conditions of the equity markets.\par \par OMAM has gone through a significant transformation since that period to ensure it met our criteria for listing. We brought in a new\par management team to oversee the transformation of the business. We took the decision to focus on long-term, institutionally driven,\par active asset management to generate alpha for our clients, and we disposed of those affiliates that were loss-making or did not\par generate the returns we expect. We decided to build a global distribution capability and in 2014, this team raised USD5.5 billion in total\par assets funded in OMAM affiliates, and non-US based clients now account for 20% of FUM.\par \par On 9 October 2014, we announced the IPO of 22,000,000 OMAM shares at USD14 a share. The underwriters also exercised an\par overallotment option on 2,231,375 OMAM shares. As a consequence, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  now owns 94,555,859 shares, or 78.8%, of the\par issued share capital of OMAM. The gross proceeds for the Group from the IPO process, including the pre-IPO dividend, totalled\par GBP317 million.\par \par The purpose of the IPO was to enhance OMAM's financial and operating flexibility to deploy capital to continue to grow, develop further\par its multi-boutique asset management business and provide the Group with enhanced financial flexibility. We will remain a supportive\par shareholder in this process.\par \par Business review\par \par The following business commentary refers to the locally reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets had a very strong year with AOP up 23% to R11.0 billion. Gross sales were up 12% to R185 billion,\par although NCCF was down 14% to R21.3 billion due to a number of large institutional outflows. FUM was up 8% to R905 billion.\par \par In South Africa, gross sales were up 14% driven primarily by product innovation. In our Retail Affluent business, APE sales were 9% up\par on the previous year, as single premium sales saw growth of 29%, bolstered by strong XtraMAX sales. Regular premium sales were\par down 6% as the tough economic environment led to lower MAX savings and Greenlight sales. However non covered sales were up 16%\par due to higher unit trust sales and strong flows into Wealth. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth's growth continued with net inflows of R8.9 billion in the\par year.\par \par APE sales in Mass Foundation (MFC) were up 11% on the prior year, with a particularly strong performance in the second half, due to\par the very successful launch of the 2-IN-ONE savings product and improved adviser productivity. We launched 2-IN-ONE in August 2014\par in response to the specific need of our customers to access a portion of their savings in a way that would not attract surrender charges,\par as well as being an affordable alternative to short term loans. Since launch, sales have totalled R630 million. The MFC business has\par continued to grow, with 255,000 net new customers added in 2014 and now has more than 2.8 million customers, with an adviser force\par of 4,142.\par \par As a result of health care \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Africa\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , we have seen a significant improvement in the life expectancy of people living\par with HIV. While this is an issue that affects the whole of society, we have taken the decision to release some of the reserves we had\par previously set aside due to mortality rates, and will be using a proportion of these provisions to increase the level of cover for our\par existing MFC customers. Additionally, given the improved mortality experience in South Africa, we will now be able to offer customers\par products which are more affordable and provide better value.\par \par Gross sales in Corporate were up 46% to R36.8 billion, with profits up 7% to R1.3 billion. Corporate achieved strong recurring premium\par growth in the year up 105%, mainly due to strong Superfund and group assurance sales. Corporate has made excellent progress with\par transforming the administration business following the launch of the new Superfund umbrella.\par \par OMIG delivered modest 2% profit growth, mainly due to increased completion fees following several successful deployments in the\par Alternatives Boutiques, although offset by lower OMSFIN profits. Low-margin institutional outflows from listed asset management\par boutiques led to an outflow R4.6 billion against R5.7 billion of net inflows in the prior year.\par \par OMF grew loans by 20% over the prior year to R9.9 billion. Its collections ratio was 91.2% and together with loan growth led to credit\par loss ratios reducing to 12.4% from 14%. Sales through the OMF branch footprint now account for more than a quarter of MFC life APE\par sales.\par \par P&C in South Africa continued to show progress in its turnaround with an underwriting margin of 0.9%, against (5.6)% last year, and an\par underwriting profit of R81 million compared to an underwriting loss of R469 million following price remediation and improved claims\par management. Gross written premium of R10.8 billion, up 2%, reflects the active management of the quality of the book, albeit at a cost\par in terms of market share. The claims ratio of 69.5% is significantly better than the prior year of 76.3%. We are exploring direct insurance\par opportunities that the recent acquisition in Kenya presents. In this regard we have entered into an agreement with three industry experts\par to consider the strategic direction, innovation and centres of excellence that would be necessary to facilitate the successful\par implementation of this initiative.\par \par Profits in Rest of Africa were up 5% as we invested in distribution, IT, brand building and improved our governance infrastructure. On a\par like-for-like basis, APE sales were up 17% on the previous year as a result of increased adviser numbers in Kenya and the inclusion of\par Ghana for the first time. Non-covered sales were up 16% due to strong unit trust flows in Zimbabwe and large sales to the National\par Social Security Fund in Kenya.\par \par Asia and Latin America profits grew by 39% due to a strong performance by Colombia, improved results in China, significant growth in\par AIVA and favourable exchange rate movements.\par \par Nedbank\par \par Nedbank produced a strong set of results, with headline earnings up 14% to R9.9 billion driven by good net interest income growth and\par a lower credit loss ratio, despite strengthening central provisioning and increasing coverage levels. Net interest income (NII) grew by 8%\par due to an increase in average interest-earning banking assets. Non-interest revenue (NIR) was up 5%, with an improved second half\par performance. Impairments were down 19% and the credit loss ratio continued to improve with all clusters now within or below their\par target levels.\par \par The combined clusters have developed competitive client value propositions and strong market positioning as reflected in headline\par earnings growth of 19.3% and an increased ROE of 19.7% (2013: 18.7%) against a higher average total capital allocated at R51.4\par billion (2013: R45.5 billion).\par \par Nedbank Capital grew headline earnings by 23.3%, with this strong performance driven by good NII growth and improvements in\par impairments. Lower NIR growth reflects the high 2013 base in trading income related to renewable-energy transactions. Pre-\par provisioning operating profit growth was 12.0%. Headline earnings growth of 15.8% in Nedbank Corporate was underpinned by strong\par NII and NIR growth. The increase in NII was supported by commercial mortgage and corporate lending activities and endowment\par benefits. The growth in NIR was from core transactional income and private-equity investments. Low levels of impairments continue to\par reflect good risk management across the portfolio.\par \par Nedbank Business Banking's strong increase of 17.8% in headline earnings and improving ROE follow the normalisation of impairments\par from a large single-client default in 2013 and solid NII growth from increased product volumes and higher endowment earnings. Lower\par NIR reflects the impact of maintaining transactional fees at 2013 levels as well as the proactive reduction of transactional banking fees\par in alignment with market practices. Pre-provisioning operating profit was up 5.8%.Headline earnings in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nedbank Retail\f28 \uc2 \f2  grew 15.7% and\par benefited from an improvement in impairments in personal loans and home loans. NIR was influenced by the strategic decision to slow\par down personal loans and maintain transactional fees at 2013 levels. Consequently, pre-provisioning operating profit decreased by 4.1%.\par \par Nedbank Wealth's headline earnings growth of 15.8% was off a high 2013 base. This was largely due to record earnings growth in\par Wealth Management and continued momentum in Asset Management, partially offset by relatively slower growth from Insurance. The\par performance in Insurance resulted from lower levels of sales of traditional insurance products, including homeowner's cover and\par personal-loan-related insurance products. The Rest of Africa Division, previously included in the Centre, reported earnings of R357m\par (2013: R173m), showing strong growth, including associate income from ETI as estimated by Nedbank on a prudent basis effective from\par the fourth quarter, as ETI reports later than Nedbank. The division also reported stronger performance from all five of its regional\par subsidiaries.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth produced a good performance with profits up 5% to GBP227 million, from GBP217 million in 2013, with strong\par performance from OMGI and the UK Platform offset by the reduction in AOP from the divested European businesses and lower profits in\par our International business. Excluding the divested European businesses, profits were up 11%. Gross sales were up 11% on the prior\par year at GBP16.0 billion, with NCCF of GBP3.7 billion 61% higher than 2013 which, led to an increase in FUM to GBP82.5 billion.\par \par In the UK, NCCF at OMGI of GBP2.5 billion was significantly higher than in the prior year (2013: GBP0.7 billion) with strong\par performance across most funds. The Global Equity Absolute Return fund was the top selling fund with net flows of GBP1.4 billion, and\par the UK Alpha fund attracted more than GBP0.8 billion of net flows. Our adviser network Intrinsic contributed GBP179 million of NCCF\par from July through to December via Cirilium. OMGI's FUM at the end of the year was GBP21.0 billion, up 31%, including GBP2.0 billion\par of FUM from Cirilium. Gross flows into OMGI from the UK Platform were GBP1.8 billion in the year (2013: GBP0.8 billion), OMGI now\par manages 12% of the FUM on the Platform, up from 8.5% in 2013. OMGI's success was recognised at the 2014 Investment Week Fund\par Manager of the Year Awards where it was awarded Global Group of the Year.\par \par The UK Platform saw gross sales of GBP5.1 billion (2013: GBP4.7 billion) although NCCF was lower than the prior year at GBP2.0\par billion due to increased re-registrations and one IFA moving GBP153 million to their own discretionary fund management solution. FUM\par on the Platform now stands at GBP30.8 billion, up 13% since the start of the year, which, along with flat costs led to an operating profit\par of GBP19 million (2013: GBP13 million).\par \par Platform sales through Intrinsic restricted advisers totalled GBP178 million, with GBP68 million of NCCF. Sales are on an upward trend,\par with GBP43 million in December against an average of GBP27 million per month in the period since acquisition. This represented 10%\par of Platform sales in December, with more than 50% going into the CRA.\par \par International's profits were disappointing at GBP37 million, down 24% on the previous year, due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements\par restricting profits and other one off costs. Gross sales were GBP1.8 billion, 4% lower than the prior year, with sales in the UK and South\par Africa higher than in the previous year but with lower sales in all other regions. All regions, except the UK, contributed to positive NCCF\par of GBP0.3 billion and FUM of GBP15.6 billion.\par \par The transaction to acquire \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  completed on 25 February 2015 and we can now start progressing with the integration of the\par business into \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  performed in line with our expectations, with NCCF of GBP1.1 billion leading to FUM of\par GBP16.7 billion.\par \par We also completed the rebrand from Skandia to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth in September 2014. The initial response to the rebrand has been\par highly promising with consumer awareness of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth at 30% and at 98% amongst the financial adviser network.\par \par Institutional Asset Management\par \par OMAM\par \par OMAM had a very good year with profits up 32% to USD211 million (2013: USD160 million) due to increases in management fees and some\par performance fees. AOP margin increased to 40% before affiliate key employee distributions. NCCF was very strong at USD9.5 billion, with\par gross inflows of USD32.0 billion driven by global equities, emerging markets equities, international equities, US dividend focus equities, US\par mid cap value equities and real estate assets.\par \par Our Global Distribution initiative performed well, raising USD5.5 billion in total assets funded in OMAM affiliates, as we continue to expand\par our non-US client base, which currently account for 20% of FUM.\par \par Non-US affiliate\par \par Underperformance in 2013 and some senior personnel turnover resulted in net outflows during the year of GBP6.3 billion. FUM now\par stands at GBP32.3 billion. Investment performance in 2014 has improved meaningfully relative to 2013, with 81% of portfolios beating\par their benchmarks on an asset weighted basis compared to 26% in 2013. The longer term track records also remain strong across the\par product line for 3 year, 5 year and longer periods. The business has now completed a re-organisation to provide stable management\par and investment teams going forward and future succession. It is confident that it now has the right platform, products and performance\par going forward, although a risk remains for further outflows due to the delayed impact of the legacy issues on certain client mandates.\par \par Responsible Business\par \par We have committed a strategic priority to be recognised as a leader in responsible business in each of the markets in which we operate\par and have appointed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gail Klintworth\f28 \uc2 \f2  as Group Customer Director to run this process. We have many excellent examples of our progress\par as a responsible business, across the five pillars of being responsible to our customers, communities, employees, the environment and\par our investments, built on a strong foundation of ethical values and good governance. We are now seeking to raise our ambition in the\par two areas where we believe we can have the most significant impact: financial wellbeing and responsible investing and we will be\par working with our partners, including the Cambridge Institute for Sustainability Leadership, to drive impactful action through each of our\par business units.\par \par Black economic empowerment\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 , through OMSA and M&F, announced its Broad-Based Black Economic Empowerment (BBBEE) transaction in 2005. This\par was aligned with and implemented in collaboration with Nedbank (see announcement by Nedbank on 23 February 2015). All the\par resultant schemes had the objective of creating sustainable value and mutual benefits for the business and a broad base of diverse\par partners and beneficiaries, including strategic Black Business Partners (BBPs), clients and community interest groups affiliated with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old\par Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The schemes were also expanded to include employees at all levels within \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par The OMSA BBBEE (except for staff schemes) transaction unwinds on 1 May 2015, with the various schemes settling any remaining\par debt due to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  under the BBBEE transaction. It is envisaged that the remaining \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares in the employee schemes\par will continue to be used to attract and retain talented Black management into \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 , while the dividends received on the remaining\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares in the client and community schemes will continue to be distributed to beneficiaries. The BBPs will take delivery of\par the remaining \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares in their schemes after 1 May 2015. Discussions are ongoing between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Nedbank and the\par BBPs on areas for future collaboration.\par \par Further details will be communicated post expiry of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  BBBEE transaction.\par \par Dividend\par \par The Board has considered the position in respect of the final dividend for 2014 and is recommending the payment of a final dividend for\par 2014 of 6.25p per Ordinary Share (or its equivalent in other applicable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ). Based on this recommendation the full-year Ordinary\par dividend would be 8.7p, a 7% increase on the prior year. No scrip dividend alternative will be available in relation to this dividend.\par \par The Board reaffirms its policy of intending to pursue a progressive dividend policy consistent with our strategy, having regard to overall\par capital requirements, liquidity and profitability, and targeting a dividend cover in the range of 2.0 to 2.25 times AOP earnings in\par future. Interim dividends will routinely be set at 30% of the prior year's full ordinary dividend.\par \par Board changes\par \par We were pleased to welcome \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ingrid Johnson\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paul Hanratty\f28 \uc2 \f2  to the Board as Executive Directors. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ingrid Johnson\f28 \uc2 \f2  was appointed\par Group Finance Director on 1 July 2014, succeeding Philip Broadley who stepped down from the Board on 31 August 2014. Paul\par Hanratty was appointed Chief Operating Officer and joined the Board on 1 July 2014. We are also pleased to announce that on 1 May\par 2015 Vassi Naidoo will join our Board as a non-executive director as well as the boards of our banking subsidiaries, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nedbank Group\par Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nedbank Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 , as their prospective new Chairman. He will succeed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dr Reuel Khoza\f28 \uc2 \f2  who has now served nearly nine\par years on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Board\f28 \uc2 \f2  and will not be seeking re-election at this year's Annual General Meeting.\par \par Outlook\par \par In our main market of South Africa, economic conditions are likely to remain challenging in the short term particularly as the continuing\par power shortages are expected to constrain growth. However, a prolonged period of low oil prices will keep inflation down which is\par positive for the consumer. Our businesses in South Africa are in good shape and we are confident about their resilience in 2015, despite\par these headwinds.\par \par In the UK, we are well positioned to benefit from investment from customers looking to take advantage of the new pension withdrawal\par rules that come into effect in April. We expect demand for advice to increase as a consequence of these changes.\par \par In the US, our focus will remain on pursuing growth initiatives, including further penetration of non-US markets and through partnerships\par with scale asset management boutiques and building its business following the successful IPO.\par \par We have invested significantly in our chosen markets to grow profits over the long term while maintaining appropriate levels of capital\par and leverage. Our focus for 2015 is on integrating the acquisitions, delivering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  improvements and creating value from these\par investments.\par \par Part 2 - Financial Performance\par \par Contents                            \par News Release                                          1   \par Part 1 - 2014 Annual Review                           4   \par Part 2 - Financial Performance                       11   \par AOP analysis                                         12   \par AOP analysis by line of business                     12   \par Tax                                                  12   \par AOP analysis b