rofit growth of 120% in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Global Investors\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OMGI).\par \par Banking & Lending profits were marginally down with good local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  profit growth in Nedbank's banking and lending profits, up\par 16%, and increased lending profits in OMEM, as a result of the increased contribution by OMF, following its consolidation from the\par beginning of the fourth quarter, and the first time inclusion of Faulu. These \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  improvements were offset by the weaker average\par rand rate.\par \par Property & Casualty earnings rose strongly driven by a turnaround in underwriting profits in South Africa, which has benefited from\par targeted management pricing actions and claims initiatives, particularly in Personal which has seen an exceptional turnaround. Both\par Commercial and Corporate & Niche also saw strong underwriting improvements.\par \par Finance costs are down in 2014 following the repayment of GBP176 million of debt in the fourth quarter of 2013.\par \par Long-term investment return (LTIR) on excess assets decreased in 2014 as a result of a lower asset base following acquisition activity\par in Emerging Markets, as well as the impact of the weaker rand.\par \par Net interest payable to non-core \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was lower in 2014 as a result of the cancellation of Bermuda loan notes in the second half\par of 2013 and 2014, as well as lower prevailing interest rates.\par \par Corporate costs marginally increased with continued efficiency savings largely offsetting ongoing Solvency II preparation costs.\par \par Other net (expenses)/income decreased due to lower fair value gains on seed capital as well as brand-building costs for the Group\par through investing in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth's positioning in the UK.\par \par Tax\par \par The AOP effective tax rate (ETR) for the Group has increased slightly to 27% (2013: 26%).\par \par Because over 80% of the profits and tax before non-controlling interests arise in emerging markets and Nedbank, movements in the\par ETR in these businesses have a large impact on the group ETR. The ETR in Nedbank remained stable but the ETR in our emerging\par markets businesses increased due to a reduction in non-taxable and low taxed income and the impact of non-deductible losses in\par Africa.\par The ETR for our \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth business is generally lower than those in our emerging markets businesses given the lower\par corporate tax rate in the UK and in the International division.\par \par Interest and head office costs incurred in the UK can be offset against profits in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth UK.\par \par Looking forward, and depending on market conditions and profit mix, we expect the ETR on AOP in future periods to range between\par 25% and 28%.\par \par AOP analysis by business unit (GBPm)    2014    2013   % change   \par Core \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets(1)           617     594         4%   \par Nedbank                                  770     797       (3%)   \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth                        227     217         5%   \par Institutional Asset Management           131     111        18%   \par                                        1,745   1,719         2%   \par Net non-operating expenses             (140)   (107)        31%   \par Adjusted operating profit before tax   1,605   1,612          -   \par \par (1) Comparative has been restated to include Property & Casualty AOP of GBP4 million\par \par AOP by business unit\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets profits rose modestly in sterling terms with good local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2