                       36,243    34,370\par Other liabilities                                                    8,019     7,329\par Total equity (including hybrid debt)                                 9,545     9,037\par Total Equity and Liabilities                   142,516   140,331\par \par The Group balance sheet at 31 December 2014 included GBP3 billion of goodwill and other intangible assets, largely in Europe and the\par US, GBP35 billion of loan and advances, of which GBP34 billion were in Nedbank, and GBP88 billion of investment and securities, of\par which GBP30 billion were in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets and GBP47 billion in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth.\par \par Investment contracts were GBP69 billion, of which GBP60 billion were unit-linked and similar contracts and GBP20 billion of investment\par contracts were in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets and GBP48 billion were in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth. Long-term business policyholder\par liabilities of GBP9 billion relate to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets.\par \par The Group had GBP3 billion of borrowed funds, of which GBP2 billion relates to Nedbank funding.\par \par Return on Equity\par \par                                                            Return on\par Group and local RoE 2014 (GBPm)           Shareholder    shareholder\par                                          equity excl.   equity excl.\par                                    AOP intangibles(1) intangibles(2)     Local RoE\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets        410          1,654          25.7%       22.8%\par Nedbank                            301          1,811          17.2%         17.2%\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth                  179            928          19.5%         16.5%\par Institutional Asset Management      96            (8)          49.3%         16.5%\par Group Head Office                (118)     2,406(1,3)              -             -\par Group RoE                          868          6,791        13.3%         13.3%\par \par (1) Shareholders' equity is at 31st December 2014. Business unit figures exclude the Group share of 'Goodwill and other intangible assets' per the\par     segmental balance sheet; these have been included at the GHO level\par (2) Calculated as AOP post-tax and NCI divided by average shareholders' equity excluding 'Goodwill and other intangible assets'\par (3) Includes 'Goodwill and other intangible assets' and excludes the perpetual preferred callable securities and non-core \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par (4) Group RoE is calculated using average ordinary shareholders' equity (i.e. excluding perpetual preferred callable securities) and excludes non-core\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par The Group RoE declined by 0.3% to 13.3%. The average Group equity is flat at GBP6,545 million (2013: GBP6,525 million) as a result\par of the net retained profits for the period being greater than the fall in the sterling value of rand based net equity at the closing year end\par exchange rates compared to that at the start of the year as well as the Bermuda capital repatriation. Reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earnings were\par lower principally due to the movement in the average rand /sterling exchange rates in the period. Constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earnings grew by\par 13%.\par \par At an individual business unit level, each business performed well in relation to their expected medium and long-term RoE target\par ranges. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth and Institutional Asset Management improved their RoE's compared with the\par prior year as a result of improved earnings and stable capital bases. Nedbank maintained its RoE (excluding goodwill) at 17.2%. Note\par that, for the purpose of the calculation, goodwill and other intangibles have been excluded from the individual business returns and are\par shown in the holding company as we sought to build scale and competitive advantage in our business through acquisition. As a\par consequence the Business unit returns are higher than the Group return.\par \par Capital\par The Group's capital position is managed to ensure the subsidiary businesses are appropriately capitalised under local and Group capital\par rules, and subsidiary and Group capital is resilient to stress scenarios. The Group has an appropriate level of capital for the current\par strategic plans, including 2015 acquisitions of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  and UAP Holdings and taking into account the evolving regulatory regime.\par The Group capital position is supported by debt and hybrid instruments and the level of Group leverage is appropriate and sustainable.\par \par Business Unit regulatory solvency strength\par The Group's subsidiary businesses continue to have strong and resilient local capital. This is consistent with the Group's operating\par model and capital philosophy to ensure capital resides where the risks lie, including risks for extreme scenarios. The table below\par summarises the principal local statutory capital positions.\par \par                                                                   Capital         Capital\par Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                                   Resources    Requirements   Surplus      2014    2013\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Life Assurance Company South Africa\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OMLACSA)(1)\par (Rbn)     47.8            15.3      32.5      3.1x    3.2x           \par Mutual & Federal(2) (Rbn)                                             2.9             2.1       0.8      1.8x    1.9x   \par Nedbank(3) (Rbn)                                                     64.4            44.1      20.3     14.6%   15.7%\par UK(4) (GBPbn)                                                         0.6             0.2    0.4      2.6x    2.6x   \par Bermuda(5) (USDbn)                                                    0.4             0.3       0.1      1.3x    1.4x\par \par (1) South Africa Statutory Valuation Methods (SVM) in accordance with FSB requirement\par (2) Capital Adequacy Requirement (CAR) in accordance with FSB requirement\par (3) Basel III valuation method and including unappropriated profits and showing total Group Capital adequacy ratio\par (4) FGD basis (not required to report to the PRA separately)\par (5) Enhanced Capital Requirement as set by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bermuda \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par Group regulatory capital - Financial Groups Directive (FGD)\par \par The Group currently measures its Group solvency and regulatory capital in accordance with the EU Financial Groups Directive (FGD).\par The FGD methodology and framework differs fundamentally to the new Solvency II regime to which we will transition in 2016.\par \par The Group's regulatory capital surplus, calculated under the FGD, was GBP2.0 billion at 31 December 2014 (31 December 2013:\par GBP2.1 billion) representing a statutory cover of 163% (31 December 2013: 168%). The reduction in surplus and coverage ratio is due\par to an increase in capital requirements as a result of growth in Nedbank's risk weighted banking assets and the continued growth in the\par protection part of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets insurance book. Capital resources remained stable at GBP5.2 billion reflecting the\par redeployment of proceeds from business disposals and the IPO of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  to fund acquisitions.\par \par Group regulatory capital (FGD basis) (GBPbn)   2014   2013\par Capital resources                      5.2    5.2\par Capital requirements                            3.2    3.1\par Surplus                                         2.0    2.1\par Coverage                                       163%   168%\par \par 30% of the Group FGD resources of GBP5.2 billion comprise of qualifying debt instruments (totalling GBP1.5 billion). These provide\par additional liquidity as well as optimising the Group's Weighted Average Cost of Capital (WACC) and consists of GBP1.0 billion of debt\par instruments issued at the Group holding company and GBP0.2 billion at the Group's South African subsidiary \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Life Assurance\par Company (South Africa) Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OMLAC(SA)) and GBP0.3 billion at Nedbank.\par \par Regulatory Capital has been assessed in the context of various stress scenarios. This analysis indicates that a 1% fall in the ZAR/GBP\par exchange rate would result in a GBP11 million reduction in the surplus (2013: GBP13 million reduction), and if the ZAR/GBP weakened\par to R25, the surplus would reduce to GBP1.7 billion. The cover ratio is resilient to movements in exchange rates in a stress scenario\par because both the capital resources and the capital requirement fluctuate with changes in those same exchange rates.\par \par Since the 2014 year end, the Group has completed the acquisition of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  and announced the proposed acquisition of a\par majority stake in UAP. These two transactions will reduce Group FGD by GBP0.7 billion and reduce the statutory cover ratio by\par approximately 20 percentage points.\par \par Future Regulatory Capital assessment- Solvency II and Solvency Assessment and Management (SAM)\par \par We are actively engaged with the Group's regulators in the application process on the future Solvency II capital regime. In line with our\par regulators' timetable, we will submit our relevant applications in Q2 2015 and anticipate receiving a response in the second half of 2015\par to facilitate implementation of Solvency II in January 2016.\par \par Irrespective of the outcome of Solvency II, the capital strength relative to the risks of our underlying business will remain unchanged.\par However, the Solvency II regime will introduce a different lens through which we look at Group capital. The full Solvency II outcome is\par not yet clear and will depend on the assumptions and aggregation models used in Solvency II calculations. The Solvency II regime will\par use a more conservative 1 in 200 stress scenario in determining capital requirements and apply a more rules based determination of\par fungibility and transferability. By comparison, the FGD regime is not a risk based regime and assumes full fungibility and transferability\par of capital across geographies. Given the inherent conservatism of Solvency II compared to the FGD regime, it is likely that Solvency II\par will result in the reporting of lower levels of surplus regulatory capital and lower coverage ratios when compared with the FGD regime.\par \par The adoption of the standard formula approach may also increase conservatism. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  intends to apply the standard formula\par approach for the purposes of Solvency II because it is more relevant. The standard formula model will be applied in determining\par regulatory capital requirements for SAM (in South Africa). Furthermore, as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth in the UK moves toward being a modern\par vertically integrated retail investment business, the internal model approach is less relevant.\par \par Only when the Solvency II rules are fully determined will the Group fully understand the extent to which SAM and Solvency II will align\par and whether South Africa will be deemed to be a Solvency II-equivalent regime. The formal PRA application process will also provide\par clarity in a number of other areas including the approach to aggregating subsidiaries in the Group calculations, contract boundaries,\par cross border diversification benefits and the equivalence of non-EU regulatory regimes (other than South Africa).\par \par During 2015, we will report to our regulators under the interim arrangements and we are preparing for the full Pillar 3 reporting under\par Solvency II and SAM, which is required from 2016 onwards when the new regimes become effective. Preparing for reporting under the\par new regulations will require significant effort and investment in reporting processes for our businesses.\par \par Economic Capital\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Economic Capital (EC) framework presents management's view of the Group's capital with underpinning assumptions that\par the full future value of insurance profits emerges over time and that full diversification can be recognised between businesses. The\par Group monitors EC through regular reporting, including risk assessments and consideration of the impacts of extreme stress scenarios\par for each business.\par \par Although current FGD rules do not apply restrictions on fungibility and transferability, we expect that this may change under solvency II.\par As we finalise assumptions and methodologies underpinning the Group's Solvency II calculation, we will consider these in relation to our\par EC framework and consider whether any refinements are required as a consequence.\par \par At 31 December 2014, the Group Economic Capital surplus was GBP5.2 billion, and Economic Capital cover ratio was 226% (31\par December 2013, GBP4.8 billion 216%). As well as the strong outcome for the Group, the results demonstrate the capital strength of\par each individual business unit. This is consistent with the Group's operating model and capital philosophy and aligned with local statutory\par capital.\par \par The Group economic capital coverage ratio has strengthened compared to the position at 31 December 2013, due to the impact on EC\par surplus of an increase in available financial resources from retained profits earned in the period net of dividends paid and corporate\par activity completed during 2014.\par \par The Group's Economic Capital positions at 31 December 2014 are shown in the table below.\par \par Economic Capital (GBPbn)                       Old                                Other\par                                             Mutual                 Old        Business       Sum of\par                                           Emerging              Mutual       Units and        Group    Group   Group                                                                                                    \par                                            Markets Nedbank(3)   Wealth  adjustments(4)   businesses  2014(5)    2013\par Available Financial Resources(1)               3.9        2.0      2.1             1.2          9.2      9.2     9.0                      \par Economic Capital at Risk(2)                    1.6        1.3      0.9             1.6          5.4      4.0     4.2\par Economic Capital Surplus                       2.3        0.7      1.2           (0.4)          3.8      5.2     4.8                          \par Economic Capital cover ratio(6)               239%       154%     234%             n/a         171%     226%    216%\par \par (1) The Available Financial Resources ('AFR') is the value of assets held in excess of economic liabilities\par (2) Economic capital at risk ('ECaR') requirement is the reduction in post-tax economic available financial resources over a one-year forward-looking time\par     horizon that should only be exceeded once in 200 years (99.5% confidence level that the event will not occur). The confidence level used for Nedbank is\par     99.93% reflecting Nedbank's more prudent approach to the Basel 99.9% requirements\par (3) Nedbank results are those calculated and disclosed as part of the Internal Capital Adequacy Assessment Process (ICAAP) but reflect the proportion of\par     plc's ownership and exclude the 10% stressed-tested capital buffer\par (4) Other Business units and adjustments reflect additions for Institutional Asset Management, OM Bermuda, group specific risks (including \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     translation risk on non-GBP surplus), and adjustments for intra-group transactions\par (5) The final Group position allows for assumed diversification between diverse business units. The business unit positions allow for diversification between\par     entities within the business unit\par (6) Economic Capital cover ratio calculated using unrounded ECAR and AFR figures\par \par Since the 2014 year end, the Group has completed the acquisition of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  and announced a proposed acquisition of UAP.\par The impact of these two transactions will be to reduce Group EC surplus by GBP0.7 billion to GBP4.5 billion and coverage to 209%.\par \par Sensitivity of Economic Capital to key market movements at 31 December 2014\par \par The economic capital positions are resilient to changes in various economic factors. The table below presents the sensitivity of the\par economic surplus and cover ratio under certain standard financial stresses, which are defined by reasonably possible individual\par movements in key market parameters while keeping all other parameters constant with the effects impacting both the available financial\par resources and economic capital at risk requirement.\par \par Economic Capital position at 31 December 2014   Group EC Surplus   Group EC coverage\par Base Economic Capital Position                        5.2                226%\par Equity markets fall by 10%                            5.0                225%\par Equity markets fall by 25%                            4.8          225%\par Interest rates fall by 100 basis points               5.2                227%\par Interest rates rise by 100 basis points      