c impacts\par MCEV increased from R51.5 billion at 31 December 2013 to R55.9 billion at 31 December 2014. This is mainly due to the contribution\par from new business and investment performance, partially offset by capital transfers. Significantly positive mortality variances were\par largely offset by adverse persistency variances, development costs, and the impact of increasing policyholder cover levels on Mass\par Foundation Cluster risk products (reflecting the strong beneficial effect of better mortality experience with policyholders). Return on\par embedded value reduced to 9.9% in 2014 mainly due to lower experience variances and other operating variances, partially offset by a\par higher contribution from expected returns and positive operating assumption changes.\par \par Economic variances were positive due to favourable investment performance in South Africa, although this was dampened by poor\par market performance in Zimbabwe.\par \par Free surplus generation  \par Our businesses have generated a net free surplus of GBP653 million in 2014 (2013: GBP811 million), reflecting a conversion rate of\par 66% of AOP post-tax and NCI (2013: 81%). This reduction is largely explained by discretionary organic investment (excluding new\par business strain) in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Market businesses of GBP97 million and GBP23 million\par respectively as theseon prior year were\par generated, with NCCF of R2.3 billion up 48% relative to prior year.\par \par Retail mass market customers in South Africa responded positively to the launch of the innovative 2-in-ONE savings product. We have\par also launched a new best in market funeral product range that offers cheaper cover and enhanced value added benefits. Both of these\par were launched in the second half of the year.\par \par Corporate made good progress with transforming the administration business following the announcement in 2013 that it would cease\par offering an administration-only service to single employer retirement funds. A new Superfund umbrella as well as a low discretionary\par with profit annuity product called \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Performance Pension was launched resulting in better and more efficient customer\par administration.\par \par We successfully launched Pensions Plus (with-profit annuity) and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Savings\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plan (retail savings product) in Zimbabwe.\par We expanded our retail mass product offering in Nigeria with the launch of risk (Family Plan) and savings products.\par \par We launched a new savings product in Mexico to help expand our retail affluent distribution through our AIVA partnership.\par \par iWYZE launched a Buildings No-claim Reward product which refunds customers their building insurance premiums after six claim free year.\par \par Our digital distribution network has been expanded to include innovative mobile applications - MyPortfolio on the OM mobile application\par in SA and Namibia and a mobile banking app was launched for CABS in Zimbabwe, the first of its kind in the country. In Swaziland we\par launched a mobile insurance offering called Likhandlela, an insurance driven loyalty incentive. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Namibia\f28 \uc2 \f2  launched OMCARD,\par a Visa approved transactional card. An app to support the Old Mutual Kenya Ask Questions brand campaign and locate Old Mutual\par Kenya branches was released. In China we offer online accidental cover insurance products covering travel by air, road and rail.\par iWYZE launched a claims notification app that allows customers to initiate claims through the app.\par \par Supporting economic transformation in SA\par \par We believe we have a responsibility to support the communities in which we operate and we continue to invest significantly in\par infrastructure and local projects that will achieve sustainable change in the lives of our stakeholders in these communities. Volunteerism\par is core to our culture with well over 1,000 staff volunteering for various community projects.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Nedbank jointly pledged USD1 million towards the \f28 \uc2 \f2 African Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  - Private sector Ebola fund.\par \par In 2014 the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Education Flagship Project made an impact on teaching and learning in 134 schools to the benefit of 4,100\par educators and 105,000 learners. Linked to the National Development Plan, significant investment has been channeled into education\par through \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Foundation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Education Trust\f28 \uc2 \f2 , bursaries, and learnership and internship programs. The Education Trust\par has produced 195 graduates in various disciplines (40 in 2014).\par \par Since inception, Masisizane Fund has disbursed R152 million to small and medium enterprises (SME) with an additional R36 million\par disbursed for wholesale funding through micro finance institutions (MFI's). The number of direct jobs facilitated to date is 7,426 (1,556 in\par 2014) and a cumulative 46,000 indirect jobs have been facilitated through partnerships with MFI's.\par \par We are committed to support responsible business initiatives and our Housing Fund, Schools Fund, IDEAS Renewables Energy Fund\par and the Agri Fund have collectively invested over R1.1 billion in 2014.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual South Africa\f28 \uc2 \f2  has achieved Level 2 BBBEE status for the fourth consecutive year.\par \par Our current BEE deal with our Black Business Partners expires in May 2015. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets and Nedbank (together\par 'OMEM and Nedbank consortium'), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brimstone Investment Corporation Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  ('Brimstone'), Women's Investment Portfolio Holdings\par Limited ('WIPHOLD') and Izingwe Financial Investments (Proprietary) Limited('Izingwe') have initiated discussions on the nature of their\par future relationships, including on-going commercial relationships and potential co-investment in BEE operating businesses.\par \par We have committed in principle, to making a financial contribution and commitment of resources to regional empowerment and\par development objectives through the undertaking of the following initiatives:\par \par      -    The development of entrepreneurs and job creation in partnership with Brimstone, initially funded by an endowment of R100\par           million, of which a third would be contributed by Brimstone and the balance by the Nedbank & OMEM consortium.\par \par      -    The large-scale commercialisation of small-scale agriculture which seeks to address rural unemployment, income generation\par           and food security sustainability in partnership with WIPHOLD, initially funded by an endowment of R100 million of which a\par           third would be contributed by WIPHOLD and the balance by the Nedbank & OMEM consortium.\par \par      -    The development of township economies, including the economies of mining communities in partnership with Izingwe, initially\par           funded by an endowment of R66million contributed by Izingwe and Nedbank and a further R33million contributed by OMEM\par           towards affordable housing developments.\par \par These initiatives are aligned to the National Development Plan and are aimed at positive sustainable outcomes to be shared by a broad\par base of communities.\par \par AOP results: Strong growth in SA and Latin America\par \par Pre-tax AOP increased by 28% (23% including LTeration\par           and food security sustainability in partnership with WIPHOLD, initially funded by an endowment of R100 million of which a\par           third would be contributed by WIPHOLD and the balance by the Nedbank & OMEM consortium.\par \par      -    The development of township economies, including the economies of mining communities in