iness, partly offset by more favourable operating\par assumption changes.\par \par Total MCEV (covered business) increased by 9% on prior year due to higher operating earnings and debt raised in capital markets,\par partly offset by lower positive investment returns than in the prior year.\par \par Outlook\par \par Emerging markets continue to operate in a challenging economic and socio-political environment. The energy constraints and likely\par electricity tariff increases in South Africa remain a concern and are likely to dampen growth prospects in 2015. However, lower oil prices\par are expected to result in a sharp decline in inflation and therefore higher disposable income for our consumers, particularly those in the\par retail mass segment. GDP in South Africa is expected to rise 2.1% in 2015.\par \par In Sub-Saharan Africa, grion from new business, partly offset by more favourable operating\par assumption changes.\par \par Total MCEV (covered business) increased by 9% on prior year due to higher operating earnings and debt raised in capital markets,\par partly offset by lower positive investment returns than in the prior year.\par \par Outlook\par \par Emerging markets continue to operate in a challenging economic and socio-political environment. The energy constraints and likely\par electricity tariff increases in South Africa remain a concern and are likely to dampen growth prospects in 2015. However, lower oil prices\par are expected to result in a sharp decline in inflation and therefore higher disposable income for our consumers, particularly those in the\par retail mass segment. GDP in South Africa is expected to rise 2.1% in 2015.\par \par In Sub-Saharan owth is expected to remain at similar levels as 2014. However, the growth outlook has deteriorated in Nigeria\par as lower oil revenue could slow down the economy and force the government to cut expenditure. General elections have been\par scheduled for March 2015. In Ghana, real GDP growth will be st