f2 . The following is an edited extract:\par \par Banking and economic environment\par \par The operating environment in 2014 remained challenging for consumers, with global markets reflecting a mixed performance, and the\par local economy remained under pressure from strike action and electricity supply constraints. SA's gross domestic product (GDP) is\par forecast to grow at 1.4% for 2014, but in the absence of strike action, economic growth would have been 1% higher according to the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 South African Reserve Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SARB).\par \par Weak economic conditions, the twin deficits and Suity holders of the parent (GBPm)      315      327       (4%)   \par \par The full text of Nedbank's results for the year ended 31 December 2014, released on 23 February 2015, can be accessed on our\par website \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.oldmutual.com/media/news/viewNews.jsp?newsId=25893"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.oldmutual.com/media/news/viewNews.jsp?newsId=25893}}}\uc2 \f2 . The following is an edited extract:\par \par Banking and economic environment\par \par The operating environment in 2014 remained challenging for consumers, with global markets reflecting a mixed performance, and the\par local economy remained under pressure from strike action and electricity supply constraints. SA's gross domestic product (GDP) is\par forecast to grow at 1.4% for 2014, but in the absence of strike action, economic growth would have been 1% higher according to the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 South African Reserve Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SARB).\par \par Weak economic conditions, the twin defiA's resultant dependency on foreign capital inflows translated into rand depreciation\par and heightened inflationary pressures in the early part of the year, prompting an increase of 75 basis points in interest rates. These\par domestic factors contributed to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  reaffirming SA's sovereign risk rating at BBB negative with the outlook remaining\par stable (placing SA one notch above investment grade), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  revising its outlook on its BBB rating to negative and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par downgrading SA's debt rating to Baa2 with a stable outlook.\par \par The SA banking industry was also tested by the collapse of \f28 \uc2 \f2 African Bank Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  ('Abil'), the country's largest unsecured lender. The\par combined strength of the system ensured that Abil's resolution was successfully managed and that the bank was placed cits and SA's resultant dependency on foreign capital inflows translated into rand depreciation\par and heightened inflationary pressures in the early part of the year, prompting an increase of 75 basis points in interest rates. These\par domestic factors contributed to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  reaffirming SA's sovereign risk rating at BBB negative with the outlook remaining\par stable (placing SA one notch above investment grade), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  revising its outlook on its BBB rating to negative and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par downgrading SA's debt rating to Baa2 with a stable outlook.\par \par The SA banking industry was also tested by the collapse of \f28 \uc2 \f2 African Bank Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  ('Abil'), the country's largest unsecured lender. The\par combined strength of the system ensured that Abil's resolution was successfully managed and that the bank was placed under\par curatorship with no significant consequences for the rest of the SA financial system.\par \par Overall the credit environment remained muted, with wholesale credit demand continuing to outpace retail demand as poor employment\par prospects, high levels of indebtedness, increased interest rates and weak confidence levels weighed against consumers. Wholesale\par credit demand was supported by renewable-energy projects, corporate action and increased deal-flow from the rest of Africa. Wholesale\par activity is expected to moderate as corporates remain hesitant to make long-term investments and increase production capacity given\par the weak economic outlook.\par \par Review of results\par \par Nedbank produced strong headline earnings growth of 14.0% to R9,880 million (2013: R8,670 million) for the year ended 31 Decunder\par curatorship with no significant consequences for the rest of the SA financial system.\par \par Overall the credit environment remained muted, with wholesale credit demand continuing to outpace retail demand as poor employment\par prospects, high levels of indebtedness, increased interest rates and weak confidence levels weighed against consumers. Wholesale\par credit demand was supported by renewable-energy projects, corporate action and increased deal-flow from the rest of Africa. Wholesale\par activity is expected to moderate as corporates remain hesitant to make long-term investments and increase production capacity given\par the weak economic outlook.\par \par Review of results\par \par Nedbank produced strong headline earnings growth of 14.0% to R9,880 million (2013: R8,670 million) for the year ended 31 December\par 2014. Growth was driven by an increase in net interest income (NII), improvements in impairments and growth in non-interest revenue\par (NIR), particularly in the second half. Headline earnings included associate income from our shareholding in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ecobank Transnational\par Incorporated\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ETI) effective for the last quarter of the year.\par \par Diluted headline earnings per share (DHEPS) increased 13.0% to 2,066 cents (2013: 1,829 cents) and diluted earnings per share\par increased 12.5% to 2,049 cents (2013: 1,822 cents). Excluding associate income from ETI and the related funding costs, Nedbank's\par organic DHEPS increased 12.3%.\par \par Economic profit (EP) of R2,112 million (2013: R2,114 million) was achieved against a higher cost of equity of 13.5% (2013: 13.0%).\par Return on average ordinary shareholders' equity (ROE) increember\par 2014. Growth was driven by an increase in net interest income (NII), improvements in impairments and growth in non-interest revenue\par (NIR), particularly in the second half. Headline earnings included associate income from our shareholding in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ecobank Transnational\par Incorporated\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ETI) effective for the last quarter of the year.\par \par Diluted headline earnings per share (DHEPS) increased 13.0% to 2,066 cents (2013: 1,829 cents) and diluted earnings per share\par increased 12.5% to 2,049 cents (2013: 1,822 cents). Excluding associate income from ETI and the related funding costs, Nedbank's\par organic DHEPS increased 12.3%.\par \par Economic profit (EP) of R2,112 million (2013: R2,114 million) was achieved against a higher cost of equity of 13.5% (2013: 13.0%).\par Return on average ordinary shareholders' equity (ased to 15.8% (2013: 15.6%) and ROE excluding goodwill was 17.2%\par (2013: 17.2%), supported by a higher return on assets (ROA) of 1.27% (2013: 1.23%).\par \par Nedbank's balance sheet is well positioned. Our Basel III common-equity tier 1 (CET1) ratio of 11.6% (2013: 12.5%) after acquiring\par approximately 20% of ETI is above the mid-point of our Basel III 2019 internal target range. Funding and liquidity levels remained\par sound, with statutory liquid assets and cash reserves increasing 18.5% to R82.6 billion (2013: R69.7 billion). Nedbank met the 60%\par minimum liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) requirement on 1 January 2015. The LCR will be increased 10% annually to reach 100% on\par 1 January 2019.\par \par Net asset value per share continued to increase, growing 9.5% to 14,395 cents (2013: 13,143 cents).\par \par Cluster perfoROE) increased to 15.8% (2013: 15.6%) and ROE excluding goodwill was 17.2%\par (2013: 17.2%), supported by a higher return on assets (ROA) of 1.27% (2013: 1.23%).\par \par Nedbank's balance sheet is well positioned. Our Basel III common-equity tier 1 (CET1) ratio of 11.6% (2013: 12.5%) after acquiring\par approximately 20% of ETI is above the mid-point of our Basel III 2019 internal target range. Funding and liquidity levels remained\par sound, with statutory liquid assets and cash reserves increasing 18.5% to R82.6 billion (2013: R69.7 billion). Nedbank met the 60%\par minimum liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) requirement on 1 January 2015. The LCR will be increased 10% annually to reach 100% on\par 1 January 2019.\par \par Net asset value per share continued to increase, growing 9.5% to 14,395 cents (2013: 13,143 cents).\par \par Clurmance\par \par Our business clusters have developed competitive client value propositions and strong market positioning as reflected in headline\par earnings growth of 19.3% and an increased ROE of 19.7% (2013: 18.7%) against a higher average total capital allocated at \par R51.4 billion (2013: R45.5 billion).\par \par Nedbank Capital grew headline earnings 23.3%, with this strong performance driven by good NII growth and improvements in\par impairments. Lower NIR growth reflects the high 2013 base in trading income related to renewable-energy transactions. Pre-\par provisioning operating profit growth was 12.0%.\par \par Headline earnings growth of 15.8% in Nedbank Corporate was underpinned by strong NII and NIR growth. The increase in NII was\par supported by commercial mortgage and corporate lending activities and endowment benester performance\par \par Our business clusters have developed competitive client value propositions and strong market positioning as reflected in headline\par earnings growth of 19.3% and an increased ROE of 19.7% (2013: 18.7%) against a higher average total capital allocated at \par R51.4 billion (2013: R45.5 billion).\par \par Nedbank Capital grew headline earnings 23.3%, with this strong performance driven by good NII growth and improvements in\par impairments. Lower NIR growth reflects the high 2013 base in trading income related to renewable-energy transactions. Pre-\par provisioning operating profit growth was 12.0%.\par \par Headline earnings growth of 15.8% in Nedbank Corporate was underpinned by strong NII and NIR growth. The increase in NII was\par supported by commercial mortgage and corporate lending activities and endofits. The growth in NIR was from core\par transactional income and private-equity investments. Low levels of impairments continue to reflect good risk management across the\par portfolio.\par \par Nedbank Business Banking's strong increase of 17.8% in headline earnings and improving ROE follow the normalisation of impairments\par from a large single-client default in 2013 and solid NII growth from increased product volumes and higher endowment earnings. Lower\par NIR reflects the impact of maintaining transactional fees at 2013 levels as well as the proactive reduction of transactional banking fees\par in alignment with market practices. Pre-provisioning operating profit was up 5.8%.\par \par Headline earnings in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nedbank Retail\f28 \uc2 \f2  grew 15.7% and benefited from an improvement in impairments in personal loans and home loans.\par NIR wment benefits. The growth in NIR was from core\par transactional income and private-equity investments. Low levels of impairments continue to reflect good risk management across the\par portfolio.\par \par Nedbank Business Banking's strong increase of 17.8% in headline earnings and improving ROE follow the normalisation of impairments\par from a large single-client default in 2013 and solid NII growth from increased product volumes and higher endowment earnings. Lower\par NIR reflects the impact of maintaining transactional fees at 2013 levels as well as the proactive reduction of transactional banking fees\par in alignment with market practices. Pre-provisioning operating profit was up 5.8%.\par \par Headline earnings in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nedbank Retail\f28 \uc2 \f2  grew 15.7% and benefited from an improvement in impairments in personal loans and home loans.\par NIR was influenced by the strategic decision to slow down personal loans and maintain transactional fees at 2013 levels. Consequently,\par pre-provisioning operating profit decreased by 4.1%.\par \par Nedbank Wealth's headline earnings growth of 15.8% was off a high 2013 base. This was largely due to record earnings growth in\par Wealth Management and continued momentum in Asset Management, partially offset by relatively slower growth from Insurance. The\par performance in Insurance resulted from lower levels of sales of traditional insurance products, including homeowner's cover and\par personal-loan-related insurance products.\par \par The Rest of Africa Division, previously included in the Centre, reported earnings of R357 million (2013: R173 million), showing strong\par growth, including associate income from ETI awas influenced by the strategic decision to slow down personal loans and maintain transactional fees at 2013 levels. Consequently,\par pre-provisioning operating profit decreased by 4.1%.\par \par Nedbank Wealth's headline earnings growth of 15.8% was off a high 2013 base. This was largely due to record earnings growth in\par Wealth Management and continued momentum in Asset Management, partially offset by relatively slower growth from Insurance. The\par performance in Insurance resulted from lower levels of sales of traditional insurance products, including homeowner's cover and\par personal-loan-related insurance products.\par \par The Rest of Africa Division, previously included in the Centre, reported earnings of R357 million (2013: R173 million), showing strong\par growth, including associate income from ETI as estimated by Nedbank on a prudent basis effective from the fourth quarter, as ETI\par reports later than Nedbank. The division also reported stronger performance from all five of our regional subsidiaries.\par \par Headline earnings at the Centre includes net endowment on surplus capital and fair-value gains in the hedged portfolios, offset by\par additional portfolio impairment provisions for ongoing economic uncertainties, with the central portfolio provision increased by R150\par million to R350 million.\par \par Detailed segmental information is available in the results booklet and on Nedbank's website at nedbankgroup.co.za under the 'Financial\par information' section.\par \par Financial performance\par \par Net interest income (NII)\par \par NII grew 8.2% to R22,961 million (2013: R21,220 million), benefiting from 9.7% growth in average interest-year consumers benefitted from lower oil prices and lower inflation\par levels. The rand remained volatile and closed the year 3% lower against the sterling and 10% down against the US dollar. The JSE All\par Share Index rose 8% and the Shareholder Weighted Index was up 11.9% during the year benefitting from higher foreign inflows.\par \par Outside South Africa, economic performance was mixed. Nigeria overtook South Africa as the largest economy on the continent,\par however political unrest, especially in the North Eastern part of Nigeria, increased. Ghana experienced inflationary challenges and a\par weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  although this has stabilised during the latter part of 2014.\par \par Kenya delivered strong GDP growth of 5.5% for the year. The stronger US dollar positively impacted Zimbabwe and inflation levels\par remaearning banking assets.\par \par The net interest margin (NIM) narrowed to 3.52% (2013: 3.57%). The endowment income benefit from higher interest rates was offset\par by asset and liability margin compression. Asset margins were impacted by the change in asset mix as lower-yielding wholesale\par advances grew faster than higher-yielding retail advances, including the reduction in our personal-loans book, and by holding higher\par levels of lower-yielding high-quality liquid assets for Basel III LCR requirements. Liability margin compression arose from higher levels of\par competition for Basel III-friendly deposits.\par \par Impairments\par \par Impairments decreased 19.0% to R4,506 million (2013: R5,565 million) and the credit loss ratio (CLR) declined to 0.79% (2013: 1.06%),\par despite increased coverage levels and the strengthenin stable, however Zimbabwean equity markets were down 19.5% in the year following the elections and ongoing weak economic\par conditions (in contrast to the prior year which was up 39%). Latin American growth was impacted by weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets against\par the US dollar and falling oil prices and China GDP growth slowed to 7.3% year-on-year while India's economy increased to 7.5% year-\par on-year in the last quarter of 2014.\par \par Business developments\par \par Africa expansion\par \par We have continued to expand our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Africa through both acquisitions and organic growth by increasing the size of our sales\par forces and product development efforts. Gross sales in our East and West African businesses continue to show strong growth and now\par account for 15% of total Rest of Africa sales, contributing an additioing of central provisions to R350 million (2013: R200 million) in line with Nedbank's\par view of a protracted weak economic environment.\par \par CLRs decreased across all clusters as a result of ongoing improvements in asset quality, prudent credit granting and strong collections.\par Higher levels of post write-off recoveries of R941 million (2013: R888 million) were recorded, including personal-loan recoveries of R343\par million (2013: R276 million).\par \par Total Nedbank defaulted advances decreased by 11.2% to R15,846 million (2013: R17,848 million), driven by continued improvements\par in the residential-mortgage and personal-loans books. Total defaulted advances now constitutes 2.5% of the book (2013: 3.0%).\par \par Coverage ratios for total and specific impairments strengthened to 70.0% (2013: 64.2%) and 43.1% (2013:strengthening of central provisions to R350 million (2013: R200 million) in line with Nedbank's\par view of a protracted weak economic environment.\par \par CLRs decreased across all clusters as a result of ongoing improvements in asset quality, prudent credit granting and strong collections.\par Higher levels of post write-off recoveries of R941 million (2013: R888 million) were recorded, including personal-loan recoveries of R343\par million (2013: R276 million).\par \par Total Nedbank defaulted advances decreased by 11.2% to R15,846 million (2013: R17,848 million), driven by continued improvements\par in the residential-mortgage and personal-loans books. Total defaulted advances now constitutes 2.5% of the book (2013: 3.0%).\par \par Coverage ratios for total and specific impairments strengthened to 70.0% (2013: 64.2%) and 43 42.3%) respectively.\par Portfolio coverage improved to 0.70% (2013: 0.68%).\par \par Non-interest revenue (NIR)\par \par During the year a number of strategic actions were implemented to position Nedbank for growth into the future. These actions included\par the slowdown in growth of personal loans, reducing the pricing of our credit life product (but with increased benefits), maintaining\par transactional fees at 2013 levels, and implementing selected fee reductions in Retail Relationship Banking and Business Banking.\par \par As a result NIR increased by 4.9% to R20,312 million (2013: R19,361 million), with strong growth of 10.2% in the second half of the year.\par \par The underlying drivers of NIR growth included:\par \par      -     Commission and fee income increasing 3.9% to R14,570 million (2013: R14,023 million), driven by good tave enhanced our retail distribution capability with the recruitment of additional 226 advisers,\par bringing our total adviser force to 327.\par \par In East Africa, we have agreed to acquire a 60.7% stake in UAP Holdings (UAP), subject to various regulatory approvals. UAP has a\par strong position in East and Central Africa and a product offering that is highly complementary to our existing businesses.\par \par The transaction for the acquisition of Faulu Kenya was completed in April 2014 and the business has been fully integrated into Old\par Mutual Kenya.\par \par In our combined Rest of Africa businesses, customer numbers have reached 3 million by adding 1.1 million new customers mainly in\par Nigeria as a result of the winning of a large public sector scheme contract and the inclusion of Kenya Faulu bank customers.\par \par Producransactional-\par            banking volume growth and ongoing client acquisition, notwithstanding the effect of no fee increases and fee reductions\par            amounting to R355 million.\par      -     Insurance income growing 3.1% to R1,986 million (2013: R1,927 million), which was achieved mainly in the second half of the\par            year, through a better claims experience and continued momentum in non-traditional insurance, offset by retail volumes.\par      -     Trading income growing 3.3% to R2,648 million (2013: R2,564 million) off a high 2013 base.\par      -     Private-equity income increasing 88.0% to R423 million (2013: R225 million) following higher profits realised on listed\par            investments of R434 million (2013: R192 million).\par      -     Fair-value gains of R35 million (2013: R40 milt and business development\par \par In 2014 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets (OMEM) made good progress in the execution of a number of key business and product strategies.\par \par In South Africa, Retail Affluent enhanced its Greenlight offering by adding a new range of severe illness benefits and enhanced\par competitiveness for disability income. The Wealth proposition in SA has been expanded with the launch of integrated private client stock\par broking (PCS) which has delivered NCCF of R2.2 billion since inception and fiduciary capabilities in April 2014 to further complement\par the holistic wealth management model. The new Wealth platform (excluding PCS) has attracted R8.9 billion NCCF in the year. The\par Wealth platform is also a key support for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual International\f28 \uc2 \f2  business, gross sales of R5 billion up 56% lion), which were recognised primarily as a result of basis risk on centrally\par            hedged banking book positions and accounting mismatches in the hedged fixed-rate advances portfolios.\par \par Expenses\par \par Expense growth of 9.4% to R24,534 million (2013: R22,419 million) is reflective of continued investment in Nedbank's franchises.\par \par The main contributors were:\par \par      -     staff-related costs increasing 9.6%, comprising growth in remuneration of 8.8% and an increase in variable incentives;\par      -     computer processing costs growing 13.9% to R3,097 million, including amortisation costs of R654 million, up 12.0%.\par      -     fees and insurance costs increasing 10.7% in line with higher volumes of revenue-generating activities such as cash handling,\par            card issuing and acquiring, and client acquisition costs in Nedbank Wealth.\par \par Associate income\par \par Associate income increased to R161 million (2013: R27 million), largely driven by the estimated income from our shareholding of\par approximately 20% in ETI, effective from 1 October 2014. Nedbank's results includes our own conservative estimate of ETI's earnings,\par as the publication dates of ETI results are not aligned to Nedbank.\par \par Statement of financial position\par \par Capital\par \par Nedbank remains well capitalised, with all capital adequacy ratios well above the Basel III minimum regulatory capital requirements and\par within our Basel III internal target ranges.\par \par Our CET1 ratio of 11.6% (2013: 12.5%) is above the mid-point of our Basel III 2019 internal target range. The decrease in the ratio\par since June 2014 is largely as a result of the expected 0.9% capital impact from our investments in ETI and Banco \u218\'00\'DAnico.\par \par The tier 1 and total capital ratios reflect the progressive grandfathering of old-style instruments in accordance with Basel III transitional\par arrangements. Because of this only 80% of Nedbank's preference shares and hybrid debt qualified as tier 1 capital in 2014. In addition,\par NED 8, a R1.7 billion old-style tier 2 subordinated-debt instrument, was called in February 2014 and replaced with R2.5 billion of new-\par style Basel III-complaint tier 2 instruments.\par \par Further details on risk and capital management will be available in the 'Risk and Balance Sheet Management review' section of\par Nedbank's analyst booklet and the Pillar 3 Report to be published on the website at nedbankgroup.co.za in March 2015.\par \par Funding and liquidity\par \par Nedbank's balance sheet remains well funded, with deposits increasing 8.4% to R653.5 billion (2013: R603.0 billion) and the loan-to-\par deposit ratio strengthening to 93.8% (2013: 96.1%).\par \par Our strategy of growing retail and commercial deposits and maintaining a conservative term funding profile continues to be reflected in\par Nedbank's average long-term funding ratio for the fourth quarter of 25.4% (average fourth quarter 2013: 26.2%), which has tended to be\par above the industry average.\par \par Nedbank maintained a sound liquidity position, with contingent liquidity well in excess of prudential liquidity requirements. The statutory\par liquid assets and cash reserves, combined with the surplus liquid-asset portfolio of R37.0 billion (2013: R28.0 billion), increased 18.5%\par to R82.6 billion (2013: R69.7 billion).\par \par Liquidity coverage ratio\par \par SARB adopted the Basel Committee's LCR phase-in arrangements where, from 1 January 2015, SA banks must meet the minimum\par regulatory requirement of 60%, which increases by 10% annually to reach 100% on 1 January 2019.\par \par Nedbank has met the minimum LCR requirement of 60%, and implemented an appropriately conservative buffer. We are well positioned\par to meet the ongoing LCR requirements throughout the transitional period.\par \par Loans and advances\par \par Loans and advances grew 5.8% to R613.0 billion (2013: R579.4 billion). Excluding low-yielding trading advances, banking advances\par growth was 8.1%, underpinned by gross new payouts of R166.8 billion (2013: R158.9 billion).\par \par Banking advances growth was primarily driven by strong growth from Nedbank Capital and Nedbank Corporate, which together\par contribute 47.5% of total banking advances and 49.7% of the year-on-year growth.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nedbank Retail\f28 \uc2 \f2 's slower advances growth reflects the 16.3% decrease in personal loans, which largely offset stronger growth in Card\par and MFC (vehicle finance) of 17.1% and 12.1% respectively.\par \par Advances growth decreasing in the Rest of Africa Division resulted from growth in the regional subsidiaries of 17.2% more than offset by\par the repayment of the ETI loan of USUSD285 million.\par \par Total assets administered by Nedbank has surpassed the R1.0 trillion level for the first time, having increased 8.7% for the year\par (2013: R939.9 billion). Assets under management increased 11.4% to R212.0 billion (2013: R190.3 billion) following good market\par performance.\par \par Value created through broad-based black economic empowerment\par \par Nedbank's South African broad-based black economic empowerment ('BBBEE') transaction introduced in 2005 included over 500 000\par direct and indirect beneficiaries. The BBBEE transaction was aligned and implemented in collaboration with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 's BBBEE\par transaction. The BBBEE transaction facilitated broad-based black ownership equating to 11.5% of the then value of Nedbank's SA\par businesses. The objective was to create sustainable value for a broad base of diverse beneficiaries, including strategic black business\par partners, employees, non-executive directors, clients and community interest groups affiliated with the company.\par \par The group's strong financial performance over the ensuing nine-year period has benefitted its BBBEE stakeholders by an estimated\par R8.2bn, based on current market prices. Valuing this benefit at the time that shares became unrestricted, during the lifetime of the\par BBBEE schemes, the aggregate value created for the BBBEE stakeholders would be R5.5bn.\par \par Further information on the specific repurchase and issue relating to the BBBEE transaction is available on the Nedbank's website at\par nedbankgroup.co.za.\par \par Economic outlook\par \par The SA economy is forecast to improve modestly off a low base, although growth will be constrained by disruptions to power supply and\par weaker growth anticipated in key export markets, particularly in the Eurozone and China.\par \par Growth in GDP is currently forecast at 2.5% for 2015 as the economy recovers from the effects of strike action and exports are boosted\par by a weaker rand. Risk to this appears to be on the downside. The sharp drop in global fuel prices has improved the inflation outlook,\par and interest rates are expected to remain unchanged at current levels until late in the year. The softer interest rate outlook and lower\par borrowing costs should support consumer credit demand and limit credit defaults in 2015, notwithstanding the weak job market and still\par high consumer debt levels.\par \par Retail banking conditions are therefore likely to improve modestly, but growth in wholesale banking may moderate from current levels as\par fixed-investment plans and credit demand will be limited by the severity and extent of infrastructure constraints, rising production costs,\par soft global demand and low international commodity prices.\par \par Prospects\par \par Our guidance on financial performance for the full 2015 year is as follows:\par \par      -    Advances to grow at mid-single digits.\par      -    NIM to be below the 2014 level of 3.52%.\par      -    CLR to be at the lower end of the through-the-cycle target range of 80 to 120 basis points.\par      -    NIR (excluding fair-value adjustments) to grow above mid-single digits.\par      -    Expenses to increase above mid-single digits.\par \par Although forecast risk remains high, for the year ahead we once again expect organic growth in DHEPS to be above nominal GDP growth\par \par Nedbank data tables (Rand)\par \par Cluster performance                 Economic Profit (Rm)             Headline earnings (Rm)           RoE (%)   \par                               2014           2013   % change      2014          2013   % change     2014    2013   \par Nedbank Capital              1,198            963        24%     2,128         1,726       23%    30.9%   29.4%   \par Nedbank Corporate            1,167          1,138         3%     2,599         2,245        16%    24.5%   26.4%   \par Nedbank Business Banking       358            308        16%     1,094           929        18%    20.1%   19.4%   \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nedbank Retail\f28 \uc2 \f2                 (48)          (308)      (84%)     2,937         2,539        16%    13.3%   11.6%   \par Nedbank Wealth          660            577        14%     1,042           900        16%    36.8%   36.2%   \par Rest of Africa               (122)           (87)        40%       357           173       106%    10.1%    8.7%   \par Business clusters            3,213          2,591        24%    10,157         8,512        19%    19.7%   18.7%   \par Centre                     (1,101)          (477)       131%     (277)        158     (275%)                    \par Total                        2,112          2,114          -     9,880         8,670        14%    15.8%   15.6%   \par \par Credit loss ratio analysis (%)    2014    2013   \par Specific impairment