% to 12.5%                                  Within target range\par Economic capital                                  Internal Capital Adequacy Assessment Process (ICAAP): A debt rating (including 10% capital buffer)\par Dividend cover                                    2.07 times      1.75 to 2.25 times                  1.75 to 2.25 times\par            \par (1) Includes associate income in line with industry accounting practices\par (2) Target will be reviewed for the impact of inclusion of associate income\par \par Shareholders are advised that these forecasts are based on organic earnings and our latest macro-economic outlook and have not been\par reviewed or reported on by Nedbank's independent auditors.\par \par Highlights                                                                                     2014     2013   % change   \par AOP (pre-tax, GBPm)                                                                             227      217         5%   \par Gross sales (GBPm)                                                                           15,992   14,434        11%   \par NCCF (GBPbn)                                                        3.7      2.3        61%   \par FUM (GBPbn)                                                                                    82.5     78.5         5%   \par Pre-tax operating margin(1)                                                                     36%      36%         0%   \par IFRS profit/(loss) after tax attributable to equity holders of the parent (GBPm)               (37)       38     (197%)\par    \par (1) Pre-tax revenue operating margin is calculated as pre-tax AOP divided by net revenue \par                                \par Excluding divested businesses                                                                  2014     2013   % change   \par AOP (pre-tax, GBPm)                                                                             199      180        11%   \par NCCF (GBPbn)                                                                                    3.6      2.2        64%   \par FUM (GBPbn)                                                                                    82.5     73.9        12%   \par \par Operating environment\par \par Investment markets in the UK and Europe have been volatile during 2014 and uncertainty remains around the global economic\par recovery. We have seen increased popularity of risk-adjusted absolute return asset classes as investors look for alternative investment\par options to attain positive returns. Equity asset classes remain more attractive than bond markets in an ongoing low interest rate\par environment. Within the equity asset classes, we have experienced strong flows as well as strong returns from our non-UK funds.\par \par The regulatory environment in the UK continues to favour our business model and we are well positioned to take advantage of the new\par pension changes that were outlined in the 2014 Budget, effective from April 2015. The increased flexibility and changes in the charging\par basis of the UK platform market have resulted in higher levels of registration and re-registration of non-insurance wrapped business for\par both the industry and our own business.\par \par In our International business and European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fund values and revenues in sterling terms were reduced by the strength of\par sterling against the US dollar in the first half and against the euro over the year.\par \par Business developments\par 2014 has been a pivotal year during which we have made significant progress in transforming our business and in becoming a vertically-\par integrated wealth and asset management business. We also successfully rebranded from Skandia to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth. Our integrated\par customer proposition encompasses advice, asset management, and platform and protection products.\par \par We completed the acquisition of discretionary investment manager \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  for a consideration of up to GBP585 million on \par 25 February 2015, which gives us all the components we need to become a fully vertically-integrated wealth management business.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  performed in line with expectations. Funds under management at the end of 2014 were at GBP16.7 billion, 10% higher\par than prior year. NCCF was GBP1.1 billion (2013: GBP1.1 billion). In line with our 12%-15% RoE target, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  will enhance\par the long-term return profile of the Group, arising from the core profitability of the business together with the expense, revenue and other\par synergies.\par \par Following the acquisition of Intrinsic in July 2014, the business is now integrated into \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth and we are well placed to\par capitalise on the RDR-driven trend towards restricted adviser and financial planners. Our UK Platform, personal protection products and\par elements of the OMGI fund range have been added to Intrinsic's product panel for its 930 restricted advisers (2013: 699). Intrinsic's\par total adviser count was 3,050 at 2014 year end.\par \par In December 2014, we also acquired the remaining 50% stake in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Intrinsic Cirilium Investment Company Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ICICL) from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Henderson\par Global Investors\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Full ownership of ICICL provides a customer proposition that integrates advice, asset management, platform and\par wealth solutions and is an excellent addition to the products we manage on behalf of our customers.\par \par During the year, we made significant progress in simplifying our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to focus on a select number of core growth markets and we\par reduced our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and regulatory risk exposure in the process. We completed the sale of Skandia Poland to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vienna Insurance\par Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the sale of Skandia Liechtenstein to Aspecta Assurance International AG and the sale of the German and Austrian businesses to\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Heidelberger Leben\f28 \uc2 \f2  Group for an aggregated consideration of EUR290 million (GBP230 million). The sale of our businesses in France and\par Luxembourg to APICIL completed on 2 February 2015.\par \par Within OMGI, the addition of new teams and fund managers covering European and Asian equities, and Fixed Income Absolute Return\par has further broadened our product range and asset management capability. We are also developing our stewardship capability as part\par of our Responsible Investment practice. OMGI is now available on all major distribution platforms in the UK and its success over the\par past year was recognised at the 2014 Investment Week Fund Manager of the Year Awards, where it was awarded Global Group of the\par Year.\par \par WealthSelect was launched successfully in the UK. The majority of WealthSelect customers are investing in our Managed Portfolio\par Service (MPS) offering and 74% are choosing an active investment portfolio. We continue to review the funds available to enhance the\par proposition and offer financial advisers and customers access to the very best fund managers at a highly competitive price.\par \par In H2 2014 we launched our Silk Life Plan product in our Asian markets focussing on high net worth customers. The on-boarding\par process takes time given the complexity of the target customer base, but we see a strong pipeline of business to complete during the\par first half of 2015.\par \par In the UK we are continuing to progress our IT transformation via our outsourcing contract with IFDS, which is expected to boost product\par capability and lower our cost base from 2017 onwards. During 2014, the project spend was GBP59 million (2013: GBP20 million). The\par project is now in the build phase and we expect the spend in 2015 to be higher than 2014.\par \par AOP results\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth AOP increased by 5% to GBP227 million (2013: GBP217 million) through strong growth in our asset management\par and UK Platform businesses offset by the reduction in AOP following the divestments of some of our European businesses. On a like-\par for-like basis, AOP increased by 11%. Profits from our invest & grow business, increased by 18% to GBP98 million over the year.\par Profits excluding the divested businesses over the second half of 2014 were similar to those of the first half.\par \par OMGI delivered GBP33 million (including GBP2 million from Cirilium) of profit in 2014, more than double the prior period (2013: GBP15\par million) with the operating margin improving from 15% to 25% as FUM rose. We have seen strong net inflows into our higher margin\par Alternatives and Equities desks with some outflows from lower margin sub-advised funds and from the Global Strategic Bond Fund.\par \par UK Platform had strong profit growth to GBP19 million (2013: GBP13 million) reflecting higher FUM over 2014.\par \par Profits from our International business reduced by 24% to GBP37 million (2013: GBP49 million) with exchange movements reducing\par fund-based income, whilst the cost base is largely in sterling. The cost of implementing 'Wealth Interactive' combined with the cost of\par recapturing our financial reinsurance and other one-off costs has also reduced profit compared with 2013. These costs are not expected\par to recur in future years.\par \par Europe Open book increased profits to GBP26 million, 18% higher than prior period (2013: GBP22 million), on the back of strong net\par flows in Italy.\par \par Excluding our divested European business from both periods, our Heritage business maintained its profitability in line with prior year\par (2013: GBP78 million) despite the reduction in FUM as the book runs off.\par \par Overall, the combined operating margin for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth remained stable over the year at 36% (2013: 36%).\par \par Net client cash flow (NCCF)\par \par NCCF of GBP3.7 billion in 2014 was 61% higher than prior period (2013: GBP2.3 billion) with strong sales in OMGI, UK Platform and\par Italy. Our open book businesses produced NCCF of GBP4.8 billion in 2014, 37% higher than 2013.\par \par OMGI NCCF of GBP2.5 billion was significantly higher than prior year (2013: GBP0.7 billion) with a strong performance across most\par funds. The Global Equity Absolute Return fund was the top selling fund this year with GBP1.4 billion of net flows, demonstrating its\par popularity with investors as equity markets remain volatile. The UK Alpha fund was the next most popular investment choice in 2014\par with net flows of over GBP0.8 billion. Cirilium generated net flows of GBP179 million in H2 2014 reflecting our 50% stake between 1 July\par 2014 and 30 November 2014 and our 100% stake in December 2014 (total net inflows in H2 2014 were GBP302 million). Over GBP0.8\par billion of NCCF in 2014 into \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Global Investors\f28 \uc2 \f2  was via our UK Platform (2013: GBP0.3 billion). WealthSelect NCCF has been\par encouraging, delivering above expectations with GBP0.7 billion NCCF since its launch in February 2014.\par \par UK Platform delivered NCCF of GBP2.0 billion, 17% lower than prior year (2013: GBP2.4 billion). Sales are 9% higher than prior year at\par GBP5.1 billion but we have experienced increased outflows during 2014 as a consequence of increased re-registration activity in the\par market.\par \par International NCCF of GBP0.3 billion was 40% lower than prior year (2013: GBP0.5 billion). NCCF in all regions, except the UK\par remained positive, despite challenging regulatory environments.\par \par Within our Europe Open business, NCCF of GBP0.7 billion was 40% above prior year (2013: GBP0.5 billion) following an exceptional\par second half in Italy. Sales in Italy were 33% higher than prior year as we continue to expand our network of strategic distribution\par partners.\par \par NCCF in our Heritage businesses had a net outflow of GBP1.1 billion, flat on prior year, reflecting the continued success of the retention\par strategies in place throughout the business. Excluding the divested business, Heritage NCCF was 15% better than prior year as\par surrender rates reduced to 10% (2013: 12%), following our continued strategic focus on asset retention.\par \par The inter-company elimination of NCCF, which measures how much of OMGI's flows are sourced through other \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth\par businesses, is GBP0.7 billion, up from GBP0.6 billion in 2013 and demonstrates our growing vertical integration.\par \par Funds under management (FUM)\par \par Funds under management rose by 5% to GBP82.5 billion at the end of 2014 due to good fund performance and strong NCCF, despite\par volatile market conditions. Excluding divested \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FUM grew by 12%. UK Platform assets were GBP30.8 billion, up 13% since\par the start of the year (December 2013: GBP27.3 billion). OMGI FUM, including Cirilium, was GBP21.0 billion, up 31% on the start of the\par year (December 2013: GBP16.0 billion). OMGI FUM now represents 25% of total \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth FUM. Investment performance\par remains good, with 70% of OMGI core funds in the first quartile over a three year period and a total of 84% of funds above the median.\par \par Gross sales\par \par Gross sales increased by 11% compared to the prior period to GBP16.0 billion (2013: GBP14.4 billion) with strong performances by\par OMGI and the UK Platform.\par \par OMGI gross sales of GBP9.2 billion were 22% higher than prior year (2013: GBP7.6 billion), reflecting strong sales performance through\par UK and international third parties and improving sales penetration from UK Platform. We saw strong flows into our Alternative\par investment desk with sales over three times higher than last year and in particular our Global Equity Absolute Return fund has\par generated sales of GBP1.7 billion over 2014. Sales in the UK equity asset classes were significantly higher than prior year driven by\par strong sales of GBP1.1 billion into our UK Alpha fund. Our multi-asset Spectrum fund range delivered GBP0.6 billion of sales in the year\par and sales into WealthSelect were GBP0.8 billion in 2014.\par \par UK Platform sales of GBP5.1 billion were 9% above prior year (2013: GBP4.7 billion). ISA sales were particularly impressive, benefiting\par from the increased ISA allowances and were 21% up on prior year. Our personal pension sales were 14% higher than prior year and\par are well-positioned to benefit from the Budget changes coming into effect in 2015. New sales and transfers through the UK Platform into\par OMGI were GBP1.8 billion (2013: GBP0.8 billion). Protection sales of enhanced critical illness products measured on an APE basis\par improved by 11%.\par \par International cross-border sales of GBP1.8 billion were 4% lower than prior year (2013: GBP1.9 billion). Sales in the UK and South\par Africa were higher than prior year. The Far East saw lower sales, but improved during the second half of 2014 as sales recovered\par following improvement in regional equity markets and strengthening investor confidence at the midpoint of the year.\par \par Within our Europe Open business, sales in Italy of GBP1.2 billion were 33% ahead of prior year (2013: GBP0.9 billion). In France, sales\par of GBP0.3 billion were 5% above prior year.\par \par Heritage top-ups on existing business, excluding the divested European businesses, were down 10% from prior year to GBP0.3 billion,\par as closed books of business ran off and new flows moved to our core offerings.\par \par Outlook\par \par We have made significant progress in transforming our business in 2014. Given stable markets, we continue to target GBP270 million\par of pre-tax AOP for 2015 before earnings from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par Following our acquisition of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Quilter Cheviot\f28 \uc2 \f2  we will enhance our range of tailored investment services for the growing high net worth\par customer segment and offer further opportunities for our existing customer base.\par \par We have already seen good growth in 2014 following the addition of our UK Platform products to the Intrinsic restricted advice panel\par and we anticipate this will continue into 2015. We expect sales of our protection products through Intrinsic to gain traction and further\par grow our business. We launched our Practice Buy-Out initiative in early 2015 which will encourage retention, as well as grow our\par adviser network. Opportunities to further scale our model will be assessed as they arise.\par \par Within OMGI, the recent addition of new teams and fund managers reflects our continued desire to broaden our product range. We\par expect continued development of our asset management capabilities, in particular through a focus on our offshore distribution strategy\par and the inclusion of Cirilium.\par \par WealthSelect is demonstrating popularity and performance as an investment solution and we anticipate that momentum will continue\par into 2015.\par \par In the UK Platform we have removed our pension drawdown fee and minimum platform charge to ensure we are competitive and well-\par positioned to attract new investment from customers looking to take advantage of the new pension withdrawal rules that come into effect\par in April 2015.\par \par With Wealth Interactive now implemented in our International business, we are able to deliver flexible and user-friendly products on an\par efficient platform, whilst adapting to the challenging regulatory environments in which we operate. We expect our Silk Life Plan to boost\par sales volumes within our Asian markets. We launched the Wealth Management Plan on 1 January 2015 in Hong Kong, to comply with\par the new regulation in the region. We obtained an offshore insurance exemption in Florida in 2014 which we expect to generate sales in\par Florida to non-resident nationals (typically Latin American clients).\par \par Our earnings profile will continue to shift to our new modern source of profits and away from our heritage businesses. We believe we\par have the right business model to drive substantial growth earnings and value.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth data tables\par \par Adjusted operating profit (GBPm)   2014   2013   % change   \par Invest & Grow markets                                       \par UK Platform                          19     13        46%   \par UK Other(1)                           9      6        50%   \par International                        37     49      (24%)   \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global Investors(2)       33     15       120%   \par Total Invest & Grow                  98     83        18%   \par Manage for Value markets                                    \par Europe - open book(3)                26     22        18%   \par Heritage business(4)                103    112       (8%)   \par Total Manage for Value              129    134       (4%)   \par Total \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth             227    217         5%   \par \par (1) Includes Protection, Series 6 pensions, UK Institutional business and Intrinsic profits\par (2) \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Global Investors\f28 \uc2 \f2  includes GBP2 million Cirilium profit in 2014\par (3) Includes business written in France, Italy and Poland\par (4) Includes UK Heritage and Europe Heritage book (Germany, Austria, Switzerland and Liechtenstein)\par \par Gross sales and funds under management (GBPbn)\par \par                                                          Net flows\par         