ion, Series 6 pensions and UK Institutional business\par (3) OMGI includes net flows of GBP0.2 billion and FUM of GBP2.0 billion from the Cirilium fund in 2014. OMGI flows also include Nordic sale-related net\par     outflows of GBP0.2 billion in 2014 (2013: GBP1.0 billion)\par (4) OMGI and intra-Group eliminations include gross inflows from Heritage business of GBP0.5 billion in 2014 (2013: GBP1.2 billion)\par (5) OMGI FUM includes GBP0.1 billion of shareholder assets in 2014 (2013: GBP0.1 billion)\par (6) Includes business written in France, Italy and Poland\par (7) Includes UK Heritage and Europe closed book (Germany, Austria, Switzerland and Liechtenstein)\par (8) FUM in 2014 has been reduced by the disposal of the Polish, Austrian and German businesses\par (9) Assets and flows managed by OMGI on behalf of other \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth businesses\par \par Institutional Asset Management\par \par Institutional Asset Management consists of \f28 \uc2 \f2 OM Asset Management plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OMAM), listed on the New York Stock Exchange, and a\par Non-US affiliate. Further information is included in the Financial Disclosure Supplement.\par \par Highlights: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Old Mutual Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 (1)                                  2014   2013   % change   \par AOP (pre-tax, USDm)                                                          211    160        32%   \par Operating margin, before affiliate key employee distributions                40%    35%              \par Operating margin, after affiliate key employee distributions                 33%    30%              \par Net client cash flows (USDbn)                                                9.5   10.1       (6%)   \par Funds under management (USDbn)                                               221    199        11%   \par IFRS profit after tax attributable to equity holders of the parent (GBPm)     77     54        43%   \par \par (1) 2013 comparatives include Echo Point which was discontinued in Q4 2013\par \par Overview\par \par OMAM generated strong 2014 results in volatile equity markets, including increased profits and FUM growth. U.S. equity markets\par performed better than other markets.\par \par Continued low interest rates have supported further growth in asset allocation towards alternatives and equity income for active parts of\par institutional portfolios.\par \par The full text of OMAM's 2014 earnings announcement, released at 8.00EST on 27 February 2015, can be accessed via the OMAM\par corporate website - \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://ir.omam.com/investor-relations/news/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://ir.omam.com/investor-relations/news/}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \par Business developments\par \par On 8 October 2014, the Group announced the pricing of its minority initial public offering ("IPO") of 22,000,000 ordinary shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 OM\par Asset Management plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OMAM), the holding company for the Group's U.S. based institutional asset management business. Shares\par began trading on the New York Stock Exchange on 9 October 2014, under ticker symbol "OMAM". An additional 2,231,375 ordinary\par shares were sold under the underwriters' greenshoe option. The offering closed on 15 October 2014. As a result, the Group continues\par to own approximately 78.8% of OMAM.\par \par In October 2014, OMAM completed a USD60 million purchase of additional ownership interests in an Affiliate.\par \par AOP results and operating margin(1)\par \par OMAM continued to deliver strong profits in 2014. AOP increased by 32% to USD211 million (2013: USD160 million) largely due to increases\par in management fees and performance fees. Revenues of USD635 million for the period were 19% higher than 2013 (USD534 million),\par resulting primarily from growth in average FUM for the year of 18% and higher performance fees than the comparative period.\par Performance fees in the period of USD34 million were up 90% over 2013 (USD18 million). Approximately one-third of performance fees were\par driven by exceptional performance in a high-water mark based alternative strategy, with the remainder diversified across a range of\par products and Affiliates.\par \par AOP margin before affiliate key employee distributions increased by 5% to 40% as a result of higher management fees and\par performance fees driving margin increases at the Affiliates and scale benefits at the holding company. On a post affiliate key employee\par distributions basis, reported operating margin increased 3% to 33%.\par \par Investment performance\par \par OMAM's aggregate investment performance is reported as weighted by the revenue generated by its products. As of 31 December\par 2014, assets representing 63%, 66%, and 78% of revenue outperformed benchmarks over the one-, three- and five-year periods (31\par December 2013: 71%, 91%, and 82%, 30 September 2014: 66%, 70%, and 81%,). On an asset-weighted basis, over the one-, three-\par and five-year periods ended 31 December 2014, 48%, 52% and 64% of assets outperformed benchmarks, compared to 57%, 91% and\par 85% at 31 December 2013 and 53%, 57%, and 68% at 30 September 2014.\par \par The decline in performance from 31 December 2013 is primarily driven by certain relatively low fee value equity strategies\par underperforming their respective benchmarks.\par \par Continued strong investment performance and enhanced distribution capabilities remain key to generating future positive cash flows.\par We are pleased with our progress in enhancing distribution capacity through our Global Distribution platform.\par \par Funds under management and net client cash flows       \par                      \par Funds under management and net client cash flows (USDbn)(1)   2014     2013   \par Opening FUM                                                198.8    158.4   \par Gross inflows                                               32.0     29.4   \par Gross outflows                                            (20.8)   (18.3)   \par Total client driven net flows                               11.2     11.1   \par Hard asset disposals                                       (1.7)    (1.0)   \par Net client cash flows                                        9.5     10.1   \par Disposals                                                  (0.4)    (1.6)   \par Market and other                                            12.9     31.9   \par Closing FUM                                                220.8    198.8   \par \par (1) 2013 Reported results include Echo Point which was discontinued in Q4 2013\par \par OMAM's FUM grew 11% to USD220.8 billion (31 December 2013: USD198.8 billion) due to USD12.9 billion of market appreciation (contributing\par 6.5% growth) and USD9.5 billion of positive net client cash flows (contributing 4.8% growth). Positive net client cash flows were particularly\par strong in the fourth quarter (USD3.8 billion), contributing a USD20.0 million positive impact to annualised revenue.\par \par OMAM's FUM consists of long-term investment products diversified across U.S. equities (39.6%), global & non-U.S. equities (38.0%),\par fixed income (6.9%) and alternative, real estate & timber investments (15.5%).\par \par Gross inflows totaled USD32.0 billion (2013: USD29.4 billion), with flows driven by global equities, emerging markets equities, international\par equities, U.S. dividend focus equities, U.S. mid cap value equities, and real estate assets. Gross inflows of USD13.1 billion were from new\par client accounts.\par \par Gross outflows totaled USD22.5 billion (2013: USD19.3 billion), driven by U.S. large cap value and international equities. Gross outflows of\par USD1.7 billion relate to investment-driven hard asset disposals by Heitman, OMAM's real estate manager.\par \par The OMAM Global Distribution initiative raised USD5.5 billion in total assets funded in OMAM affiliates in 2014 as we continued to work\par with our Affiliates to expand their non-U.S. client base in key markets and jurisdictions around the world. Non-U.S. clients currently\par account for approximately 20% of FUM (31 December 2013: 19%).\par \par Outlook\par \par Our business remains focused on pursuing growth initiatives, including developing capabilities in multi-asset class, LDI and global/non-\par U.S. equities and further penetration of non-US markets through our Global Distribution initiative, as well as growth opportunities\par through partnerships with at-scale asset management boutiques with strong investment and executive talent and a vision to enhance\par and expand their business.\par \par OMAM announced its inaugural quarterly dividend of USD0.08 per share based on a 25% pay out on 2014 Economic Net Income (ENI) of\par USD1.26 per share.\par \par Non-US affiliate\par \par Underperformance in 2013 and some senior personnel turnover resulted in net outflows during the year of GBP6.3 billion. FUM now\par stands at GBP32.3 billion. Investment performance in 2014 has improved meaningfully relative to 2013, with 80% of portfolios beating\par their benchmarks on an asset weighted basis compared to 26% in 2013. The longer term track records also remain strong across the\par product line for 3 year, 5 year and longer periods. The business has now completed a re-organisation to provide stable management\par and investment teams going forward and future succession. It is confident that it now has the right platform, products and performance\par going forward, although a risk remains for further outflows due to the delayed impact of the legacy issues on certain client mandates.\par \par NON-CORE BUSINESS - BERMUDA\par \par Business Strategy and development\par \par Bermuda has maintained its strategic focus on risk reduction while managing for value. Investment returns, policyholder surrenders and\par the success of hedging strategies have contributed to a significant reduction in capital requirements and approval from the Bermuda\par regulator to release USD218 million of capital in 2014 (following a release of USD550 million in 2013). Hedging strategies are focused on\par managing the risk associated with policies with Guaranteed Minimum Accumulation Benefits (GMABs), primarily Universal Guarantee\par Options (UGOs).\par \par Policyholder surrender experience\par \par There was a decrease in surrender experience in 2014 compared to 2013. This was expected, and reflected the resumption of more\par normal levels of surrenders following the completion of policyholder top-ups in relation to UGOs that reached their fifth year anniversary.\par Fifth year anniversaries commenced in 2012 and ended in the first half of 2013. The value of surrenders in 2014 was USD316 million\par (2013: USD1,210 million). Analysis below illustrates how the UGO policy surrender experience around the fifth anniversary period\par significantly reduced the size of the UGO book and related risk exposure:\par \par                                  2014     2013     2012     2011   \par OM Bermuda policy count                                            \par Policies with UGO               8,668    9,940   17,207   27,820   \par Other policies                  2,024    2,895    4,768    7,008   \par Total                     10,692   12,835   21,975   34,828   \par Decrease (versus prior year)    (17%)    (42%)    (37%)    (14%)   \par \par Bermuda Guarantees\par \par All policies with UGOs provide policyholders with a Capital Return Guarantee of 120% of their original investment value at the 10 year\par anniversary date. In addition, there is a Highest Anniversary Value (HAV) feature whereby certain policyholders are guaranteed the\par highest policy value at any preceding anniversary date. The UGO features, including the HAV features, mature on the 10 year\par anniversaries of these policies in 2017 and 2018. Other policies under administration have less onerous guarantees which do not give\par rise to significant volatility and risk.\par \par 120% Capital Return Guarantee\par \par On an account value basis, at 31 December 2014, 78% of the policies with UGOs had a value below the 120% Capital Return\par Guarantee (31 December 2013: 91%). In May 2014, the business implemented a dynamic tail hedging strategy in respect of the UGO\par policies with the 120% Capital Return Guarantee. This approach replaced the previous 50% dynamic hedging strategy in protecting the\par company from significant market losses by hedging the risk of equity and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposures.\par \par The dynamic tail hedging strategy systematically adjusts the hedge coverage in response to market movements by progressively\par increasing (or decreasing) hedge coverage as markets fall (or rise) compared with the constant coverage ratio that the 50% dynamic\par hedging strategy previously employed.\par \par Tail hedging provides improved protection against equity and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposures and eases the liquidity strains required to\par support a hedging program under normal market conditions. Economic capital requirements are comparable under the two approaches.\par An increased volatility of earnings may be experienced under the dynamic tail hedging approach.\par \par Dynamic tail hedging coverage was 16% of exposures as at 31 December 2014, at which time the median account value to invested\par premium ratio was in the region of 106%. The tail hedging approach would increase coverage toward 100% if policy values fell below\par 72% of invested premiums.\par \par Highest Anniversary Value feature\par \par On an account value basis, at 31 December 2014, 85% of UGO policies have the HAV feature (31 December 2013: 86%).\par \par In order to hedge the equity and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risks associated with the HAV, structured "look-back" options (HAV Options) were acquired in\par Q2 2013 for the 10-year risk associated with the HAV feature of the Hong Kong policies. The HAV Options protect against markets\par rising above the 120% guarantee and subsequently falling. It was not considered economical to purchase a hedge for the non-Hong\par Kong UGO GMAB HAV exposure as this exposure represented less than 10% of the UGO GMAB book. The HAV Options are\par performing as expected with an HAV coverage ratio of 102% on the whole book as at 31 December 2014.\par \par Hedging strategies are under constant review and the approach to manage the 120% Capital Return Guarantees may be expected to\par evolve as the 10 year anniversary period approaches in 2017 and 2018.\par \par Abridged statement of IFRS financial position\par \par The statement of financial position illustrates the excess assets backing the liabilities of the Bermuda business.\par \par USDm                          2014   2013   % change   \par Assets                                               \par Cash and hedge collateral     58    103      (44%)   \par Treasuries                     -     62     (100%)   \par Group Seed investments       260    260          -   \par Inter-company loan notes     297    466      (36%)   \par Other assets                  15     32      (53%)   \par Total Assets(1)              630    923      (32%)   \par Liabilities                                          \par GMAB reserves                 82     84       (2%)   \par Other liabilities            149    222      (33%)   \par Total Liabilities(1)         231    306      (25%)   \par Total Equity                 399    617      (35%)   \par \par (1) Total assets and liabilities excludes separate account assets and liabilities of USD975 million (2013: USD1,234 million)\par \par Funding requirements can be met by drawing down the inter-company loan notes which are structured in tranches, allowing capital and\par treasury management flexibility. Cash funding is typically required to fund operating costs, "Other policies" surrenders, and hedging\par activity.\par \par The Treasury portfolio was liquidated in December 2014 to fund the capital repatriation of USD58 million approved by the Bermuda\par regulator.\par \par At 31 December 2014, liabilities relating to the 120% Capital Guarantee and HAV of USD78 million (2013: USD79 million) are included in the\par GMAB reserves with USD66 million (2013: USD66 million) attributable to the 120% Capital Guarantees and USD12 million (2013: USD13 million)\par attributable to the HAV guarantees. Other liabilities primarily relate to less onerous products with market related guarantees.\par \par Capital position and outlook\par \par The table illustrates the reduced capital and capital requirements of the business:\par \par OM Bermuda statutory capital surplus (USDm)   2014   2013   \par Capital                                      387    604   \par Capital requirements           295    418   \par Surplus                                       92    186   \par Cover (times requirements)                   1.3    1.4   \par \par Statutory capital of USD387 million compares to IFRS equity of USD399 million, adjusted for certain inadmissible assets. Statutory capital\par reduced to USD387 million as at 31 December 2014 (31 December 2013: USD604 million) following \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bermuda \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BMA)\par approval to the release of USD218 million of capital in H2 2014. Of the amount released, USD58 million was in the form of cash and USD160\par million by the cancellation of inter-company loan notes. At 31 December 2014 the BMA statutory capital requirement was USD295 million\par (2013: USD418 million).\par \par The reduction in statutory capital cover to 1.3 times at 31 December 2014 (31 December 2013:1.4 times) reflects the release of capital\par and reduction in capital requirements as the risk profile of the business has improved.\par \par The 2014 release of capital followed similar BMA approval in 2013 for the release of a total USD550 million of capital. The reduced level of\par capital and capital requirements is consistent with the reducing size of the remaining liabilities and the effectiveness of the de-risking\par actions undertaken.\par \par In view of the slower run off of the book after the fifth year policy anniversaries completed in 2013 and lower overall business levels, we\par do not anticipate further sizeable capital releases in the run-up to the 10 year policy anniversaries in 2017 and 2018, when further\par guarantee top-ups are required. The Company's economic capital is calculated to withstand a 1-in-200 stress event.\par \par IFRS results of Old Mutual Bermuda\par \par Old Mutual Bermuda was closed to new business in March 2009, subsequently it has been reported as a non-core business and as\par such its results continue to be excluded from the Group's IFRS AOP. However, interest paid by \f28 \uc2 \f2 OM plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  in relation to the inter-company\par loan from Bermuda is charged in determining the Group's AOP.\par \par IFRS post-tax profit for the period was USD1 million (2013: USD51 million profit). The higher profit for 2013 was mainly due to a favourable\par guarantee performance, attributable to improved interest rates, reduced equity volatility, and favourable surrender experience, and non-\par recurring substantial net realized gains on the liquidation of the bond portfolio.\par \par Sponsor\par Merrill Lynch South Africa (Pty) Ltd\par \par \par \par Date: 27/02/2015 09:00:00 Produced by the JSE SENS Department\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument JSEXCH0020150227eb2r00005\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
