\par\par \b \uc2 HIGHLIGHTS-South African  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  comments on rates\b0\par\par\uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2013\par 15:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JOHANNESBURG, Sept 19 (Reuters) - Below are comments from South African Reserve Bank Governor \uc2 Gill Marcus\uc2  at her latest decision on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION\par \par \uc2 The inflation forecast of the Bank has deteriorated further since the previous meeting of the MPC. The forecast for 2013 is unchanged at an average of 5.9 percent, but inflation is now expected to average 5.8 percent in 2014 compared with 5.5 percent in the previous forecast.\par \par \uc2 The current breach of the inflation target is still expected to be temporary, and the peak was possibly reached in August.\par \par \uc2 The deterioration in the forecast is mainly due to changes in assumptions related to the exchange rate of the rand and petrol prices, but given the overnight developments these assumptions will be revisited on an ongoing basis.\par \par \uc2 The risk to the inflationary outlook from the exchange rate remains elevated and dependant on its future trend. A sustained depreciation trend could pose a significant risk to the inflationary outcome.\par \par \uc2 ECONOMIC GROWTH\par \par \uc2 The domestic growth outlook is unchanged since the previous MPC meeting. The real GDP growth rate of 3.0 percent recorded in the second quarter of 2013 was driven mainly by a recovery in the manufacturing sector, following the marked contraction in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 Despite the rebound, growth was below the estimated potential of around 3.5 percent and the negative output gap widened further. The bank's forecast of GDP growth is unchanged: we still expect growth to average 2.0 percent in 2013 and 3.3 percent and 3.6 percent in the next two years, respectively.\par \par \uc2 U.S. FEDERAL RESERVE\par \par \uc2 The decision by the Fed to delay tapering surprised the markets, and emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in particular responded strongly with many seeing significant overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The expectation of an end of U.S. quantitative easing prompted a sell-off of emerging market assets, which has the risk of further undermining the weaker growth outlook that has been apparent for some time. While financial markets in these countries have reacted positively to the Fed's decision not to begin tapering at this stage, the underlying weaknesses are expected to persist.\par \par \uc2 EXCHANGE RATE\par \par \uc2 The rand is expected to remain sensitive to global sentiment as well as to the current account deficit, which widened to 6.5 percent of GDP in the second quarter of 2013.\par \par \uc2 The deficit is expected to narrow in the coming months in response to the depreciated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, this response is expected to be slow, given the import-sensitive nature of capital expenditure by the state-owned enterprises in particular.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, export revenues are hampered by weak external demand, strike activity in the mining and motor vehicles sectors in particular, and declining terms of trade.\par \par \uc2 The stepping-up of exports is critical to address the current account deficit. While the rand has appreciated significantly in the last few days, the experience of the past few months is indicative of its vulnerability to future changes in U.S. monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 STRIKES AND WAGE INCREASES\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing output is expected to be adversely affected by protracted strikes particularly in the motor vehicle sector.\par \par \uc2 There are a number of issues as to what is driving the issue of wage increases. If you look at it, the demand of wages and the actual settlement are vastly different.\par \par \uc2 We are concerned that it remains above the inflation target band even if relatively reasonably compared to where the wage demands start.\par \par \uc2 I think it is the responsibility of employers and employees to find ways in which you come to some understanding of what is do-able.\par \par \uc2 The risks to jobs are great and that's what we are emphasising.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION RISKS\par \par \uc2 Currently, a sustained breach of the inflation target is not our central forecast, but the upside risks to the inflation outlook require careful monitoring.\par \par \uc2 Should the risks to the medium-term inflation outlook deteriorate significantly, the MPC will not hesitate to take appropriated action in order to maintain the integrity of the inflation targeting framework and to anchor inflation expectations at a lower level.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Each country is taking a decision in relation to its circumstances.\par \par \uc2 If you take a Brazil for instance, if you talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Brazil has close to 400 billion dollars in reserve, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  has 45 billion.\par \par \uc2 The choices Brazil has about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in relation to its reserve is very different from the choices we've got.\par \par \uc2 The choices depend on your own circumstances and what works.\par \par \uc2 We've taken the positions we've taken, which is that a floating exchange rate is the shock absorber in these kinds of circumstances. (Reporting by Johannesburg Newsroom; editing by David Dolan)\par \par \uc2 SAFRICA-RATES/HIGHLIGHTS (HIGHLIGHTS) | ABN | M | D | SF | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130919e99j000nt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
