\par\par \b \uc2 South African Rand, Indian Rupee Lead Gains Among Emerging-Market  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Davidson   \par \uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2013\par 20:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The South African rand climbed against the dollar Friday, after the country's finance minister signaled the country could take steps to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline.\par \par \uc2 The rand gained about 1% against the dollar, which traded recently at ZAR10.26160, according to CQG data.\par \par \uc2 In an interview Friday with the Wall Street Journal, South African Finance Minister Pravin Gordhan said the rand's decline "has gone too far" and could boost inflation and damage the country's growth and job creation. Mr. Gordhan added the ministry will "have to watch the situation on a day-by-day basis and see what lessons we can learn from others and what defensive measures we can develop on our own side."\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have tumbled since Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  hinted in May that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could start winding down its stimulus program, which has boosted global liquidity and paved the way for investors to add higher-yielding emerging markets to their portfolios.\par \par \uc2 Countries with wide current-account deficits, such as South Africa, Brazil and India, have been hit hardest, as investors worry about how these countries will fund their current account gaps with fewer inflows from foreign investors. Many of these central banks have stepped in with dollar sales or policy measures to ease their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' declines, but \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  has so far resisted pressure to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the rand to continue to fall despite its gains against the dollar Friday, due to the country's wide current account deficit, slowing growth and high inflation. John Wood, director of African \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Standard Chartered in New York, expects dollar-buying to resume on any bouts of weakness against the rand.\par \par \uc2 "The situation in South Africa is still really on the negative side," he said. "It's probably buy [the] dollar on dips, that's the market sentiment at the moment."\par \par \uc2 Other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were able to recoup some of this month's losses in light trading ahead of the long holiday weekend in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee strengthened 1.6% against the dollar, which bought INR66.10, despite data released Friday that showed India's economy slowed in the second quarter. The country's gross domestic product grew by 4.4% in the three months to June, compared with the same period last year, marking its slowest growth since early 2009.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's two consecutive sessions of gains came after India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Wednesday said it would sell dollars to the country's three state-run oil refiners through a designated commercial bank, shifting the bulk of the refiners' demand for dollars away from the open market. Oil refiners are India's biggest buyers of dollars, which they use to pay for crude-oil imports.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose against some other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gaining 0.8% on the Brazilian real after the country posted its worst public-sector deficit since 2002. The deficit reached $8.97 billion in July, up from $5.12 billion the previous month. Brazil's second-quarter GDP surprised on the upside, though, expanding by 3.3% compared to the same period last year, beating analysts' expectations. The dollar bought BRL2.3808 in recent trading.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish lira also slid, as concerns about possible U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Syrian conflict remained in focus. On Friday Secretary of State John Kerry laid out the evidence against Syrian President Bashar al-Assad's regime and explained why the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  must respond to the attacks. Turkey is not only geopolitically exposed to neighboring Syria, but it is a net energy importer. If oil prices go up on Syria worries, that only puts more pressure on Turkey's already-wide current account deficit, a main reason investors have already pulled money from the country, analysts say. The lira fell 0.5% against the dollar, which bought TRY2.0465, according to CQG.\par \par \uc2 --Erin McCarthy contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lauren Davidson at lauren.davidson@dowjones.com [ 30-08-13 1847GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130830e98u000o1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
