\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Labor Strife Weighs On South Africa\b0\par\par\uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2013\par 01:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par A9\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 8/28/13) \par    By Devon Maylie \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PRETORIA -- South Africa's economy is growing at its weakest rate in four years, as labor unrest and a volatile \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  undercut the country's claim to offer a gateway to an otherwise fast-growing continent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Africa's biggest economy grew at an annualized rate of 3% in the second quarter compared with the first three months of this year, South Africa's statistics agency said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 That pace fell below economists' forecasts for a 3.3% expansion and was well under the 7% that the government has said it needs to cut into an official unemployment rate of 25.6%.\par \par \uc2 The quarterly growth puts South Africa on track to grow 2% this year -- slower than last year's 2.5% and the lowest rate since 2009, when the economy shed one million jobs. Economies across sub-Saharan Africa, meanwhile, are expected to grow an average of 5.1% this year, according to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It is going to take a notable recovery in the global economy to lift the tide, along with internal reforms," said Goolam Ballim, chief economist for Johannesburg-based Standard Bank.\par \par \uc2 A 5.8% current-account deficit and persistent turmoil at factories and mines makes South Africa's economy particularly vulnerable to declining trade and funding prospects, he said.\par \par \uc2 As a result, South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is down 23% against the U.S. dollar this year, touching a four-year low of 10.46 to the dollar on Thursday. The slide accelerated after the minutes of a Federal Reserve meeting suggested the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might soon withdraw some of the bond buying it used to prop up the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 The rand fell again on Tuesday to just shy of Thursday's low, trading as weak as 10.45 to the dollar as India's rupee and Turkey's lira also fell to record lows.\par \par \uc2 Unlike India, where officials are curbing gold imports and the amount of money that can be sent abroad, and Brazil, where officials pledged to spend $60 billion to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the end of the year, South African officials have limited scope and financial firepower to support the weakened rand. South Africa's small \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold reserves -- $47 billion compared with India's $280 billion -- aren't enough to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for long.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suffering from a wave of strikes, prompting complaints from manufacturers about the cost of doing business in the country.\par \par \uc2 South Africa has touted itself as the launchpad for business on the continent: It has the most advanced capital markets and an infrastructure-building drive is under way to better link the country with its neighbors.\par \par \uc2 Yet labor turmoil has tarnished that case. Officials under President Jacob Zuma have struggled to quell worker unrest that has simmered since August 2012, when police shot and killed 34 miners on strike outside Johannesburg.\par \par \uc2 More than 100,000 airport technicians and construction workers went on strike Monday, demanding a wage increase of more than double South Africa's 6.3% annual inflation rate.\par \par \uc2 A weeklong strike at plants operated by \uc2 Ford Motor Co\uc2 . and other car makers is costing the economy $70 million a day, according to an industry group.\par \par \uc2 "When the labor market is unpredictable it creates a lot of anxiety," said \uc2 David Schoch\uc2 , Ford's president for the Asian-Pacific region, including Africa. "It's something we have to watch closely."\par \par \uc2 At the mines that account for about half of South Africa's exports, operators are cutting jobs amid protracted labor disputes and declining production prospects. The world's largest platinum producer, \uc2 Anglo American Platinum Ltd\uc2 ., said last week that it would cut 6,900 jobs in South Africa.\par \par \uc2 If gold and platinum-mining unions insist on big wage increases for their members, mining companies warn, more cuts could follow.\par \par \uc2 "A huge double-digit increase will damage some companies," \uc2 Harmony Gold Mining Co\uc2 . Chief Executive Graham Briggs said earlier this month. "Some \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't survive that."\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 27, 2013 19:55 ET (23:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130827e98r0064y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
