\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ Blog:Emerging-Market  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Gets Ugly. Very ugly.\b0\par\par\uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2013\par 11:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's MoneyBeat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Katie Martin\par \par \uc2 Well, this isn't pretty.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been wobbling ever since the Fed started talking openly about the possibility that it might [start talking with a scary voice now] eventually reel back its bond-buying program. Terrifying stuff.\par \par \uc2 But suddenly, that drip-drip of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness has become a flood. The dollar burst above the ZAR10 line in the sand against the rand yesterday. That level was seen as a pretty punchy year-end target a few weeks ago. Friday, we hit ZAR10.2850. Gulp. The mood has changed. All those limits (stop losses) that investors have on their positive bets are getting triggered, greasing the sell-off along.\par \par \uc2 "It's just stop-loss after stop-loss after stop-loss," said Bartosz Pawlowski, head of strategy for emerging Europe at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "All the flaws are being exposed. South Africa isn't about carry but about its huge current-account deficit. Turkey isn't about its super-stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but about a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has been playing with fire."\par \par \uc2 It's not just the usual suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the rand, lira and so on, that are taking a blow. This is a bigger shift. The Peruvian sol--hardly the most rock n roll \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the most popular bet--has been walloped. As Mr. Pawlowski pointed out, the sol has just wiped out all of its gains since the start of 2012, in more or less a straight line since mid-May.\par \par \uc2 Relatively spared so far (spoiler alert: this is a short list) are the Czech koruna, which has already been held lower by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, and the Israeli shekel, which is backed by strong fundamentals, gas revenues, and so on, plus a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has already tried to manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower.\par \par \uc2 The final word goes to Bhanu Baweja, emerging market analyst at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . In a note Thursday regarding the big jump in the dollar against emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said: "Should we fade this? Anything can happen in the markets so we say this in all humility, but in our opinion, NO WAY, especially if your trading horizon is more than a day."\par \par \uc2 You don't see capital letters in research like this very often. Just saying.\par \par \uc2 Where does this end? With \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Unlikely. \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as a potential candidate, but is \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not flush with reserves and such a move would likely to little good anyway. Plus, while these moves are large, we're not seeing big gaps in rates, with the dollar going from, say, ZAR10.20 to ZAR10.80 with a big fat hole in the middle.\par \par \uc2 Emergency rate hikes across emerging markets? That's the nuclear option. But it is certainly possible that any central banks that had been vaguely planning to cut rates in the coming weeks and months might be having second thoughts now. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 31, 2013 05:27 ET (09:27 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130531e95v002mj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
