\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Emerging Economies May See Repeat Of Inflationary Cycle\b0\par\par\uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 19:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- LTRO expected to spur investors to buy EM assets\par \par \uc2 -- Some EM central banks, however, resist \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation\par \par \uc2 -- Rising oil and commodity prices bring back inflation fears\par \par \uc2 (Updates to add details throughout) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Prabha Natarajan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 )--The global economy is seeing echoes of 2009 and 2010, where easy monetary policy from advanced countries created a vicious cycle in which emerging markets attracted short-term money inflows from yield-seeking investors that in turn led to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and inflationary forces.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This risk, some say, is that this cycle--in which some see the makings of a competitive depreciation "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war"--will exacerbate the problem of imported inflation and eventually force emerging market central banks to hike interest rates. That in turn could give rise to a "hard landing" slowdown in growth for both emerging markets and the world as a whole.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loaned EUR529.30 billion to 800 European banks. This boost in liquidity is expected to send a fresh influx of buyers into emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which had already recovered more than half of their 2011 losses within the first six weeks of this year, largely in response to a similar \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  action in December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To add to their concerns, the resumption of a rapid surge in oil and commodity prices adds to the inflation challenges facing developing economies and limits their room for maneuver.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, emerging market countries' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and economies benefit from the incoming foreign capital, but many of them struggle to keep pace with the rapid pace of change in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  valuation, which make life harder for their key export industries. Back in 2009 and 2010, rapid exchange rate appreciation led policymakers to impose capital controls and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , both of which can scare off needed long-term investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many economists are sympathetic to these countries' positions--a paper released Wednesday by the traditionally hands-off \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  even advocated "temporary" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a tool for containing volatility. But it's also widely recognized that central banks run can stoke inflation if they keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  too weak, mostly because it makes imports more expensive than they would otherwise be. Eventually that can prompt a countervailing response with interest rates, which in turn could stall growth in the emerging market world. This is pretty much how the cycle played out in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's a sense of deja vu of last year, when [such easing in developed markets] triggered emerging market economies to start \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wars," said Alessio de Longis, portfolio manager of Oppenheimer \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Opportunities Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The situation turns particularly volatile when rising oil and commodity prices are thrown into the mix, as they are now. Net oil importing countries get hit with an immediate inflation threat and although energy producer countries such as Mexico, Chile, Brazil, Russia, Colombia and Malaysia benefit from higher export receipts, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation challenge is even bigger in these countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While emerging market central banks can justify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against speculative moves in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it's harder to explain countering capital flows that finance its growth. "Central banks have to strike a fine balance resisting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation but not turning away investors," de Longis said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital flows into emerging economies are an "intense challenge," especially when these countries are easing rates, said Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alexandre Tombini\f28 \uc2 \f2 , whose country has been a magnet for foreign money inflows, at a presentation on Wednesday. Already this year, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has initiated dollar purchases to weaken the real and on Thursday the government expanded the coverage of a tax on foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans to cover those of three-year periods from the previous two year limit. Tombini also reiterated his belief that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can get its benchmark Selic rate down to single digits, from its current level of 10.5%, which should make the real less attractive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar is trading at BRL1.7176, currently, and many analysts see little upside potential to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other central banks have joined the fray. Colombia has begun \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Africa\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is buying dollars again. Others with strengthening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , such as Mexico and Chile, are likely for now to stick with monetary easing rather than direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, until now, it was thought that many central banks--including the all important \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 --would be able to recover the growth lost last year by continuing to ease monetary conditions. But if inflation rears up again, not all central banks will be able to cut rates to boost domestic growth, said Guillaume Tresca, emerging market strategist at Credit Agricole .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Inflation has accelerated in Central Europe, and higher oil and commodity prices could lead only to higher prices," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tresca expects emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to face a correction in the coming weeks as the focus shifts to oil prices and return of inflation concerns. While there may be some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that do well, emerging Europe outside of Russia is expected to retreat, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --By Prabha Natarajan, Dow Jones Newswires ; 212-416-2468; prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2