\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-S.Africa's Davies says rand overvalued\b0\par\par\uc2 294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2011\par 10:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Discussions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy ongoing\par \par \uc2 * Rand has helped contain inflation\par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, background)\par \par \uc2 JOHANNESBURG, June 6 (Reuters) - South Africa's rand \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overvalued and the government is currently discussing what to do about it, the country's trade minister said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "Do we have a desirable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? We don't," Rob Davies said at a media briefing in suburban Johannesburg.\par \par \uc2 "The rand is overvalued. What can we do about it? That is a difficult question... The discussion about what are going to do about it is ongoing."\par \par \uc2 The rand <ZAR=D3> has gained about 14 percent against the dollar in the last 12 months, drawing concern from both manufacturers and unions.\par \par \uc2 A stronger rand is a negative for exporters, as it eats into profits when revenues from abroad are brought home.\par \par \uc2 The rand has helped to contain inflation, which has been inside the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 3 to 6 percent target band since February 2010.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Pravin Gordhan said last month South Africa was fortunate to have a stronger rand and the goverment would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nLDE74306D]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year spent over 15 billion rand ($2.24 billion) helping the Reserve Bank to accumulate reserves to limit rand gains.\par \par \uc2 The bank has said it does not target a level for the exchange rate but will continue to build reserves "as and when" appropriate.\par \par \uc2 South Africa last year rejected the idea of taxing capital inflows such as those implemented by Brazil and other emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturers would rather see the rand at about 8.50 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tiisetso Motsoeneng; Writing by Phumza Macanda; Editing by David Dolan)\par \par \uc2 SAFRICA-RAND/ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110609e766000hq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
