\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ 2nd UPDATE: South Africa Cautions On Joining " \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  War"\b0\par\par\uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 15:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   (Adds detail, comment) \par  \par    By Robb M. Stewart \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JOHANNESBURG (Dow Jones)--South Africa signaled Monday it won't join countries acting to weigh on their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  despite the growth-hampering impact of a stronger rand, saying that it looks to the upcoming meeting of the Group of 20 industrial and developing countries for a coordinated approach.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Pravin Gordhan cautioned Tuesday against competitive action to depress \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which he said may eventually lead to a trade war as countries establish barriers to protect their own economies and jobs.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Gill Marcus separately said that while the rand is undoubtedly overvalued, in part due to attractive interest rates in South Africa, there is no clearcut answer to what can be done.\par \par \uc2 The rand rose to highs over two-and-a-half years against the dollar, and is up more than 27% since the start of 2009. This has sparked calls from the Congress of South African Trade Unions to force the rand lower with capital controls and further cut the Reserve Bank's benchmark interest rate, currently at 6% after a series of reductions.\par \par \uc2 "There are no clearcut or easy choices. All options involve significant costs and trade-offs, and there are no guarantees that they will work," Marcus told an investor lunch in Cape Town.\par \par \uc2 Controls on the inflow of capital have a questionable track record in reducing the amount of capital coming in or on exchange rates, while direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expensive and not always effective, Marcus said.\par \par \uc2 Brazil recently increased a tax on inflows to 4% from 2%, and last week Thailand imposed a 15% withholding tax on non-residents. Marcus said the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's move to reduce purchases of euros in July has prevented the franc from rising, and recent Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounting to trillions of yen a day had only a marginal impact on the yen.\par \par \uc2 James Lord, an emerging market analyst at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  in London, said the risk of capital controls being introduced in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears limited. "They are likely to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market like they have been doing, but that's not going to make a huge difference."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has steadily increased its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, including using longer-term \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap transactions. It has no target rate for the rand.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Gordhan, meanwhile, urged the gathering G20 finance ministers in Seoul ahead of the key summit Nov. 11-12, to put the interests of the global economy ahead of national interests.\par \par \uc2 "What started as what appeared to be an individual problem in South Africa, Brazil, Korea, is now global," Gordhan said at a consumer goods conference in Johannesburg, adding that it was up to the "big players" to come up with a global strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if competitive devaluation is to be avoided.\par \par \uc2 Gordhan, who called on South Africans to work together to develop an economy that can grow at 7% a year in order to create jobs and eliminate poverty, said there is a risk of a global trade war if countries act unilaterally to lower their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Robb M. Stewart, Dow Jones Newswires; +27 11 783 7848; robb.stewart@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Clare Connaghan in London contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 19, 2010 09:22 ET (13:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101019e6aj003bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
