\par\par \b \uc2 SCENARIOS-How can South Africa rein in the rand?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Phumza Macanda    \par \uc2 906 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 12:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 JOHANNESBURG, Oct 8 (Reuters) - South African policymakers are watching with a sense of unease as the rand continues to hit new highs against the dollar, driven by a steady flow of international funds into emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The rand <ZAR=D3> has gained 27.5 percent since the beginning of 2009 and was last trading around 6.92 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Analysts see it hitting 6.50 in the near term, and that would open up the door for a rise to 6.00, a level last seen in the second quarter of 2006.\par \par \uc2 Against the euro <EURZAR=R> it is also up 27.5 percent since the beginning of 2009.\par \par \uc2 Apart from some hand-wringing and attempts to talk the rand down, the government has been unable to curb the gains, and it cannot afford to rely on hopes of coordinated action on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from global policymakers meeting from Friday in Washington.\par \par \uc2 South Africa's economy will pay a heavy price if the rand stays at current levels. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already weighed on the manufacturing and mining sectors, key exporters whose sluggishness led to slower growth in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 Following are some measures available to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though given the rand is one of the most heavily traded emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with average daily turnover of $4.2 billion -- the bulk of it offshore -- none are guaranteed to work.\par \par \uc2 RESERVES BUILD-UP - likely to continue The South African Reserve Bank (SARB) has said it will continue to accumulate reserves by selling rand to buy dollars, but has baulked at the cost of 'sterilisation' -- soaking up the extra rand in the monetary system by issuing short-term debt.\par \par \uc2 While reserves have climbed steadily, the policy has had little impact, and data released this week showed the pace of reserves accumulation in September was slower than previous months. [ID:nLDE6960GA].\par \par \uc2 This more modest participation could be viewed as a go-ahead to the market to buy more rand, said Razia Khan, Head of Africa Research at Standard Chartered.\par \par \uc2 Rand Merchant Bank\uc2  estimates the SARB would have to consistently buy over $500 million a month to have any meaningful long-term impact. Purchases have averaged $400 million a month so far this year.\par \par \uc2 That said, with reserves at $40.8 billion, South Africa still lags other emerging markets, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue on this steady path.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Pravin Gordhan said in Washington on Thursday the SARB would have to find resources for more aggressive reserves accumulation. [nN07178683]\par \par \uc2 LONGER-TERM \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  SWAPS - likely to continue\par \par \uc2 In an effort to mitigate sterilisation costs, the bank in August started using longer-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps: it buys dollars in the spot market and enters into longer-term contracts to settle them.\par \par \uc2 The bank's forward position -- which reflects its unsettled spot, forward and swap transactions -- rose to $1.1 billion in September from $504 million in August, signalling willingness to step up the fight.\par \par \uc2 In coming months this will be the gauge to watch, but even if it rises significantly, the impact on the rand is likely to be limited due to the SARB's relatively scant resources, which prevent it from taking an aggressive position.\par \par \uc2 FURTHER \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  CONTROLS RELAXATION - very likely Another option is to relax the 20 percent cap on South African pension funds investing abroad, and hope the domestic money leaving the country balances out foreign inflows.\par \par \uc2 An announcement on further relaxation is widely expected at the National Treasury's medium-term budget statement on Oct 27.\par \par \uc2 "There's about 1 trillion rand in pension funds. If foreign exposure rises to 30 percent, you could have potential outflows of 100 billion rand and that should weaken the rand, but it's difficult to quantify," said Dawie Roodt, chief economist at Efficient Group.\par \par \uc2 Relaxed exchange controls would, however, only have a short-term impact because South Africa still offers better returns than developed markets.\par \par \uc2 CAPITAL CONTROLS TAX - small probability\par \par \uc2 The idea of taxing short-term capital inflows was discussed by the ruling African National Congress at a major policy conference in September -- but it went nowhere, as South Africa needs foreign capital to fund its current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 FURTHER INTEREST RATE CUTS - Likely\par \par \uc2 The bank has cut its repo rate by 600 basis points to 6.0 percent since December 2008.\par \par \uc2 The strong rand has helped keep a lid on price pressures, and consumer price inflation is at a four-year low. Any further decline will support a rate cut, and some economist are saying there is space for additional easing of about 50 basis points.\par \par \uc2 But even if the SARB cuts to 5.5 percent, that is still much more attractive for investors than the near-zero rates on offer in developed economies.\par \par \uc2 GRIN AND BEAR IT\par \par \uc2 Africa's biggest economy is in a tight spot.\par \par \uc2 Unlike its emerging market peer Brazil, which doubled its tax on capital inflows to 4 percent last week, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  has relatively limited reserves, making \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a riskier business.\par \par \uc2 It is also acutely aware of the fine line between short-term 'hot' money and long-term foreign investment, and the risk of measures against the former inadvertently affecting the latter.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Phumza Macanda; Editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 SAFRICA-RAND/ (SCENARIOS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|G|AFN|SF|RNP|DNP|PGE    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101008e6a8000sf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
