\par\par \b \uc2 SAfrica rand rangebound vs dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 334 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2010\par 09:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Traders say 7.2490 level difficult to crack\par \par \uc2 * Futures point to flat bourse start\par \par \uc2 JOHANNESBURG, Aug 5 (Reuters) - South Africa's rand was rangebound against the dollar in early Thursday trade with little prospect of a breakout, while stock futures pointed to a flat start for the local bourse.\par \par \uc2 At 0648 GMT the rand <ZAR=D3> traded at 7.29 to the dollar, 0.28 percent off Wednesday's close at 7.2695.\par \par \uc2 "Dollar/rand seems to be treading quite a tight range after lows overnight when we made it down to 7.25," said a Johannesburg trader.\par \par \uc2 "I think 7.25/35 will be the range we're trading in ...It's been a bit of a sideways week."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's labour unions are calling for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying its current strong levels are undermining exports, but the Reserve Bank insists the exchange rate should be set by the market.\par \par \uc2 The rand briefly weakened earlier this week on news the ruling ANC is proposing a tax on capital inflows to curb its strength, but a senior party official said on Wednesday the plans would be subject to a long process of consultation.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday the JSE's Top-40 September futures contract <ALSIc1> was down 0.04 percent, suggesting a flat opening for the Johannesburg stock exchange at 0700 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Government bonds weakened a bit, pushing the yield on the 2015 <ZAR157=> up 1.5 basis points to 7.665 percent and that for the 2036 <ZAR209=> 2.5 basis points higher to 8.505 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Stella Mapenzauswa; Editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par 