\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Beware Of South African  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Risk - SocGen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2010\par 09:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0731 GMT [Dow Jones] After Poland last week \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Africa\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be next. ZAR has been flying high of late, and is the only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the EMEA region with both real and nominal effective exchange rates above the pre-crisis level, the bank says. Any break below current USD/ZAR levels around 7.20-30 (now 7.2898) would accelerate the down move to the 6.45 region, the bank says. "We reckon such a move is likely to trigger an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," it concludes. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0730 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  expects Germany's new federal note, bobl 2.25% April 2015, to come around 4-5bps cheaper in yield terms than the current five-year benchmark. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says this pricing is in line with current "grey" [preliminary] market pricing. "We believe this bond should prove to be relatively attractive for investors, given that five-year German paper has also cheapened significantly in asset swap terms in recent months," strategist Steven Mansell says. Adds that this "of course" hinges on investors continuing to believe in the relatively sound medium-term fiscal credentials of Germany, which in \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's view haven't been derailed by recent events surrounding Greece. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0729 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD may be finding support for now as general market sentiment improves and buries immediate concerns about Greece's debt problems. But, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  warns that euro zone industrial production data, which is expected to show only a 0.2% rise in February after a 1.7% increase in January, will weigh on the pair today. The pair is now at 1.3636. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD lower, tracking USD weakness vs Asian units after \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore tightens \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy first time in 2 years, says senior trader at U.S. bank. Tips pair in 7.7585 -7.7605 band near-term. "Both local and foreign banks are eager to sell the U.S. dollar. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to keep their strength as today's MAS move shows its willingness to allow the Singapore dollar to rise," trader says. Pair last at 7.7597 vs 7.7603 yesterday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] So does Singapore's surprise revaluation shed any light on a Chinese move? Although President Hu continues to tell his Washington hosts that a yuan revaluation is an internal affair, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says that the Singapore "decision adds to signs that China will also soon move to revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end flat before 1Q GDP data tomorrow; Dow Jones poll tips GDP +11.5%, much faster than Beijing's target of 8% growth for 2010. "Singapore's strong first quarter data spurred expectations China's growth might be too fast and that Beijing will have to tighten its monetary policy further to prevent overheating," says Shanghai-based analyst with local insurer; but MOF's 20-year bond sale suggests solid demand for low-risk fixed-income products--MOF sells CNY28 billion bonds at 3.96%, near lower end of market expectations of 3.95%-4.00%. Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 124.28. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Nomura International\uc2  expects the prospects of Germany's EUR7Bln supply of a new federal note, bobl 2.25% April 2015, to be balanced by the depth of safe-haven bidding versus current low yield levels. Separately, ING strategist Wilson Chin says that demand for German bonds is likely to remain firm in the current fragile euro-zone spread environment, and despite the generally low short-end rates. "We expect the takedown of the bond to be smooth," Chin says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones]--GBP/CHF has been consolidating after a recent rally and a break below 1.6315 has changed the trend back to neutral, Sarasin says. Expects the cross to move in 1.6120-1.6515 range. Stands at 1.6216. (martin.gelnar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0721 GMT [Dow Jones] The GBP may be still well bid given Tuesday's sharp rise in exports and the general improvement in risk sentiment. But, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 : "We maintain our view that further gains in cable above 1.5400 could prove difficult to sustain." The GBP is now at $1.5401 after making it as high as $1.5437. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0718 GMT [Dow Jones] Strength in Singapore shares may enable STI to close above 3000 for first time since June 2008. STI +1.7% at 3020.68 vs 3003.70 midday; near-term resistance at 3057, lower end of technical gap formed June 9, 2008. Gains not confined to just blue chips, with all FTSE ST sub-indexes positive, market breadth at three gainers for each decliner. "The better (Singapore) GDP numbers released this morning are certainly helping. The banks for instance have taken off. Other cyclicals, too, like NOL," says dealer at foreign brokerage. Market volume encouraging, with good mix of small, large caps dominating trade. DBS (D05.SG) +4.9% at S$15.54, OCBC (O39.SG) +2.5% at S$9.17, UOB (U11.SG) +4.1% at S$20.76, NOL (N03.SG) +7.5% at S$2.29. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0718 GMT [Dow Jones] Calls by Japan's ruling DPJ Party for a USD up at Y120 may appear a bit excessive but the party may yet get much of what it wants, says Nicholas Hastings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus. Pressure on the Bank of Japan to bend to the government's will and ease monetary policy will only intensify ahead of the Upper House elections in July at the same time that normalization of policy in the U.S. becomes even more likely. In other words, yield differentials between the U.S. and Japan will only widen and, in the near term at least, this could push the USD back up to its 2010 high, just under Y95.00. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0713 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian sovereign bonds may consolidate, CDS spread may widen, once initial bounce from latest good news on Greece passes, says \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 . "Global growth story is gaining traction and thus there is reason to think that prices could surpass their previous peaks (in October). Nevertheless, given the good run we have recently...the market may choose to consolidate." Recommends snapping up existing Indonesian 2020 government bonds, on improving global risk sentiment, liquidity. Keeps Sell on Vietnam 2020 government bonds, due to country's high inflation, low \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Remains Buy on 2 pair-trades: Buy Malaysia/Sell Korea 5-year CDS, Buy on Sell Korea/Buy China 5-year CDS, expects spreads in both trades to continue tightening. (aries.poon@dowjones.com) [ 04-14-10 0331ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100414e64e00051\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
