s, with the key focus on leadership behaviours and improving compliance \par to operating standards at the shop floor.                                       \par Prior to the launch on 8 November 200 7, eight employees lost their lives at    \par the South African \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the quarter, with one fatality at Obuasi in \par Ghana. Following the launch, there were no fatalities for the remaining 53 days \par of the quarter and year. The South African \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reduced their fatal       \par injury rate by 17% year -on-year, while there was an increase in the fatality   \par rate at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outside of South Africa.                                     \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti remains committed to a continuing focus on raising safety     \par standards , and there is a commitment to use OHSAS 18001 and OHSAS 18002 as the \par templates for the safety management framework. At the end of the year, half of  \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were certified OHSAS 18001 compliant, with the balance on        \par schedule for certification during 2008.                                         \par Gold production for the quarter was 5% lower at 1.37Moz, mainly as a result of  \par the safety \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Africa\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  difficulties at Geita. \par Total cash costs at $404/oz, was 13% higher than the previous quarter, which    \par was impacted by lower production, appreciation of local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and the     \par purchase of uranium to meet contractual obligations.                            \par Adjusted headline earnings for the quarter were $18m, compared with $81m in the \par third quarter. Adjusted headline earnings were adversely distorted by annual    \par accounting adjustments which totalled $64m and included adjustments on          \par rehabilitation, inventory, and current and deferred tax provisions. This was    \par further exacerbated by stronger local operating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , higher exploration  \par expenditure and lower production, specifically in South Africa, Ghana and       \par Tanzania.                                                                       \par In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Africa\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , gold production dropped 9% to 17,503kg, following safety       \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , stoppages and the one-day NUM strike. Total cash costs increased \par 14% to R87,949/kg on the back of lower production and adverse by-products       \par contribution, following the decision to take advantage of weaker uranium prices \par in October 2007 when 300,000 pounds of uranium were purchased. Excluding the    \par purchase of additional uranium, total cash costs increased 4% quarter-on-       \par quarter.                                                                        \par The other African assets had a mixed quarter, with significant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       \par improvements from Siguiri and Sadiola, which posted increased gold production   \par of 36% and 14% respectively. Morila and Obuasi were on par with the previous    \par quarter. Iduapriem was affected by power outages, with gold production          \par declining 13%, while Yatela and Navachab declined 27% and 5% respectively.      \par Geita was adversely affected by discharge pump failures and the treatment of    \par hard ore, resulting in gold production being 47% lower.                         \par The international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remained steady during the quarter, with Cerro     \par Vanguardia and  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti Brasil Mineracao improving their gold          \par production by 2% and 5% respectively, while Sunrise Dam was marginally lower,   \par as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to mine the higher grade area. Gold production at    \par CC&V improved 48% quarter- on-quarter, as delays from improved leach pad        \par stacking depths were rectified. Consequently, total cash costs at CC&V reduced  \par by 10% and adjusted gross profit improved 87% to $28m for the quarter.          \par During the quarter, following a review process, a regional operating structure  \par was established with Robbie Lazare (Africa), Ron Largent (Americas) and Graham  \par Ehm (Australasia) appointed as Executive Vice Presidents accountable for their  \par respective operating regions, reporting directly to the CEO. This restructuring \par is designed to further strengthen the focus on delivering improved \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   \par performance and safety improvement.                                             \par Two teams have also been established in the Africa region, with Johan Viljoen   \par appointed Regional Head for Southern Africa, and Christian Rampa Luhembwe       \par appointed Regional Head for West Africa. Following the disappointing recent     \par performance from Geita, Richard Le Seur has been appointed as the new Managing  \par Director at Geita, reporting directly to Robbie Lazare.                         \par YEAR                                                                            \par The company's total Mineral Resource before depletion increased by 34.1Moz for  \par the year. After depletion, this represents an increase of 26. 0Moz, from        \par 181.6Moz in 2006 to 207.6Moz in 2007, of which 6.95Moz (attributable) were      \par delineated by  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti's greenfields exploration teams for the year,   \par at three key prospects, namely Tropicana (Western Australia), Mongbwalu (DRC)   \par and Gramalote (Colombia). Significant other additions include 17.1Moz at        \par Mponeng and 4.7Moz at CC&V, both due to improved economics and revised          \par methodologies.                                                                  \par In 2007,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti recorded an increase in total ore reserves before    \par depletion of 13.0Moz. After depletion, this represents a 9% increase            \par year-on-year, from 66.9Moz in 2006 to 73.1Moz in 2007. Significant additions    \par included 3.8Moz at Moab Khotsong, due to the inclusion of Project Zaaiplaats, a \par deepening of Moab Khotsong to access deeper Vaal Reef blocks to the South West  \par of the current mine, and 3.4Moz at Mponeng, due to the inclusion of the Carbon  \par Leader Reef project below 120 Level.                                            \par Production for 2007 declined by 3% or 158,000oz against the previous year, with \par Great Noligwa and TauTona showing production declines of 132,000oz and          \par 65,000oz respectively, affected by mining redesign following safety concerns at \par TauTona and by lower grades at Great Noligwa, as mining moves into the lower    \par grade SV3 area. Both Sunrise Dam in Australia and Siguiri in Guinea achieved    \par record production levels of 600,000oz and 280,000oz (attributable),             \par respectively, for the year.                                                     \par Total cash costs increased by 16% to $357/oz, due to lower production, stronger \par local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  , higher by -product losses (uranium purchases), higher        \par royalty payments (higher gold price), increased maintenance activities and      \par inflationary pressure. Combined with a higher spend on exploration activities   \par year- on-year to the value of $59m, adjusted headline earnings reduced from     \par $411m in 2006 to $278m.                                                         \par A dividend of 53 South African cents (7 US cents) per share was declared for    \par the six months ended 31 December 200 7. This represents a similar dividend      \par payout level to adjusted headline earnings, as per the interim year             \par declaration, resulting in a total dividend for the year of 143 South African    \par cents (20 US cents) per share.                                                  \par On 1 4 January 2008,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti agreed to acquire 100% of  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Golden Cycle   \par Gold Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (GCGC), for an aggregate consideration of approximately $149m. \par GCGC, which is listed and trades on the NYSE Arca Exchange, is a Colorado       \par -based holding company with its primary investment being its joint venture      \par interest in CC&V and which is majority owned by  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti. The          \par successful completion of the acquisition, will allow  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti to       \par consolidate 100% interest in CC&V. Under the terms of the CC&V joint venture    \par agreement,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti was entitled to 100% of the net proceeds from the  \par CC&V mine until GCGC has repaid its initial loans.                              \par On 24 January 2008,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti entered into agreements to sell its       \par royalty interests in El Chanate (Sonaro, Mexico) and Marigold (Nevada, USA) to  \par Royal Gold for $13.75m. The transaction is subject to due diligence and is      \par expected to be completed by the end of the first quarter.                       \par On 25 January 2008, the South African national power supplier, Eskom,           \par communicated that it could not guarantee power supply to the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \par Precautionary steps were immediately taken for the safety of all employees,     \par with no employees transported underground to carry-out mining activities,       \par together with the cessation of milling activities. Following extensive          \par discussions with Eskom and government, a power supply of 90% has been offered,  \par although at the time of writing, this was still to be attained, which means     \par that first quarter 2008 production from South African \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been       \par severely disrupted. The company is still reviewing a scenario in which only 90% \par of power is available to its South African \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .                          \par Equally important is Eskom's ability to maintain a continuous power supply, at  \par a 90% level, given that since 25 January 2008, the company has experienced      \par daily fluctuations in available power which in turn has further disrupted the   \par attempt to return to normal production levels and milling rates.                \par Since 2004, Eskom and  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti have been working to improve energy     \par efficiencies and reduce consumption, with both organisations committing funds   \par and resources to the programme. These combined efforts have achieved a 17%      \par improvement in energy efficiencies by the Company during this period.           \par Subject to the power stability and availability at the 90% level, the           \par production for the 2008 year is expected to be within the range of 4.8Moz to    \par 5.0Moz. Total cash costs are anticipated to be between $425/oz and $435/oz ,    \par based on the following exchange rate assumptions: R7. 35/$, A$/$0.88, BRL1.81/$ \par and Argentinean peso 3.10/$. Capital expenditure for the year is estimated to   \par be $1,259m, and will be managed in line with profitability and cash flow.       \par Production for the first quarter of 2008, based on 90% stabilising power supply \par and associated operating recovery , is estimated to be 1.10Moz at an average    \par total cash cost of $46 7/oz , as suming the following exchange rates: R 7.35/$  \par , A$/$0. 89, BRL1.8 1/$ and Argentinean peso 3. 10/$. Capital expenditure       \par is estimated at $328m.                                                          \par The table below provides guidance for the year in respect of forecast ounces,   \par total cash costs and capital expenditure, taking into consideration the impact  \par of a 90% power supply in South Africa, as well as the current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2        \par constraints at Geita.                                                           \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  forecast for 2008                                                   \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                        Forecast      Expected     Forecast Capital     \par                              Production     Cash Cost          Expenditure      \par                            Ounces (000)       US$/oz*               US$m**      \par South Africa               1,800 - 1,900     395 - 415                  383     \par Argentina                      200 - 205     310 - 320                   22     \par Australia                      400 - 420     595 - 605                  411     \par Brazil                         400 - 415     290 - 300                  113     \par Ghana                          580 - 620     420 - 430                  172     \par Guinea                   