gth in the fourth quarter, with dollar  \par prices reaching a high of $845/oz in early November, on the back of US credit   \par concerns and fears that the US economy may stagnate or enter a recession. The   \par first two months of the quarter were also characterised by highly volatile US   \par dollar gold prices , with gold trading in a range of $728/oz to $841/oz.        \par The average US dollar gold price for the quarter was $78 8/oz, 16% higher than  \par the previous quarter's average price of $680/oz. The rand gold price saw        \par record highs of some R187,000/kg and averaged R171,334/kg for the quarter, some \par 10% higher than the previous quarter's average of R15 5,005/kg.                 \par JEWELLERY DEMAND                                                                \par Having performed well in the first half of the year, jewellery demand suffered  \par from price volatility exhibited in the fourth quarter, particularly in          \par traditional markets such as the Middle East and India.                          \par Gold jewellery demand in the Gulf countries was particularly affected by this   \par period of price volatility, as local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are linked to the dollar and so \par the full effect of US dollar gold price volatility was felt by local consumers  \par . This came at a time of inflationary concerns and escalating rents, which      \par dampened gold purchases considerably, and it is likely that the region's        \par consumption will show a reduction in tonnage terms for the fourth quarter,      \par compared to the same period in 2006.                                            \par Both the Egyptian and Turkish markets performed well, owing to increased        \par economic stability as well as good consumption from the tourist sector.         \par These markets were also protected against the worst impact of US dollar price   \par volatility, as local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  performed strongly against the dollar.          \par In India, demand was adversely impacted by price volatility and the lack of     \par seasonal buying opportunities during the fourth quarter. However, over the      \par year as a whole, consumption is expected to show an increase, due to record     \par demand levels in tonnage and value terms achieved in the first half of the      \par year.                                                                           \par Chinese consumption remained steady despite high and volatile prices. While     \par there was good demand for 18 carat gold jewellery at the top end of the US      \par market, middle and mass market retailers were negatively impacted by a general  \par downturn in retail sales.                                                       \par Looking forward to 2008, a major concern is that retailers, particularly those  \par in price sensitive markets, will only re-stock slowly, amidst concerns that     \par gold prices may continue to show the volatility exhibited in the first part of  \par the fourth quarter. In China, manufacturers have reported orders at only one    \par third of typical levels for this time of year. High absolute price levels will  \par also act as a constraint on demand, as manufacturers will have access to        \par reduced levels of gold working inventory finance.                               \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SALES                                                              \par The second \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gold Agreement entered its third period in September    \par 2007. Sales occurring to date in this new period of the agreement are estimated \par to be approximately 135t , and have taken place without any disruption to the   \par market.                                                                         \par INVESTMENT MARKET                                                               \par The fourth quarter was an active period in the investment sector. On the        \par exchanges, the average net long position during the quarter of some 24Moz.      \par Investment in Exchange Traded Funds (ETFs) continued the strong performance     \par exhibited in the third quarter into the period under review.                    \par Total holdings at year end stood at close to 28Moz, with a total value of over  \par $23bn, of which some $17bn is held in the US -listed ETF, StreetTracks.         \par INDUSTRIAL DEMAND                                                               \par The industrial sector accounts for 12% of physical demand, of which the         \par electronics industry accounts for some 70% of demand, and continues to show     \par growth over the previous period.                                                \par PRODUCER HEDGING                                                                \par Producer de- hedging slowed in the fourth quarter                               \par from the exceptional levels of previous quarters ,                              \par particularly the first half of the year. No new gold                            \par hedges of any significant proportions were                                      \par reported during the quarter.                                                    \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                                                       \par The US dollar continued its sharp depreciation against the Euro and reached a   \par new low of Euro/US$1.49 in late November. This was as a consequence of the      \par ongoing credit crisis and the perceived need for further interest rate cuts in  \par order to stimulate the economy. US dollar woes were further exacerbated by a    \par rising oil price, which was continuing to trade through its own record highs of \par around US$90/bbl and peaked at US$ 96/bbl.                                      \par In South Africa, expectations of higher interest rates saw the Rand strengthen  \par during the first month of the quarter. Sentiment was further buoyed as the      \par single largest foreign investment in a South African company was announced,     \par when the Investment and Commercial Bank of China announced their intention to   \par purchase a 20% stake in Standard Bank South Africa. Risk aversion in            \par international markets once again caused a reversal of the Rand's fortunes in    \par November, however this was not sustained and the Rand closed the quarter        \par unchanged.                                                                      \par The Australian dollar and Brazilian Real both strengthened marginally over the  \par quarter, gaining 1% and 3% respectively.                                        \par Hedge position                                                                  \par As at 31 December 2007, the total net delta tonnage of the hedge was 10. 39Moz  \par or 323t (at 30 September 2007: 10.58Moz or 329t). The reduction in the hedge    \par book from deliveries and maturing contracts was mostly offset by an increase in \par the hedge delta due to the higher gold price.                                   \par The marked-to-market value of all hedge transactions making up the hedge        \par positions was a negative $4. 27bn (negative R29. 10bn), of which $2.4bn         \par (R16.2bn) is on balance sheet as at 31 December 2007 (at 30 September 2007:     \par negative $3.52bn or R24.17bn).                                                  \par This value was based on a gold price of $836.30/oz, exchange rates of R6.84/$   \par and A$/$0. 88 and the prevailing market interest rates and volatilities at that \par date. The increase in the negative marked-to-market value was primarily due to  \par the higher spot gold price.                                                     \par For the quarter, the company's received price of $687/oz, was 13% lower than    \par the average spot price of $788/oz for 2008, the gap in the received and spot    \par prices is likely to be between 18% to 20% going forward, provided that gold     \par trades in a price range of $700/oz and $900/oz.                                 \par As at 6 February 2008, the marked-to-market value of the hedge book was a       \par negative $4.69bn (negative R36.02bn), based on a gold price of $887.10/oz and   \par exchange rates of R 69/$ and 7. A$/$0.89 and the prevailing market interest     \par rates and volatilities at the time.                                             \par These marked-to-market valuations are not predictive of the future value of the \par hedge position, nor of future impact on the revenue of the company. The         \par valuation represents the cost of buying all hedge contracts at the time of      \par valuation, at market prices and rates available at the time.                    \par                Year                   2008            2009            2010      \par DOLLAR GOLD                                                                     \par Forward                                                                         \par contracts       Amount (kg)          22,817          21,738          14,462     \par                US$/oz                 $314            $316            $347      \par Restructure                                                                     \par Longs           Amount (kg)         *11,304                                     \par                US$/oz                 $647                                      \par Put options soldAmount (kg)          25,962           3,748           1,882     \par                US$/oz                 $682            $530            $410      \par Call options                                                     