ther      \par risks. Distributions from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be subject to foreign investment and   \par exchange control laws and regulations and the quantity of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       \par available in offshore countries. In addition distributions from joint ventures  \par are subject to the relevant board approval.                                     \par The credit facilities and other financing arrangements contain financial        \par covenants and other similar undertakings. To the extent that external           \par borrowings are required, the groups covenant performance indicates that         \par existing financing facilities will be available to meet the above commitments.  \par To the extent that any of the financing facilities mature in the near future,   \par the group believes that these facilities can be refinanced on similar terms to  \par those currently in place.                                                       \par 15. Contingent liabilities                                                      \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti's material contingent liabilities at 31 December 2007 are     \par detailed below:                                                                 \par Groundwater pollution - South Africa -  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti has identified a       \par number of groundwater pollution sites at its current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in South        \par Africa, and has investigated a number of different technologies and             \par methodologies that could possibly be used to remediate the pollution plumes.    \par The viability of the suggested remediation techniques in the local geological   \par formation in South Africa is however unknown. No sites have been remediated and \par present research and development work is focused on several pilot projects to   \par find a solution that will in fact yield satisfactory results in South African   \par conditions. Subject to the technology being developed as a remediation          \par technique, no reliable estimate can be made for the obligation.                 \par Provision of surety - South Africa -  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti has provided sureties in \par favour of a lender on a gold loan facility with its affiliate Oro Africa (Pty)  \par Ltd and one of its subsidiaries to a maximum value of R100m ($15m). The         \par suretyship agreements have a termination notice period of 90 days.              \par Sales tax on gold deliveries - Brazil - Mineracao Serra Grande S.A.(MSG), the   \par operator of the Crixas mine in Brazil, has received two tax assessments from    \par the State of Goias related to payments of sales taxes on gold deliveries for    \par export, one for the period between February 2004 and June 2005 and the other    \par for the period between July 2005 and May 2006. The tax authorities maintain     \par that whenever a taxpayer export gold mined in the state of Goias, through a     \par branch located in a different Brazilian State, it must obtain an authorisation  \par from the Goias State Treasury by means of a Special Regime Agreement (Termo de  \par Acordo re Regime Especial - TARE). The Serra Grande \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is co -owned with \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kinross Gold Corporation.\f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti Brasil Mineracao Ltda. manages      \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and its attributable share of the first assessment is             \par approximately $39m. Although MSG requested the TARE in early 2004, the TARE,    \par which authorized the remittance of gold to the company's branch in Minas Gerais \par specifically for export purposes, was only granted and executed in May 2006.    \par In November 2006 the administrative council's second chamber ruled in favour of \par Serra Grande and fully cancelled the tax liability related to the first period. \par The State of Goias has appealed to the full board of the State of Goias tax     \par administrative council. The second assessment was issued by the State of        \par Goias in October 2006 on the same grounds as the first one, and the             \par attributable share of the assessment is approximately $24m. The company         \par believes both assessments are in violation of Federal legislation on sales      \par taxes.                                                                          \par VAT Disputes - Brazil - MSG received a tax assessment in October 2003 from the  \par \par State of Minas Gerais related to sales taxes on gold allegedly returned from    \par the branch in Minas Gerais to the company head office in the State of Goias.    \par The tax administrators rejected the company's appeal against the assessment.    \par The company is now dismissing the case at the judicial sphere. The company's    \par attributable share of the assessment is approximately $8m.                      \par Tax Disputes - Brazil - Morro Velho and  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti Brasil Mineracao      \par are involved in disputes with tax authorities. These disputes involve eleven    \par federal tax assessments including income tax, social contributions and annual   \par property tax based on ownership of properties outside of urban perimeters       \par (ITR). The amount involved is approximately $8m.                                \par 16. Concentration of risk                                                       \par There is a concentration of risk in respect of reimbursable value added tax     \par and fuel duties from the Malian government:                                     \par Reimbursable value added tax due from the Malian government amounts to an       \par attributable $42m at 31 December 2007 (30 September 2007: attributable $37m).   \par The last audited value added tax return was for the period ended 31 March 2007  \par and at the balance sheet date an attributable $25m was still outstanding and    \par $17m is still subject to audit. The accounting processes for the unaudited      \par amount are in accordance with the processes advised by the Malian government in \par terms of the previous audits.                                                   \par Reimbursable fuel duties from the Malian government amounts to an               \par attributable $7m at 31 December 2007 (30 September 2007: attributable $8m).     \par Fuel duty refund claims are required to be submitted before 31 January of the   \par following year and are subject to authorisation by firstly the Department of    \par Mining an d secondly the Custom and Excise authorities. The Customs and Excise  \par authorities have approved an attributable $2m, which is still outstanding,      \par whilst an attributable $5m is still subject to authorisation. The accounting    \par processes for the unauthorised amount are in accordance with the processes      \par advised by the Malian government in terms of the previous authorisations. As    \par from February 2006 all fuel duties have been exonerated.                        \par The government of Mali is a shareholder in all the Malian entities. Management  \par is in negotiations with the Government of Mali to agree a protocol for the      \par repayment of the outstanding amounts. The outstanding amounts have been         \par discounted to their present value at a rate of 6.5 %.                           \par There is a concentration of risk in respect of reimbursable value added tax and \par fuel duties from the Tanzanian government:                                      \par Reimbursable value added tax due from the Tanzanian government amounts to       \par $16m at 31 December 2007 (30 September 2007: $18m). The last audited value      \par added tax return was for the period ended 30 June 2007 and at the balance sheet \par date $14m was still outstanding and $2m is still subject to audit. The          \par accounting processes for the unaudited amount are in accordance with the        \par processes advised by the Tanzanian government in terms of the previous audits.  \par The outstanding amounts have been discounted to their present value at a rate   \par of 7.8%.                                                                        \par Reimbursable fuel duties from the Tanzanian government amounts to $37m at 31    \par December 2007 (30 September 2007: $30m). Fuel duty claims are required to be    \par submitted after consumption of the related fuel and are subject to              \par authorisation by the Customs and Excise authorities. Claims for refund of fuel  \par duties amounting to $21m have been lodged with the Customs and Excise           \par authorities, which are still outstanding, whilst claims for refund of $ 16m     \par have not yet been submitted. The accounting processes for the unauthorised      \par amount are in accordance with the processes advised by the Tanzanian government \par in terms of the previous authorisations. The outstanding amounts have been      \par discounted to their present value at a rate of 7.8%.                            \par 17. Attributable interest                                                       \par Although  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti holds a 66.7% interest in Cripple Creek & Victor     \par Gold Mining Company Limited, it is currently entitled to receive 100% of the    \par cash flows from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until the loan, extended to the joint venture by  \par AngloGold Ashanti USA Inc., is repaid.                                          \par 18. Borrowings                                                                  \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti's borrowings are interest bearing.                            \par 19. Announcements                                                               \par On 12 November 2007, it was announced that due to further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2            \par management restructure, Neville Nicolau resigned from the board to pursue other \par opportunities with immediate effect.                                            \par On 12 December 2007,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti announced the successful closing of a    \par US$1.15 bn syndicated revolving loan facility. The new 3-year facility will be  \par used to refinance an existing US$700m revolving credit facility, an AUD200m     \par facility and for general corporate purposes.                                    \par On 14 January 2008,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti announced that it had agreed to acquire   \par 100% of  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Golden Cycle Gold Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (GCGC) through a merger transaction in    \par which GCGC's shareholders will receive 29  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti ADRs for every 100  \par shares of GCGC common stock held. GCGC currently holds a 33% shareholding in    \par Cripple Creek & Victor while  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti hold the remaining 67%. The      \par merger transaction will result in Cripple Creek & Victor being a wholly-owned   \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The transaction is subject to a number of          \par regulatory and statutory approvals, including approval by GCGC shareholders.    \par The transaction, at the date of announcement was valued at approximately        \par US$149m.                                                                        \par On 18 January 2008,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti provided \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guidance to its fourth \par quarter 2007 results, in which it was stated that its South African and Geita   \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had experienced production difficulties resulting in the group's     \par production for the quarter to be of the region of 1,368,000 ounces.             \par Following the announcement made on 25 January 2008, in which  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti  \par advised that Eskom (the South African electricity supply body) would be         \par interrupting power supplies to the company's South African \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,          \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti halted mining and gold recovery at these \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .          \par Subsequently,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti announced on 29 January 2008, that it had begun \par the process to restart production at its South African \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  following a   \par meeting with Eskom and industrial electricity consumers at which, Eskom had     \par agreed to provide  \f28 \uc2 \f2 AngloGold\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ashanti with 90% of its electricity demand prior to \par the shut down so as to return the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to normal production.              \par 20. Dividend                                                                    \par The directors have today declared Final Dividend No. 103 of 53 (Final Dividend  \par No. 101: 240) South African cents per ordinary share for the year ended 31      \par December 2007. In compliance with the requirements of STRATE, given the         \par company's primary listing on the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSE Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the salient dates for payment of  \par the dividend are as follows:          