\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-SAfrica's Mboweni-dust settled but credit risks remain   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gordon Bell and Wendell Roelf   \par \uc2 602 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2007\par 21:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background)   \par \par \uc2 CAPE TOWN, Oct 5 (Reuters) - The dust may have settled on the global credit crisis following \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but serious risks remain, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserve Bank Governor Tito Mboweni said on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 He also said in a speech in Cape Town checks had shown South African banks were not exposed to the troubled U.S. subprime sector.   \par \par \uc2 "As we prepare for our MPC (monetary policy committee), the dust appears to have settled (on global market turbulence) ... it seems to us that there has been a sufficient correction in the market, but we have to remain vigilant."   \par \par \uc2 The situation had been unclear ahead of the previous meeting to decide on local interest rate movements in August, although it was decided at the time to monitor developments.   \par \par \uc2 The MPC meets on Oct. 10-11 with some analysts expecting another increase in the repo rate after rises in June and August that took it to 10 percent, due to inflation staying outside the bank's 3 to 6 percent target.   \par \par \uc2 However, Mboweni warned that there may still be further volatility ahead, with only a fraction of the total exposure to the U.S. subprime market having been unwound.   \par \par \uc2 It had been estimated that total private bank exposure was between $1.5 trillion and $2 trillion, with the unwinding so far only $10 billion to $15 billion.   \par \par \uc2 "So we are not out of the woods yet, what we have seen so far is ... just a picnic. A bigger problem might be coming," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Global markets have reeled under fears of a credit squeeze hitting world economic growth, but liquidity injected into the financial system by U.S., European and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have calmed jittery investors.   \par \par \uc2 NO WIDER MANDATE   \par \par \uc2 South Africa's stock market is back at a record high after following other bourses sharply lower during the height of the turbulence since late July.   \par \par \uc2 The turmoil had its origins in U.S. subprime mortgage market losses but spread to a wider credit squeeze that rippled through most economies.   \par \par \uc2 Mboweni said South African banks were not "much" exposed to the subprime woes.   \par \par \uc2 "As far as I can see, so far our banks have been very prudent and they have not gotten much exposure as we would have thought."   \par \par \uc2 Mboweni added he was not in favour of an wider mandate for the Reserve Bank, further than focusing on inflation, like in the United States, although central banks must work to safeguard the financial system.   \par \par \uc2 South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is independent and mandated to target inflation, but the government has come under pressure from trade unions to adjust this to include economic growth and employment.   \par \par \uc2 "It is a very difficult mandate, it is a kind of mandate I wouldn't like to have," he said, referring to the U.S. Federal Reserve that also takes into account wider economic and job trends.   \par \par \uc2 Mboweni also voiced support for his \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  counterpart in Zimbabwe, who he said was trying to uphold high standards in very difficult circumstances.   \par \par \uc2 Zimbabwe is in the midst of a severe economic crisis, with annual inflation of about 6,600 percent and chronic shortages of fuel and forein \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Reserve Bank of Zimbabwe Governor Gideon Gono has criticised President Robert Mugabe's government for moves to freeze prices and wages, and for plans to take over foreign banks and mines.   \par \par \uc2 SAFRICA-MBOWENI/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|RNP|PCM|PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071005e3a5001v8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
