\par\par \b \uc2 IMF finds no exact cause of 2001 S.Africa rand fall.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lesley Wroughton  \par \uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 07:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Jan 9 (Reuters) - After a comprehensive study, IMF economists said they had been unable to pinpoint the cause of the South African rand's 42 percent drop in value against the U.S. dollar to a record low in the last four months of 2001.  \par \par \uc2 The unexpected fall of the rand between September and December of that year shook public confidence in government fiscal policy and threatened to push up inflation, prompting President Thabo Mbeki to appoint a commission of inquiry.  \par \par \uc2 Some policymakers blamed the fall on speculators taking short positions against the rand.  \par \par \uc2 Like the government's Myburgh Commission report issued in August 2002, the IMF economists concluded that no clear answer existed for the rapid depreciation of the rand.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF, in a paper issued late Thursday, cited a vague combination of financial market developments as a likely contributor to the rand's drop to 13.85 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The rand recovered all the lost ground in 2002 and is now trading at around 6.57 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF raised questions over whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserve Bank should have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in limiting the size of the fall.  \par \par \uc2 "Essentially, we find that financial market developments are the most likely source of the depreciation, but the exact cause remains unclear," authors Ashok Bhundia and Jan Gottschalk concluded.  \par \par \uc2 "A preliminary examination of relative policy interest rates suggest that monetary policy did not contribute directly to the depreciation, but this still leaves open the question of whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have been more active in trying to limit the extent of the depreciation," the IMF added.  \par \par \uc2 The Myburgh commission, appointed by Mbeki, pointed to broad economic factors behind the depreciation, among them a slowdown in the global economy and declines in the current account balance in the last quarter in 2001.  \par \par \uc2 Also mentioned as a possible contributor was global risk aversion toward emerging markets, such as South Africa, following problems in Argentina and neighboring Zimbabwe.  \par \par \uc2 The commission however could not explain the speed of the depreciation nor could it find definite evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF team said the depreciation most likely originated in South Africa.  \par \par \uc2 "It was the rand that lost in value against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and not the U.S. dollar that gained in strength, indicating that the nominal disturbance originated in the South African economy," it said.  \par \par \uc2 One possible source of such a disturbance, the economists suggested, could have come from an easing in the monetary stance of the South African Reserve Bank relative to the United United States, in which case lower interest rates or an acceleration in money supply would have been the main cause of the depreciation.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e019001bi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
