\par\par \b \uc2 Curtain may fall on S.Africa apartheid era  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  tool.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariam Isa  \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2003\par 11:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 JOHANNESBURG, Dec 5 (Reuters) - South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is finally in a position to close its forward book, a loss-making legacy of apartheid which has helped to prevent it building up the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The book - which refers to the bank's dollar purchases on the forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - has been steadily wound down from a peak of over $25 billion in 1998, when authorities used it in a vain attempt to defend the rand from speculators.  \par \par \uc2 Created during the apartheid era to combat economic sanctions it losses have run to billions of dollars and now its days are probably numbered.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of October the forward book stood at just $2.3 billion. There has been no update since November, the month when the Reserve Bank boosted its dollar purchases to $1 billion, but analysts say it probably stands at $1-$1.5 billion now.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the book will be closed during the first quarter of 2004," said SCMB senior economist Goolam Ballim.  \par \par \uc2 "It will mark the extermination of one of the legacies of financial mismanagement during apartheid," Ballim said, estimating the forward book at $1.25 billion now.  \par \par \uc2 Reserve Bank Governor Tito Mboweni made two pledges when he became the first black governor to take the post in 1999: not to use \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and to eliminate the bank's uncovered \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  liabilities.  \par \par \uc2 He achieved the second goal in May this year, when the bank's so-called net open forward position (NOFP) - the difference between its net reserves and forward book - became a positive rather than a negative balance.  \par \par \uc2 The NOFP had been cited as the achilles heel of the volatile rand and a big obstacle to its sustained recovery, as it forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars even as the local unit depreciated - as it has over most of the past two decades.  \par \par \uc2 Mboweni has also vowed to close the forward book, which has cost the country billions of dollars since it was set up in the 1970s. But some within the bank believe it would be prudent to keep it open, albeit at a more manageable level.  \par \par \uc2 "My feeling is they should wind it down significantly further, but not close it completely - the Reserve Bank's focus should be on building headline gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," Brait economist Colen Garrow said. He puts the book at $1.5 billion.  \par \par \uc2 KEY ISSUE IS BUILDING RESERVES  \par \par \uc2 South Africa's forward book was originally created to allow state utilities hedge their debt on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when apartheid-era sanctions made it difficult to do so, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  taking on the risk on behalf of the government.  \par \par \uc2 But when sanctions tightened during the 1980s, officials at the Reserve Bank decided to use the book to defend the rand.  \par \par \uc2 After the fall of apartheid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose dramatically during the 1998 global emerging market crisis, building up a huge loss-making position for the coming years as the rand continued to weaken.  \par \par \uc2 In 2001 losses on the forward book amounted to 28 billion rand ($4.1 billion). To cover them, the Treasury said it would issue seven billion rand of zero coupon bonds to the Reserve Bank over each of the coming four years.  \par \par \uc2 Garrow said it may not have to fund that entire amount now because the rand's sustained rally over the past two years - which amounts to close to 80 percent against the dollar - may mean that the forward book is producing profits.  \par \par \uc2 But in any case, the main issue is to remove the obstacles which prevent the Reserve Bank from building South Africa's reserves, which at $7.88 billion are far behind its emerging market peers and makes the rand vulnerable to speculators.  \par \par \uc2 According to IMF data - which excludes the gold component included in South Africa's reserves - China has $384 billion, and Turkey has $33 billion. The Czech Republic - with an economy half the size of South Africa - has about $26 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Most economists say that there is no "magical number" for adequate reserves, but Bank of America's emerging market analyst Callum Henderson says $20 billion would be more reasonable.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031205dzc5002pi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
