\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Strong SAfrica rand no threat to ratings-agencies.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariam Isa  \par \uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 18:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  JOHANNESBURG, Oct 1 (Reuters) - South Africa's robust rand may curb domestic exports, the main engine of economic growth, but it does not pose a threat to the country's investment-grade credit ratings, two top rating agencies said on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  The rand's surge to three-year peaks against the broadly weaker dollar this week has fanned fears that the economy will be severely hit by its strength, with many top local firms warning that earnings will fall - possibly leading to job cuts.   \par \par \uc2  But Standard and Poor's and Moody's told Reuters in separate interviews that the overall outlook for the country's economy remained sound, with inflation on a sustainable downward trend and government coffers still in reasonably good shape.   \par \par \uc2  "As far as broader issues are concerned, from a credit point of view we are not worried, the fiscal picture looks reasonably robust," Conrad Reuss, S&P managing director for sovereign ratings, said in a telephone interview from London.   \par \par \uc2  "The strong rand will not affect the rating that we have on South Africa, or the stable outlook." S&P raised its long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ratings for South Africa to BBB from BBB-minus in May, citing the strength of the country's finances.   \par \par \uc2  The rand has appreciated by about 65 percent against the dollar since hitting a record low of 13.85/dollar late in 2001, making it the world's star performer in 2002 and so far in 2003.   \par \par \uc2  It dipped to 6.87/dollar on Tuesday, its best level since September 2000. Its latest spurt has been driven by the broadly weaker greenback, with strong commodity prices and the appeal of South Africa's high interest rates to foreigners also factors.   \par \par \uc2  The trend has prompted howls of anguish from trade unions as well as the country's top miners, which earn dollars for their exports and pay costs in rand. Importing firms may also be squeezed by hedging in anticipation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness.   \par \par \uc2  "We are still seeing some dynamics in the export sector," Moody's Vice President and Senior Credit Officer Kristin Lindow told Reuters in a telephone interview from New York.   \par \par \uc2  "The external position of the country is still in good shape, the current account deficit is still small - I wouldn't call for doom and gloom yet."   \par \par \uc2  Moody's assigned South Africa a BAA2 rating for its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated debt in December 2001, and upgraded the outlook to positive in February 2003.   \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Trevor Manuel has warned that his forecast budget deficit for fiscal 2003/4 would modestly breach the 2.4 percent of gross domestic product seen in the February budget, partly because of falling corporate tax revenues.   \par \par \uc2  LOWER INTEREST RATES TO BOOST GROWTH   \par \par \uc2  But Reuss said that the negative impact of slowing exports - which account for 35 percent of South Africa's GDP - should be at least partially offset by lower domestic interest rates and a pickup in the global economy.   \par \par \uc2  South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slashed its key repo rate by 350 basis points to ten percent so far this year, unwinding most of the hikes it made last year to quash soaring prices ignited by the rand's historic slump late in 2001.   \par \par \uc2  Lindow said it was not so much the level of the rand which was an issue as the uncertainty about what it was going to do next, and the fact that companies may have made plans on the basis of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  being weaker than it is now.   \par \par \uc2  "Companies that are thinking of going in are worried about the volatility of the exchange rate," she said.   \par \par \uc2  "But products produced in South Africa can still be competitive at seven rand (to the dollar). The problem is that plans have been made with a different level of the rand...but who knows how long it will stay there, it could go back."   \par \par \uc2  Both Reuss and Lindow said that it was wise for \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at curbing the rand's rally, as this could backfire - as it did when the rand dived in the emerging market crisis in 1998.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031001dza1002hg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
