\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Lira nudges up on troop optimism, rand eyes MPC. \b0\par\par\uc2 438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2003\par 11:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The Turkish lira edged up against the dollar on Wednesday on optimism the government and military were close to agreement on sending troops to Iraq, while the South African rand was rangy ahead of Thursday's interest rate decision. Thelira strengthened to under 1.4 million per dollar  early on with dealers finding encouragement from signs that no obvious rift emerged between military top brass and government leaders from a meeting on the troops issue on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  By 0930 GMT, the lira traded at 1,400,000 to the dollar \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   from 1,401,500 at Tuesday's close.\par  "From what we hear there's nothing negative coming out of\par  these meetings...there had been concern there was going to be\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par some sort of opposition," said Dwyfor Evans, emerging markets analyst\par at Bank of America.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even if the military and government do agree, the matter will still\par have to go through parliament, which is unlikely to be\par straightforward.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Deputies' rejection of a bill to allow U.S. troops to use Turkish\par facilities to prepare for an attack on neighbouring Iraq earlier this\par year effectively shut the door on billions of dollars worth of U.S. aid\par and loans.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The lira also found some support from reasonably well received debt\par auctions on Tuesday. Optimism over the country's economic recovery\par prospects brought lower-than-expected yields at Tuesday's auctions of\par 238-day and 406-day paper.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Evans said dealers would be nervous about pushing the lira much\par stronger from current levels, given the persistent risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cap its gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RAND AWAITS RATE DECISION\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Africa\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's bi-monthly two-day Monetary Policy\par Committee meeting got underway, with a majority of analysts expecting a\par 100 basis point rate cut.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, rand dealers were treading cautiously given the considerable\par risk of a deeper cut following dovish comments from bank Governor Tito\par Mboweni last week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There is a good case for an aggressive move of at least 150 basis\par points," said Razia Khan, chief Africa economist at Standard\par Chartered.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "At the last MPC meeting, a majority of members were in favour of a cut\par of more than 100 basis points. Since then, June inflation data has\par surprised on the downside."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par London dealers said the rand was twitching at the prospect of a big\par cut, having already fallen back from last week's three-month highs\par against the greenback.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The main repo rate currently stands at 12 percent. The rand traded a\par shade lower at 7.41 to the dollar .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030813dz8d00ioi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
