\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Poland and South Africa take centre stage. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2002\par 13:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 28 (Reuters) - Poland and South Africa take centre stage in emerging markets this week with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts focusing on interest rate policy and key economic indicators and forecasts. \par \par \uc2  The zloty was trading higher against the euro on Monday with markets cautious ahead of Tuesday's unveiling of the government's medium term economic policy programme and Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate decision. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was also being watched in the Czech Republic where the crown was trading sideways amid wariness over the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the euro. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2  was bracing for key inflation figures with the rand dropping back in early trading following Friday's move to seven-week highs against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  POLISH RATE POLICY \par \par \uc2  The zloty pushed up to two and a half week highs against the euro around 3.5620 , according to Reuters data, mainly as a result of broad weakness in the euro. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to six month highs against the dollar beyond $0.8625 . \par \par \uc2  Attention locally, however, was firmly focused on two events later in the week. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the government presents its medium-term economic policy programme aiming to revive Poland's flagging economic growth and curtail surging unemployment, which stood at 17.4 percent at the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  Since government policy will in part determine the future strategy of the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said the size of this week's expected interest rate move was still not clear. \par \par \uc2  Most, however, expected a cut of 150 basis points. \par \par \uc2  "I'm looking for a fairly substantial rate cut - 150 basis points. There is a possibility they could do more or even less. In other words it's not a done deal," said Juliet Sampson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  Even with a 150 basis point cut, Poland's interest rate differential would remain substantial compared with comparable emerging market economies. \par \par \uc2  In 2001, the bank cut interest rates six times to 11.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  "We're not anywhere near the point at which the carry on zloty positions is not attractive," said Sampson. \par \par \uc2  Last Wednesday, Premier Leszek Miller said the government's economic recovery package would not work without lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  In the neighbouring Czech Republic, traders were cautious ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-making board meeting on Thursday. Monetary policy will be reviewed and the bank is expected to lower both its inflation and growth outlooks. \par \par \uc2  Following last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a further firming of the crown against the euro, markets were also reluctant to push the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  further up. \par \par \uc2  The crown was trading at 31.85 to the euro . \par \par \uc2  "People are pretty nervous about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trading sideways," said Jake Moore, emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  SOUTH AFRICAN INFLATION \par \par \uc2  The rand slipped back against the dollar on Monday in what analysts said was a small technical correction following a month of more or less consistent appreciation. \par \par \uc2  On December 21, the rand hit an all time low of 13.8100 to the dollar. On Monday it was trading at 11.5050. \par \par \uc2  "We had a massive collapse in the rand. The market looks like it's getting rid if its long dollar positions," said Moore. \par \par \uc2  December consumer price data on Tuesday and producer price data for the same month on Wednesday are being anxiously awaited to see whether further interest rate hikes are likely this year. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll on Friday predicted the benchmark CPIX index would rise by an annual rate of 6.3 percent, the same level as in November, but still above the government's three to six percent target range. \par \par \uc2  Bond markets were particularly sensitive to the forthcoming data but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said the rand could be affected in the event of a major suprise. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Turkish Economy Minister Kemal Dervis said on Monday that Turkey would "do what was required" to deal with a key IMF-backed banking law prior to an IMF meeting on Feb 4 set to approve a $12-billion loan deal for the country. \par \par \uc2  Stocks on the main Istanbul National-100 index fell 2.1 percent to 12,600.68 points, and the lira edged down to best dollar bids of 1,344,000 on Monday. Benchmark bond yields rose to 71.05 percent from Friday's 70.72 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020128dy1s00ngt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
