\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-S.Africa's Manuel says rand victim of strong dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alister Bull \par \uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 20:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PRAGUE, Sept 22 (Reuters) - South African Finance Minister Trevor Manuel said on Friday that the country's battered rand had been a victim of dollar strength and played down the fallout from high oil prices on growth and interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Manuel, in Prague for the annual IMF/World Bank meeting, said that the South African \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had stuck to a cautious approach when it held interest rates steady this week. \par \par \uc2  But he said that they would take full account of the external nature of the energy price shock and recognise that the domestic economy should not be unduly penalised. \par \par \uc2  "You are not going to change OECD's oil output by bumping up interest rates. You may in fact be inducing unneccessary pain on the economy on the long run," he told Reuters in an interview. \par \par \uc2  South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets interest rate policy and enjoys constitutionally guaranteed independence. Manuel said that emphasising that its actions made a difference to the lives of ordinary South Africans contained no threat. \par \par \uc2  "This is not a warning. I am saying that this is a call for the Reserve Bank to make. We've given them complete instrument independence. As they take stock of the situation we would anticipate that they would evaluate all these issues," he said. \par \par \uc2  Manuel has recently cautioned that the country's forecast for GDP growth of three percent this year might be revised lower due to the impact of devastating floods earlier this year, which badly hurt the country's agricultural output. \par \par \uc2  But he declined to be more specific before publication of the government's three-year budget framework next month. \par \par \uc2  RAND BULLIED TO LIFETIME LOWS BY STRONG DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The rand has been dragged to record lows this week but Manuel said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the victim of a rampant dollar and had managed to appreciate against other commodity-based units as well as gaining against the euro. \par \par \uc2  "It is important to bear in mind that because our imports come largely from Europe the impact is not as bad as a stronger dollar in our exchange rate basket," he said. \par \par \uc2  The rand hit a lifetime low of 7.28 against the dollar this week but has advanced against the Australian and New Zealand dollars as well as against the troubled euro. \par \par \uc2  It closed in Johannesburg on Friday around 7.22, taking heart from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the euro. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Africa\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dependence on oil imports, as well as gold sales for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings, has highlighted the economy's vulnerability to swings in world commodity prices, but Manuel stressed this was not a fatal dependence. \par \par \uc2  "What has modulated the oil price in South Africa is that there is substantial contribution from synthetic fuels and that has taken some of the sharpness from the edge of the price rises," he said. \par \par \uc2  Apartheid South Africa, isolated by international sanctions, was forced to develop substantial technology to secure alternative energy sources from which the nation - now under majority black rule - is now benefiting. \par \par \uc2  (IMF newsroom, 4202-6117-6229). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06rtu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
